*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution provides citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage.
Any foreigners residing in [COUNTRY] for more than three years may vote in municipal elections. Any residents from a member country of [NGO] may vote in municipal elections regardless of how long they have resided in [COUNTRY]. Only citizens, including those living outside [COUNTRY], may vote in legislative and presidential elections.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : In recent legislative elections, individuals and parties were free to declare their candidacies and candidates for a total of 72 seats. The main [OPPOSITION PARTY] won 40 seats in the parliament with approximately one-half of the vote, returning the party to power for the first time in many years. The former [RULING PARTY] won 29 seats with 37 percent, and another [POLITICAL PARTY] won the remaining three seats with 6 percent of the vote. International observers characterized these elections as generally free and fair.
The most recent presidential election took place that year. The [RULING PARTY] candidate, who had the support of the former [RULING PARTY], won the election with approximately three-quarters of the vote.
Election observers from [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] characterized these elections as free, transparent, and credible. Observers noted some irregularities, however, including voters being pressured near polling stations to vote for certain candidates and allegations of vote buying.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit the participation of women or members of [ETHNIC GROUP], and they did participate. Women’s participation fell in positions within the central government and remained at less than half of positions on the supreme court, and especially in prosecutorial positions. At the local level, in community associations and on city councils, women had less representation than men. Women held 17 of the 72 parliament seats and occupied three of the 11 cabinet-level positions in government ministries. Women filled three of the eight seats on the supreme court, including the presidency.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a stable democracy with competitive elections and periodic transfers of power between rival parties. Civil liberties are generally protected, but access to justice is impaired by an overburdened court system, and crime remains a concern. Other outstanding problems include persistent inequities for women and migrant workers.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the parliament adopted new legislation that instituted a 40 percent gender quota for candidate lists in future national and local elections.
Later that year, the government announced it would expand a camera surveillance system in a major city and several other islands. While observers voiced privacy concerns when the system was introduced in the capital and several other cities a few years prior, the security forces claim the system helped solve several hundred crimes in the capital.
Earlier that year, the government formed a corruption prevention body, which is meant to identify areas of the economy that are vulnerable to corruption and evaluate the effectiveness of existing anticorruption efforts.

---

## A Electoral Process

The president is directly elected for up to two consecutive five-year terms. The prime minister, who holds most executive authority, is nominated by and accountable to the parliament, and is formally appointed by the president.
Incumbent president the president of the [RULING PARTY] was reelected with 74 percent of the vote. The main challenger was an independent candidate, who took about 23 percent. The voting was generally considered free and fair. The prime minister, also of the [RULING PARTY], was appointed a month after legislative elections.
Members of the [POPULATION FIGURE]-seat parliament are directly elected in multimember constituencies to serve five-year terms. In the legislative election, the [RULING PARTY], then in opposition, won 40 seats. The governing [OPPOSITION PARTY] was reduced to 29 seats, and another [OPPOSITION PARTY] took 3. International observers assessed the elections as largely free and fair.
The legal framework provides for fair and competitive elections. The electoral commission, whose members are elected by a two-thirds majority in the parliament, is generally considered impartial.

