*State Department Human Rights Report*

b. Freedoms of Peaceful Assembly and Association

The government restricted freedoms of peaceful assembly and association.

Freedom of Peaceful Assembly

The government did not respect the legal right to public assembly, demonstration, and mobilization. Prodemocracy marches and protests were not allowed. Police failed to protect peaceful protesters from attacks; they also committed attacks and provided logistical support to other attackers. Human rights organizations reported police stopped traffic for and otherwise protected progovernment demonstrations.
The security forces consistently refused to accept applications or denied permits to use public spaces for prodemocracy marches, using unclear parameters. A denial of permission from the security forces resulted in significant repression and violence against protesters when they carried on with the protest. On a specific date, the security forces used tear gas and shot marbles and pellets at prodemocracy protesters, whose application to march was denied. Paramilitary groups attacked churches throughout the year during masses in commemoration of protesters killed during a major prodemocracy uprising.
On another date, police surrounded a church in a major city where a priest hosted a group of mothers of political prisoners on a hunger strike to demand the release of their children. Police impeded access and prevented anyone inside the church from exiting, and later that day the government cut off water and electricity to the church. The following day, the security forces arrested [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals who arrived at the church to show solidarity with the striking mothers by providing them with water and charged those individuals with trafficking weapons, munitions, and explosives. Local media and lawyers for the accused said police planted military-grade weapons inside the individuals’ vehicles after they were detained. An attorney for some of the detained individuals reported they had been beaten in prison. The trial of the detained individuals was scheduled to begin at a later date.
On another date, security forces and riot police, who had surrounded and blocked access to a major cathedral as families of political prisoners began a hunger strike inside, allowed inside the cathedral a group of at least [POPULATION FIGURE] regime-aligned individuals, who assaulted a priest and desecrated sacred items and spaces. The regime-aligned individuals spent the night camped out on the altar of the cathedral, menacing the hunger strikers who had locked themselves inside the sacristy. The siege ended when the Red Cross evacuated the hunger strikers.
Through various press releases and arrests, the security forces claimed protesters were responsible for destruction of public and private buildings, setting fires, homicides, and looting. While the majority of protesters were peaceful, some turned violent as they responded to security forces and paramilitary provocations and use of force by throwing stones and employing homemade mortars and weapons to defend their positions.

Freedom of Association

The law provides for freedom of association, including the right to organize or affiliate with political parties; nevertheless, the parliament used their accreditation powers for political purposes. Parliamentary accreditation is mandatory for [NGO]s to receive funding, have bank accounts, or employ workers licitly. The interior ministry has oversight of regulatory compliance by [NGO]s and provides certificates. Many [NGO]s that worked on topics of democracy, human rights, and women’s issues complained that the ministry purposefully withheld certification to hinder their work and access to funding. A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] affiliated with the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] community publicly stated that the government retained humanitarian goods in customs with unclear requirements on how to get the products into the country.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Note

To read this report in [LANGUAGE], please click here.

## Overview

The election of the [RULING PARTY] leader, the president, for many years began a period of democratic deterioration marked by the consolidation of all branches of government under the [RULING PARTY]'s control, the limitation of fundamental freedoms, and unchecked corruption in government. In recent years, state forces, with the aid of informally allied [ARMED GROUP]s, responded to a mass antigovernment movement with violence and repression. The rule of law collapsed as the government moved to put down the movement, with rights monitors reporting the deaths of at least 325 people, extrajudicial detentions, disappearances, and torture. The crisis continued as arbitrary arrests and detentions continued, and perceived government opponents reported surveillance and monitoring.

## Key Developments

In recent months, following the death in state custody of a political prisoner, the [OPPOSITION PARTY] alliance suspended its participation in a dialogue with the government aimed at negotiating a solution to the [COUNTRY]'s political crisis. The government then formally ended the talks.
Between a few months prior and the middle of the year, authorities released almost 400 political prisoners detained during a major protest, though many were released to house arrest. However, arbitrary detentions have continued, prompting a condemnation by the [NGO] in recent months of the government’s “persistent repression of dissent and the ongoing pattern of arbitrary arrests.”
In the middle of the year, the government passed legislation that granted amnesty for crimes committed in the context of a major antigovernment protest. Critics worried that the law effectively shields state authorities from prosecution for extrajudicial killings and other abuses, and bans people who had been detained for protesting from participating in future demonstrations.
State harassment and repression against the media continued. A major newspaper, one of the [COUNTRY]'s oldest, announced that it was being forced to close because authorities were preventing it from obtaining newsprint and ink.

---

## E Associational and Organizational Rights

Freedom of assembly deteriorated severely, when at least 325 people were killed and at least 2,000 were injured in a ferocious crackdown on an antigovernment protest movement that began after authorities announced social security reforms; it soon turned into a broader antigovernment movement aimed at forcing the regime from power. A majority of the abuses have been attributed to the security forces and armed allied groups, which the [NGO] said in a report operate with “total impunity.” In recent years, the security forces issued a statement declaring unauthorized marches and demonstrations “illegal.” The security forces have since denied permits for public demonstrations, and have occupied public spaces to prevent protests.
In recent years, the security forces blocked or dispersed a number of attempted demonstrations. More than 100 people were arrested for attempting to protest in the capital, but ultimately released. A protest meant to commemorate the start of the protests the year before was prevented by riot police, who surrounded groups of marchers before they could begin marching; an [OPPOSITION PARTY] coalition said 22 people were arrested. Later, the security forces violently dispersed another gathering commemorating the death of a teenager who was killed at a protest the previous year. And later that year, more than a dozen activists were arrested and charged with trafficking ammunition and firearms after they attempted to deliver water to a church where relatives of detained prisoners were on a hunger strike.
Legislation passed that year states that protesters who are released must not take part in actions that lead to further “crimes,” effectively prohibiting them from again participating in antigovernment demonstrations.
Groups critical of the government or that focus on issues like corruption have operated within an increasingly restrictive environment under the [RULING PARTY] administration, which among other measures has used registration laws to choke off their sources of funding. For several years, human rights defenders and leaders of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s have experienced severe harassment, arbitrary detention, and arbitrary expulsion. A number of [NGO]s, most of which focused on democracy, human rights, or press freedom, saw their registration cancelled. Human rights organizations reported continued monitoring and surveillance.
The [RULING PARTY] controls many of the country's labor unions, and the legal rights of non-[RULING PARTY] unions are not fully guaranteed in practice. Although the law recognizes the right to strike, approval from the relevant government body is almost never granted. Employers sometimes form their own unions to avoid recognizing legitimate organizations. Employees have reportedly been dismissed for union activities, and citizens have no effective recourse when those in power violate labor laws.

