*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s presidential republic is maintained through regular democratic elections. The judiciary and the media are independent, and civil liberties are generally upheld. The government has sought to combat official corruption in recent years. Many in the country’s large [POPULATION FIGURE] of foreign workers remain vulnerable to exploitation.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the director of the [COUNTRY]'s narcotics enforcement agency was a victim of an arson attack, in which his vehicle was set on fire. He believes the action could be interpreted as retaliation for a series of upcoming drug trials.
Earlier that year, the government placed a moratorium on all cryptocurrencies, after a company claimed to be a government-backed venture favoring diplomatic ties with a foreign power, despite the country’s allegiances to another nation.

---

## C Functioning of Government

[COUNTRY]'s democratically elected government determines and implements policy without undue interference. Traditional chiefs formally play an advisory role regarding customary matters through the national council of chiefs. While they also exercise informal influence over government policy, this is widely seen as a positive check on potential mismanagement or abuse of power by elected officials.
An agreement with a major world power, in effect since [COUNTRY] became independent, ensures self-government in the country but also provides for close military and economic relations with that power, including responsibility for [COUNTRY]'s defense. A scheduled review of the agreement resulted in a bilateral agreement on development aid and other benefits, but legislation to implement that agreement remained stalled in the legislature of the major world power for several years, during which time the legislature continued to appropriate basic aid funds on an annual basis. At the end of a recent year, the president of the major world power signed a defense bill that included implementing provisions for the agreement review. Negotiations for the extension of the agreement began recently: the major world power hopes an extension will counter the influence of another major world power in the region.
[COUNTRY] reconfirmed its support and recognition of a certain government as an independent state, amidst several other nations in the region withdrawing their backing and strengthening ties with the other major world power. In a recent year, the government of the other major world power instructed agencies to cease commercial flights and group tours to [COUNTRY]—likely a response to [COUNTRY]'s diplomatic relations with the certain government. In a recent year, the tourism industry in [COUNTRY] was expected to be further impacted by tensions with the government of the other major world power. Earlier, [COUNTRY] placed a moratorium on all cryptocurrencies, after a company claimed to be a government-backed venture favoring diplomatic ties with the other major world power, despite [COUNTRY]'s allegiances to the certain government.
High-ranking public officials have faced corruption charges in recent years, and several have been convicted. The government and lawmakers have deliberated on strategies to deal with corruption more effectively, and recently the finance minister issued a statement to reiterate the government's ethics rules and a whistleblower protection policy. The office of the ombudsman has asked for a clearer legal mandate with greater independence, as it currently operates as part of the president's office under an executive order.
Recently, a major international organization said that [COUNTRY] authorities had pledged to implement certain reforms, and that it had consequently removed [COUNTRY] from its list of tax havens.
Legislation provides for public access to official documents and hearings, and government officials are obliged to submit annual financial disclosures that are available to the public. However, authorities have sometimes resisted disclosing requested information, particularly at the subnational level.

