*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution and law provide for freedom of speech, including for the press, but the government often restricted this right.
Freedom of Expression : The government restricted citizens’ ability to criticize its actions. It also restricted some political symbols, musical lyrics, and theatrical performances.
The government published guidelines that banned the public from wearing a specific symbol, saying that it would henceforth be considered a military uniform and therefore the exclusive property of the state. This symbol had been worn by supporters of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] movement. The [RULING PARTY] reported that the security forces had started arresting [OPPOSITION PARTY] supporters whom they found wearing the symbol. The security forces on numerous occasions also confiscated [OPPOSITION PARTY] movement insignia. The security forces raided the [OPPOSITION PARTY] offices, arrested several supporters, and confiscated items with pro-[OPPOSITION PARTY] messages. The security forces said the items bore messages inciting violence. The security forces released the supporters later that day and said it only called them in for interrogation.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The country had an active media environment with numerous privately owned newspapers and television and radio stations. These media outlets regularly covered stories and often provided commentary critical of the government and officials. The security forces’ media unit, however, closely monitored all radio, television, and print media, and security forces subjected numerous journalists to harassment, intimidation, and arrest. Government officials and [RULING PARTY] members owned many of the private rural radio stations and imposed reporting restrictions. Media practitioners said government and security agents occasionally called editors and instructed them not to publish stories that negatively portrayed the government. The communications regulator wrote to broadcast houses ordering the suspension of several journalists for violating minimum broadcast standards when they aired live images of an [OPPOSITION PARTY] procession through the capital. The regulator also ordered the media houses to submit all footage aired that day for investigation. The [NGO] and several private attorneys filed an application in court to block the regulator's action, which a court granted, indicating that the regulator had overstepped its mandate.
Violence and Harassment : Security forces subjected journalists and media houses to violence, harassment, and intimidation. The security forces arrested several local and international journalists who were working undercover to report on a story. The security forces stated that it arrested the journalists on charges of “illegal possession of classified information.” The security forces released the journalists on police bond but said investigations into the case continued. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] contacts also reported that the president expelled a journalist from a press conference after the reporter asked a question about the country’s fiscal debt.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : The government penalized those who published items counter to its guidelines, and directly and indirectly censored the media, including by controlling licensing and advertising, instructing editors to suspend critical journalists, arresting and beating journalists, and disrupting and ransacking photojournalistic exhibitions. The media, under government pressure, practiced self-censorship. A television station aired live footage as the [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader launched his presidential bid in his home but edited out parts of his speech that were critical of the regime and of the president. The communications regulator announced that it required online publishers, bloggers, and influencers to register with them for an annual license. The security forces on several occasions switched off and broke into radio station studios that hosted an [OPPOSITION PARTY] politician for talk shows. The security forces switched off a radio transmission, and then forced its way into the studios where the politician was attending a talk show and arrested him.
Libel/Slander Laws : Authorities used libel and slander laws to suppress criticism of government officials. The authorities arrested a journalist for offensive communication and criminal libel in relation to stories he published about companies with ties to the president’s family.
National Security : Authorities cited laws protecting national security to restrict criticism of government policies. Security agencies arrested numerous dissidents on charges of incitement of violence. Security officials arrested an events manager and [OPPOSITION PARTY] supporter as he held a press conference to announce an event, on charges of inciting violence and disturbing the president’s peace. The security officials arraigned him in court and released him on bail. The case continued.

Internet Freedom

The government restricted and disrupted access to the internet, censored online content, monitored internet communications without appropriate legal authority, and punished internet users who expressed divergent political views. The security forces arrested a pastor and former journalist on the accusation that he wrote “grossly offensive” posts on social media that referred to the president in a derogatory manner. The security forces said it would use “its acquired abilities to monitor comments on social media,” and punish offenders. The pastor told local media that security officers beat him until he bled in the face and took photos of his bruised face, before demanding that he promise never to insult the president’s family. The police released the pastor without charge.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The government restricted some artistic presentations. The government throughout the year blocked the [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader from holding concerts at various locations across the country, allegedly because his previous concerts fell short of security guidelines. The government blocked concert performances by a musician after he announced that he had joined the [OPPOSITION PARTY]. The government published new regulations on the performing arts that required all artists to seek government clearance before recording any material or staging performances.

