*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

While [COUNTRY] holds regular elections, their credibility has deteriorated over time, and the country has been ruled by the same party and the president for decades. The ruling party, [RULING PARTY], retains power through the manipulation of state resources, intimidation by the security forces, and politicized prosecutions of opposition leaders. [COUNTRY]'s civil society and independent media sectors suffer from legal and extralegal harassment and state violence.

## Key Developments

Police repeatedly used force to disrupt rallies, protests, and other events organized by the political opposition, in some cases arresting opposition leaders.
The media regulator ordered the suspension of 39 journalists at 13 outlets, though the move was later blocked by a court, and issued a directive requiring social media accounts with large followings to register and submit to official monitoring.
A prominent academic was sentenced to 18 months in prison under legislation for posts that were critical of the president.
The interior minister called on thousands of [NGO]s to cease their activities after they failed to pass a reregistration process.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The media sector features many independent outlets, but their journalists face arrest, harassment, intimidation, and assault in reprisal for their work. A team of foreign journalists were arrested and temporarily detained while investigating the illegal sale of drugs from public health facilities that year. Authorities raided or shut down a number of radio stations for hosting a prominent opposition figure, and several journalists were arrested while attempting to cover protests and [OPPOSITION PARTY] events. In recent months, police forcibly dispersed journalists who had gathered to protest police brutality against their colleagues.

Both reporters and outlets are at risk of suspension and other forms of regulatory interference. The media regulator, a government body, ordered a major newspaper to shut down its website, accusing it of failing to register under a recent directive requiring all online media to obtain permission to operate. A few months later, the regulator ordered the suspension of 39 journalists at 13 television and radio stations over their coverage of protests that followed the arrest and detention of a popular musician and opposition figure. Two activists went to court to challenge the suspensions on behalf of a [NGO] that represents journalists, and the supreme court in the capital ruled in their favor, blocking the regulator's action. Later, the regulator required social media accounts with large followings to register and submit to official monitoring. The regulator warned several broadcasters that their coverage of political affairs and protests violated their license agreements, and ordered a local radio station to suspend a popular political talk show, citing complaints from the security forces.

