## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

Multiparty presidential and legislative elections held that year ushered in a new government and laid a foundation for the continued development of democratic institutions. Despite extreme poverty, terrorism, and government attempts to curtail press freedoms, civil society and organized labor remain strong forces for democracy and for the respect of civil liberties.

## Key Developments

Islamist militants launched violent attacks in northern and eastern regions of [COUNTRY] throughout the year, targeting a religious minority group and individuals wearing religious paraphernalia. Clashes with militants and reprisals by the security forces forced [POPULATION FIGURE] people to flee their homes by year’s end.
In recent years, the security chief and the interior minister took action against individuals accused of plotting a coup attempt, with two high-ranking officials being convicted by a military tribunal, receiving 20– and 10-year prison terms respectively.
The parliament adopted a revised penal code that criminalizes the dissemination of information related to terrorist attacks; the revised code also criminalizes speech that can “demoralize” the defense and security services.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Laws are promulgated and debated by the parliament. While democratic institutions continue to develop, they are not yet strong enough to withstand the influence of the security forces and other elite groups. Attacks by a jihadist militant group, which have increased in frequency in recent years, severely impede the government’s ability to implement its policies in insecure regions.
Corruption is widespread, particularly among customs officials and municipal police. Anticorruption laws and bodies are generally ineffective, though local [NGO]s provide some accountability by publicizing official corruption and its effects.
The successful elections that year and installation of a civilian government signified a marked improvement in government accountability and transparency. However, government procurement processes are opaque, and procedures meant to increase transparency are often not followed. Government officials are required to make financial disclosures, but the information is rarely made public, and penalties for noncompliance do not appear to be enforced.

