*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedom of speech, including for the press, but the government sometimes failed to respect this right. There were significant limitations on freedom of speech. Some journalists self-censored their criticisms of the government due to harassment and fear of reprisal.
Freedom of Expression : The constitution equates criticism of the constitution with sedition. Punishment for sedition ranges from three years’ to life imprisonment.
The law limits hate speech but does not define clearly what constitutes hate speech, which permits the government broad powers of interpretation. The government may restrict speech deemed to be against the security of the state; against friendly relations with foreign states; and against public order, decency, or morality; or that constitutes contempt of court, defamation, or incitement to an offense. Legislation passed that year criminalizes any criticism of constitutional bodies. A law referencing defamation of individuals and organizations was used to prosecute opposition figures and civil society.
A law passed in recent years, ostensibly to reduce cybercrimes, provides for sentences of up to 10 years’ imprisonment for spreading “propaganda” against the national history, the national anthem, or the national flag. Human rights groups, journalists, media outlets, and political opposition parties denounced the law for suppressing freedom of expression and criminalizing free speech.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Both print and online independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views; however, media outlets that criticized the government experienced negative government pressure. The government maintained editorial control over the country’s public television station and mandated private channels broadcast government content at no charge. Civil society organizations said political interference influenced the licensing process, since all television channel licenses granted by the government were for stations supporting the [RULING PARTY].
Violence and Harassment : Authorities, including the intelligence service, and student affiliates of the [RULING PARTY], subjected journalists to physical attack, harassment, and intimidation, especially when tied to the law. The law was viewed by human rights activists as a tool to intimidate journalists. The [NGO], an association of newspaper editors, stated the law stifled investigative journalism. Individuals faced a threat of being arrested, held in pretrial detention, subjected to expensive criminal trials, fines, and imprisonment, as well as social stigma associated with having a criminal record.
A journalist was arrested in a case filed under the law for mistakenly posting on social media a photograph of a police commissioner instead of a superintendent of police. Although the court twice denied the journalist’s bail, the court rejected a police request to interrogate the journalist. Observers commented police interrogation occasionally involved mistreatment of the detained. The journalist remained in prison for several years.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : Independent journalists and media outlets alleged the intelligence service influenced media outlets in part by withholding financially important government advertising and pressing private companies to withhold their advertising as well. The government penalized media that criticized it or carried messages of the [OPPOSITION PARTY]’s activities and statements. [NGO] alleged media self-censorship was growing due to “endemic violence” against journalists and media outlets and the “almost systematic impunity enjoyed by those responsible.”
Privately owned newspapers, however, usually enjoyed freedom to carry diverse views. Political polarization and self-censorship remained a problem. Investigative journalists often complained of their management and editors “killing” reports for fear of pressure from the government and its intelligence agencies. Some journalists reportedly received threats after publishing their stories.
According to some journalists and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], journalists engaged in self-censorship due to fear of security force retribution and the possibility of being charged with politically motivated cases. Although public criticism of the government was common and vocal, some media figures expressed fear of harassment by the government.
A journalist was detained to investigate accusations of violating the law by reporting “false information” about the number of votes cast during a major election. Following the election, the journalist reported that official initial elections results showed the number of votes cast was higher than the number of eligible voters. An elections official later corrected the official vote tally, lowering the number of votes cast, but reporters had already published their stories. The journalist was then arrested under the law for spreading false information. Although the journalist was released on bail, the case remained active.
Journalists claimed the government penalized media that criticized the government or broadcast the [OPPOSITION PARTY]’s activities and statements. The government cancelled the publishing rights of a daily newspaper in a major city. This move sparked a human chain protest in the city. Journalists claimed the government penalized the newspaper because it published reports criticizing the [RULING PARTY]’s local member of parliament.
Libel/Slander Laws : Libel, slander, defamation, and blasphemy are treated as criminal offenses, most commonly employed against individuals speaking against the government, the prime minister, or other government officials. The law provides for sentences of up to 10 years’ imprisonment for spreading “propaganda” against the national history, the national anthem, or the national flag. As of mid-year a total of [POPULATION FIGURE] petitions requesting an investigation had been filed under the law with more than [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals arrested.
A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] member was arrested for allegedly defaming the prime minister after saying on a television talk show that the prime minister was neglecting a particular sport in favor of another. The member was granted bail, but the charges were not dropped.
Nongovernmental Impact : [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and [ETHNIC GROUP] writers and bloggers reported they continued to receive death threats from violent extremist organizations.

Internet Freedom

The government restricted and disrupted access to the internet and censored online content in isolated incidents. The government prohibited Virtual Private Networks and Voice over Internet Protocol telephone but rarely enforced this prohibition.
In several incidents the government interfered in internet communications, filtered or blocked access, restricted content, and censored websites or other communications and internet services. It suspended or closed many websites based on vague criteria, or with explicit reference to their pro-[OPPOSITION PARTY] content being in violation of legal requirements.
The [NGO] is charged with the regulation of telecommunications. It carries out law enforcement and government requests to block content by ordering internet service providers to take action. The [NGO] filtered internet content the government deemed harmful to national unity and religious beliefs.
The government blocked a news website hours after it published an article detailing the alleged involvement of a senior security figure in a disappearance. A local news and discussion site that published a summary of the article was also inaccessible during that time. No other local or foreign outlets covered the story.
In recent years, the country’s security services instructed the [NGO] to block websites by emailing all International Internet Gateways. The [NGO] launched a new system that allowed the agencies to block websites centrally without having to involve the [NGO].

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

Although the government placed few restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events, authorities discouraged research on sensitive religious and political topics that might fuel possible religious or communal tensions. Academic publications on the national history were also subject to scrutiny and government approval.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

The [RULING PARTY] has consolidated political power through sustained harassment of the opposition and those perceived to be allied with it, as well as of critical media and voices in civil society. Corruption is a serious problem, and anticorruption efforts have been weakened by politicized enforcement. Due process guarantees are poorly upheld and security forces carry out a range of human right abuses with near impunity.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the prime minister began another term in office following the [RULING PARTY]'s victory in parliamentary elections, which were marked by violence, the intimidation of opposition candidates and supporters, allegations of fraud benefiting the ruling party, and the exclusion of nonpartisan election monitors.
The [OPPOSITION PARTY] declared a boycott of parliament following the election, but later some of its members who won seats decided to take the oath of office. Meanwhile, the [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader remained in prison, having been convicted of corruption charges ahead of the polls.
More than [POPULATION FIGURE] [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] refugees who had fled their home country in recent years remained in [COUNTRY], where most live in precarious camps that lack basic services. The [COUNTRY] government became increasingly hostile toward the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] during the year, cutting off cell phone service in the camps and erecting barbed-wire fencing around them.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

Journalists and media outlets face many forms of pressure, including frequent lawsuits, harassment, and serious or deadly physical attacks. A number of journalists were arrested or attacked in connection with reporting on topics including crimes committed during a past conflict, and election irregularities during both parliamentary and local polls. A climate of impunity for attacks on media workers remains the norm, and there has been little progress made on ensuring justice for a series of murders of bloggers that occurred over several years. Dozens of bloggers remain in hiding or exile.
Legislation passed that year allows the government to conduct searches or arrest individuals without a warrant and criminalizes various forms of speech, and was vehemently opposed by journalists.
Forms of artistic expression contained in books, films, and other materials are occasionally banned or censored.
A religion is designated as the official religion, though the constitution designates secularism as among the “high ideals” the charter is grounded in. Although religious minorities have the right to worship freely, they occasionally face legal repercussions for proselytizing. Members of minority groups—including certain ethnic and religious minority communities—face harassment and violence, including mob violence against their houses of worship. In a recent incident, a mob attacked residences of a religious minority group in a major city after rumors circulated that a member of the group posted blasphemous content on social media, and several people were shot and killed by police who responded to the chaos. Police later reported that the account had been hacked. The incident was one of a number in recent years in which violence against religious or other minorities appears to have been deliberately provoked using social media.
Those with secular or nonconformist views can face societal opprobrium and attacks from hardline groups.
In recent years, academic institutions have faced frequent threats from a variety of actors, resulting in reduced autonomy and rising self-censorship. Faculty hiring and promotion are often linked to support for the [RULING PARTY], and campus debate is often stifled by the [RULING PARTY]'s student wing. In separate incidents, members of the [RULING PARTY] student wing attacked a nonviolent protest at a major university, and the following month beat a student to death after he posted criticism of the [RULING PARTY] on social media. Separately, a researcher who exposed the presence of harmful substances in major food brands was maligned and threatened by members of the government.
Several years ago, campus demonstrations by students and professors who objected to a government policy were set upon by assailants and violently dispersed by the security forces, and many participants were arrested.
Changes made to textbooks used widely throughout the educational system—at the behest of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUPS], who demanded the removal of content they claimed was “atheistic”—raised concerns about the influence of these groups over government policy and standards. Separately, extremists have attacked secular professors.
Open private discussion of sensitive religious and political issues is restrained by fears of harassment. Prior to a recent election, repression of dissent created a climate of fear and self-censorship. Censorship of digital content and surveillance of telecommunications and social media have become increasingly common.

---

## F Rule of Law

Politicization of and pressure against the judiciary persists. Recently, the Chief Justice of the Supreme Court retired; he left the country and said, in an autobiography, that he had been forced to retire after threats from the military intelligence because of rulings he had made against the government. Later, the Anti-Corruption Commission charged the former chief justice with corruption in absentia. Other allegations of political pressure on judges continued to emerge, as did allegations that unqualified [RULING PARTY] loyalists were being appointed to court positions.
Individuals’ ability to access justice is compromised by endemic corruption within the court system and severe backlogs. Pretrial detention is often lengthy, and many defendants lack counsel. Suspects are routinely subject to arbitrary arrest and detention, demands for bribes, and physical abuse by police. Criminal cases against [RULING PARTY] activists are regularly withdrawn on the grounds of “political consideration,” undermining the judicial process and entrenching a culture of impunity.
Legislation permits arbitrary detention without charge, and the criminal procedure code allows detention without a warrant. A counterterrorism law includes a broad definition of terrorism and generally does not meet international standards. Concerns have repeatedly been raised that the tribunal’s procedures and verdicts do not meet international standards on issues such as victim and witness protection, the presumption of innocence, defendant access to counsel, and the right to bail. The tribunal continued to hand down sentences, including death sentences, recently.
Terrorist attacks by [MILITANT GROUP] continued to decline following a crackdown on these groups, during which more than 15,000 people were arrested. A monitoring group documented no civilian fatalities related to extremism recently, compared to 43 a few years ago. However, a [MILITANT GROUP] claimed credit for several nonlethal bomb attacks on police officers in the capital recently.
A range of human rights abuses by law enforcement agencies—including enforced disappearances, custodial deaths, arbitrary arrests, and torture—have continued unabated. A [NGO] report documented the use of detention and enforced disappearance against members of the [OPPOSITION PARTY], despite the government’s promise to address the issue. The government initiated a “war on drugs,” during which thousands were arrested and over 100 people were killed.
A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported a total of 391 extrajudicial killings perpetrated by law enforcement agencies recently. A report from the [NGO] found that 507 people had been subject to enforced disappearance over the past decade. Prison conditions are extremely poor; severe overcrowding is common, and juveniles are often incarcerated with adults.
Members of [ETHNIC GROUP] and [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] face some discrimination under law as well as harassment and violations of their rights in practice. Indigenous people in a major region, [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], and other [ETHNIC GROUP] remain subject to physical attacks, property destruction, land grabs, and occasional abuses by security forces.
[COUNTRY] has hosted roughly [POPULATION FIGURE] [ETHNIC GROUP] who fled from a neighboring country. The vast majority do not have official refugee status; suffer from a complete lack of access to health care, employment, and education; and are subject to substantial harassment. In response to a sharp escalation in violence directed against [ETHNIC GROUP] in the neighboring country, many refugees poured across the border into [COUNTRY], creating a humanitarian crisis. Most live in precarious camps that lack basic services. Authorities reached a repatriation agreement with the neighboring country, but the UN refugee agency said conditions in the neighboring country were not fit for the refugees’ return and that safeguards for them were “absent.” Subsequent efforts to repatriate [ETHNIC GROUP] have failed, and the government has become increasingly hostile toward the refugees. Recently, authorities cut off cell phone service in refugee camps and erected barbed-wire fencing around them.
A criminal ban on same-sex sexual acts is rarely enforced, but societal discrimination remains the norm, and dozens of attacks on LGBT+ individuals are reported every year. A number of LGBT individuals remain in exile following the murder of a prominent LGBT activist by [MILITANT GROUP]. Some legal recognition is available for transgender people, though in practice they face severe discrimination.

