*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedom of speech and press, and the government generally respected these rights, although with some unofficial limits.
Freedom of Expression : Individuals could generally criticize the government publicly or privately, but civil libel and slander laws placed limits on freedom of speech, and self-censorship was widespread. Some media outlets avoided criticizing government officials due to fears of legal sanction and to retain government advertising, which, according to [NGO], was the largest source of media revenue. Other outlets avoided addressing sensitive human rights issues like female genital mutilation/cutting (FGM/C). Court decisions against journalists sometimes involved exorbitant fines.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views. Some media outlets, journalists, and broadcasters charged fees to publish articles or host radio programs. According to [NGO], civil suits relating to libel, slander, and defamation were sometimes used to curtail freedom of expression and intimidate the press. [NGO] also expressed concern media outlets owned directly by politicians and government officials were crowding out privately owned media and advocated for legislation to prohibit ownership of media by public officials.
Violence and Harassment : Law enforcement officers occasionally harassed newspaper and radio station owners because of their political opinions and reporting, especially those that criticized government officials. Government officials also harassed media members for political reasons. For example, a major newspaper reported cabinet members were pressuring a major corporation to fire a public relations manager who criticized the government on social media and through a website, where he previously worked. Separately, a judge summoned a publisher of a newspaper for possible contempt of court because of an article he wrote that speculated there was a plot to exonerate defendants in a major corruption case.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : Although generally able to express a wide variety of views, some journalists practiced self-censorship to avoid harassment. Journalists and media directors also practiced self-censorship to maintain advertising revenue from the government, the largest advertiser in the country. There were several reports that politicians and government agencies offered “transportation fees” to journalists to secure coverage of events.
For several months, the radio show of a government critic was frequently unavailable. On at least a few occasions, the broadcast seemed to feature older, pro-government clips, leading to speculation that the station was being jammed or otherwise interfered with. In response, the critic made a number of threats of violence in online broadcasts. The government’s reactions to these and other broadcasts from the critic, which the government deemed as inciting violence, included a suspension of a radio station’s broadcast license due to nonpayment of fees and inciting violence. Amid groups of protesters supporting the critic, law enforcement officers, escorted by armed units, stormed the radio station’s studio, shut the broadcast down, and seized the station’s broadcasting equipment.
Libel /Slander Laws : Legislation passed that year repealed criminal libel and slander laws. Government officials occasionally used the threat of civil suits to intimidate critics. The interior minister filed a defamation suit against a radio station and its hosts, alleging the two radio personalities “slandered, badmouthed, vandalized and vilified” the minister by accusing him of financial impropriety. The minister later dropped the suit.
[NGO] continued efforts to self-regulate the media and ensure adherence to standards including investigation and settlement of complaints against or by the press. [NGO]’s media council, launched in recent years to address court cases against the media, continued to mediate cases.

Internet Freedom

In the lead-up to and during a planned protest, the government disrupted access to the internet. A website reported widespread social media blockages on both major mobile networks. When protesters dispersed, access was restored. Cell phone providers announced to customers they had blocked internet access at the instruction of the government. The information minister later confirmed the government requested the shut down and invoked unspecified national security concerns for doing so, while also criticizing protest organizers.
There were no additional reports the government censored online content, and there were no reports the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority.
There were reports of government officials threatening legal action and filing civil lawsuits to censor protected internet-based speech and intimidate senders.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events.

---

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution and law provide citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : Legislative by-elections in recent years filled one seat in the upper house of the parliament and one seat in the lower house made vacant by the deaths of the incumbents. The electoral commission declared [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidate winner of the upper house race; on a later date, because of irregularities in voter registration, the electoral commission conducted a run-off in several voting precincts for the lower house seat. On the same day, the electoral commission declared [RULING PARTY] candidate winner. There were incidents of violence, including stone-throwing and property damage, at campaign events.
Political Parties and Political Participation : There were reports new positions in government were created for members of the [RULING PARTY] as a reward for, or benefit of, party affiliation without regard to the need to create such positions and outside the standard hiring process of the civil service agency. Some government officials who were not members of the [RULING PARTY] were harassed. A “blacklist” of nonparty members working in government circulated widely on social media. Newspapers reported a group of men associated with the [RULING PARTY] stormed the office of a government director after seeing his name on the blacklist; police intervention was required to restore order. The director resigned after the incident.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit participation of women or members of minorities in the political process, and they did participate. Some observers believed traditional and cultural factors limited women’s participation in politics as compared with the participation of men. Women participated at significantly lower levels than men as party leaders and as elected officials. Election law requires that political parties “endeavor to ensure 30 percent” female participation. Several candidates competed for a vacant seat in the upper house and several competed for a vacant seat in the lower house in recent by-elections; of the upper house candidates, several were women, and of the lower house candidates, one was a woman. Overall, a significant percentage of candidates in the by-elections were women. Similar by-elections in previous years saw just one female candidate of a total of several candidates. The electoral commission had a policy to track voters by gender at all polling stations, but implementation during the by-elections was uneven, and the commission did not provide final data.
Members of a religious minority group were active participants in the by-elections. Unlike in previous elections, a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported no incidents of discrimination or harassment during the by-elections.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has enjoyed more than a decade of peace and stability since the second civil war ended. During this time, the country has made considerable progress rebuilding government capacity, reestablishing the rule of law, and ensuring the political rights and civil liberties of citizens, and that year saw the first peaceful transfer of power between leaders in many years. However, [COUNTRY] still faces serious issues with corruption, violence against women, and discrimination against LGBT+ people.

## Key Developments

The government partially restricted access to social networks and messaging services in recent years, ahead of an [OPPOSITION PARTY]-led rally calling for economic reform and an end to pervasive corruption. 
In recent months, the supreme court associate justice was removed by the parliament after he issued a writ in favor of petroleum dealers fighting a government levy on gasoline sales. The justice criticized the trial, saying it was conducted unconstitutionally, and appealed to a regional court; the case was under consideration at the end of the period.
A lower-house by-election was partially rerun after an [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidate alleged irregularities on the part of electoral officers; the candidate and her supporters were physically attacked twice during the election campaign, which ended with a victory for the [RULING PARTY] candidate.

---

## A Electoral Process

The president of [COUNTRY] is directly elected, and can serve up to two six-year terms. Since the end of the civil wars, [COUNTRY] has had three peaceful presidential elections. The most recent election, held that year, was commended by domestic and international observers who assessed it as generally peaceful and credible, while also noting difficulties including long queues at polling places and challenges related to voter identification.
A runoff between the president and the incumbent vice president of [COUNTRY], the top two finishers in the first round of the poll, was delayed when a third-place finisher challenged the first-round results on grounds of fraud. The supreme court found that the fraud claim was not supported by evidence, and the runoff was held several weeks later than scheduled, in late December. The president won the runoff with 61.5 percent of the vote, and the vice president conceded defeat. Observers noted procedural and administrative improvements in the runoff compared to the first round. The president’s inauguration marked the first peaceful transfer of power in many years.
[COUNTRY] has a bicameral legislature composed of a 30-member Senate and a 73-member House of Representatives; senators are elected to nine-year terms, and representatives to six-year terms.
Lower-house elections were held concurrently with the first round of the presidential election. The [RULING PARTY] won 21 seats with 15.6 percent of the vote, while the [OPPOSITION PARTY] won 20 seats with 14 percent of the vote. Another party won 8.7 percent of the vote, but won 3 seats. A party allied to the [RULING PARTY] won 5 seats with 5.9 percent of the vote. The remaining 24 seats were won by independents and other parties. While there were some administrative problems, including complaints that registered voters could not be found on the voter rolls, observers said the elections were generally peaceful and well administered, with only minor incidents of violence during the campaign.
Two by-elections were held in a major city, which includes the capital, to fill a Senate seat and a lower-house seat. A candidate from another party won the Senate seat, but an opposition candidate claimed that the lower-house contest was marred by irregularities on the part of electoral officers, prompting the electoral commission to order a partial rerun. The [RULING PARTY] candidate won the lower-house seat in late August. Turnout for the contests was low. Another senatorial by-election was held in a different area, with the allied party candidate winning the seat. Turnout was also low in this contest, and some voters were unable to participate for want of sufficient identification.
The electoral commission’s independence is mandated by law, and political parties expressed confidence in its impartiality during the election campaign. However, its capacity is limited, and it struggles to enforce electoral laws.
The elections were the first to apply the provisions of legislation passed that year, which laid out rules applying to government officials seeking elected office, and included measures aimed at avoiding conflicts of interest. The electoral commission attempted to enforce the legislation, but the supreme court reversed its rulings in two instances where it disqualified high-profile candidates for failing to meet the eligibility requirements. The electoral commission also failed to enforce an election law provision stipulating that parties must field candidates in at least half of all constituencies. Eleven parties did not meet this requirement, but were permitted to run. The electoral commission additionally struggled to complete voter lists.
The electoral commission also struggled to conduct the by-elections in the major city. The polls were originally scheduled for early in the summer, but were held later that month due to a lack of funding, which also hampered voter education efforts. [COUNTRY] has a population of [POPULATION FIGURE].

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

[COUNTRY]'s constitution provides for freedom of speech and the press, but these rights are sometimes restricted in practice. Investigative reporters frequently receive threats, including by members of the government who vowed to sue in response to journalistic inquiries. The president has previously taken an adversarial stance toward media, making vague denunciations of “fake news” that purportedly threatened national stability. [COUNTRY] also maintained onerous criminal and civil libel laws, but the government adopted legislation that effectively decriminalized libel, “sedition,” and “criminal malevolence” that year. Defamation remains a civil offense, and journalists risk jail time for nonpayment.
Meanwhile, the suspension of media licenses issued in recent years remained in force. The government originally pledged to conduct a review process for licenses, but no progress was made. Critics argued the move was aimed at newly established news outlets perceived to be critical of the government.
A private radio station in the capital was attacked twice by armed assailants, and broadcast equipment was stolen during one of the attacks. The government sued a radio host, who was also a leader of an [OPPOSITION PARTY], and a cohost for civil defamation, alleging that they engaged in slander when discussing accusations of impropriety during a major election. Later that year, the government forced the radio station off the air, sending police officers to seize the station’s equipment.
Religious freedom is protected in the constitution, and there is no official religion. However, a significant portion of the population, around [POPULATION FIGURE] percent, belongs to a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group, while a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group reports discrimination. In recent years, a proposal to amend the constitution to establish a particular religion as the official religion contributed to interreligious tensions. The former head of state shelved the proposal, but some discussion of it reemerged during a major campaign. Since taking office, the president has made efforts to reach out to the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] population.
The government does not restrict academic freedom, though educational quality and infrastructure remain inadequate.
People are generally free to engage in private discussion while in public spaces, but some topics are taboo, such as discussion of issues affecting certain minority groups. The government is not known to illegally monitor online communications, though it did restrict access to social media networks and messaging services ahead of a major protest.

