*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

Citizens do not have the ability to choose their government. The ruler rules through hereditary birthright. While [COUNTRY] is a constitutional entity, for many years the ruler has invoked an article of the constitution that allows them to assume emergency powers. The present ruler continued this practice and most recently renewed the state of emergency for an additional two-year period in a proclamation.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : Political authority and control rest entirely with the ruler. The legislative body, composed primarily of appointed members with little independent power, provided a forum for public discussion of proposed government programs, budgets, and administrative deficiencies. It convenes once per year for approximately two weeks. Council members serve five-year terms at the pleasure of the ruler.
Persons age 18 and older may vote by secret ballot in local consultative council elections. Candidates must be of a certain religious affiliation, approved by the relevant government ministry, and have been a citizen or permanent resident for more than 15 years. The councils communicate constituent wishes to higher authorities through a variety of channels, including periodic meetings chaired by a government minister. The government also meets with groups of elected local leaders to allow them to express local grievances and concerns.
Political Parties and Political Participation : [RULING PARTY] is the only registered political party. The party pledged to support the ruler and the government. It made no criticisms of the government during that year.
Participation of Women and Minorities : The constitution requires that all ministers be of a certain ethnic group and religious affiliation except as permitted by the ruler. The cabinet included members of certain ethnic minority communities, and members of [ETHNIC GROUP] also held senior government positions. Women accounted for more than half of civil service employees, and many held senior positions, including at the deputy-minister level. Women are subject to an earlier mandatory retirement age than men (55 versus 60 years), which may inhibit their career progression. The law requires that elected local leaders be of a certain ethnic and religious background.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is an absolute monarchy in which the monarch exercises executive power. There are no elected representatives at the national level. Freedoms of the press and assembly are significantly restricted. Online speech is monitored by the authorities, but lively nevertheless.

## Key Developments

In recent years, [COUNTRY] implemented a controversial penal code based on Islamic law; the code, which applies to Muslims and non-Muslims, includes whippings, amputation, and death by stoning as possible sentences. Shortly after, the monarch issued a de facto moratorium on the death penalty, but did not address the code’s other provisions.
The government continued prosecuting cases of corruption. A former official of a major industry was indicted over undocumented income from other sources; the trial was ongoing. 
A former civil servant received a sentence in absentia for criticizing regulations in a social media post made several years prior. The civil servant, who fled to another country to seek asylum, was the first to be convicted under legislation targeting sedition.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

Genuine political activity by opposition groups remains extremely limited. The [RULING PARTY] was permitted to register after pledging to work as a partner with the government and swearing loyalty to the head of state; it is the only registered party.
There are no national-level elections in which opposition forces could gain power. Since an [OPPOSITION PARTY] was deregistered without explanation, the [RULING PARTY] has been [COUNTRY]'s sole legal political party. It has no formal political role, few activities in practice, and a small membership, and is unable to challenge the head of state's power in any meaningful way.
With the dominance of the head of state and lack of elections, residents have few avenues for genuine and autonomous political participation. However, people have some very limited ability to challenge unpopular policies through the organization of social movements.
[Certain ethnic minority communities] and [a religious minority group] have few opportunities for political participation, even on a local level. Village council candidates must be Muslim, and ministers and deputy ministers must be Muslim and from [an ethnic group] unless the head of state grants an exception.

---

## C Functioning of Government

None of [COUNTRY]'s national-level policymakers are chosen through elections. The head of state wields broad powers, and is counseled by appointed advisory bodies and the appointed legislature.
In recent years, the government enacted amendments to legislation aimed at preventing corruption, which strengthened the anticorruption framework by establishing new conflict of interest rules for public officials, among other provisions. The government claims to have a zero-tolerance policy on corruption, and its anticorruption agency has successfully prosecuted a number of lower-level officials in recent years. A few years ago, two former judges were indicted for allegedly embezzling over $7 million from a court's bankruptcy office. Their trial was still in session at the end of that year.
Recently, prosecutors accused a former [NGO] officer of possessing “unexplained property.” Prosecutors alleged that the individual did not maintain necessary documentation for previous income; their trial began shortly after and was ongoing. 
Although the appointed legislature has no independent power, it formally passes the state budget and engages in question-and-answer sessions with government officials. The legislature meets once each year for a session lasting approximately two weeks. However, in general there is little transparency in the operations of the [COUNTRY] government.

