*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a monarchy in which the king plays a dominant role in politics and governance. The parliament’s lower house is elected, but the electoral system puts the [OPPOSITION PARTY] at a disadvantage, and the chamber wields little power in practice. The media and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s are hampered by restrictive laws and government pressure. The judicial system lacks independence and often fails to ensure due process.

## Key Developments

Mass demonstrations against unemployment, stagnant or declining public-sector salaries, and corruption became common, with teachers launching a major strike over wages. The increase in dissent prompted the authorities to arrest dozens of activists.
The government continued to issue work permits to [REFUGEE GROUP] refugees in keeping with a funding agreement, with more than 153,000 distributed, though the presence of some [POPULATION FIGURE] registered and many more unregistered [REFUGEE GROUP] refugees placed serious strains on the country’s public services.

---

## A Electoral Process

The king holds broad executive powers. He appoints and dismisses the prime minister and cabinet and may dissolve the bicameral parliament at his discretion. The prime minister, a former economist and minister of education, was appointed in recent years, replacing the previous prime minister, who resigned in the wake of mass protests over proposed tax increases and hikes in fuel and electricity prices. Constitutional amendments adopted for several years ago empowered the king to make a number of other appointments, including a regent, without a royal decree countersigned by the prime minister or other cabinet ministers.

The king appoints the members of the upper house of the parliament. The lower house is elected for four-year terms or until the parliament is dissolved. Its members win office through races in multimember districts, with seats reserved for women candidates and certain ethnic and religious minority communities. 

In recent elections for the lower house, the first held under a new electoral system, the main opposition party participated after boycotting the previous two elections. It took a number of seats, and its allies won several more. However, as in past polls, most seats went to independents who are typically tribal figures and businesspeople and considered loyal to the monarchy. Vote buying and other forms of manipulation remained a problem.

Elections were held for mayors, local and municipal councils, and new councils created under a decentralization law. However, a percentage of the council seats are appointed, and the councils have no legislative authority. A portion of the seats in the municipal council of the capital are also appointed by the government. As with the parliamentary elections, independent tribal candidates won the vast majority of seats, while the main opposition party and its allies won a plurality of the few seats captured by party-based candidates.

Elections are administered by the election commission, which generally receives positive reviews from international monitors in terms of technical management, though irregularities continue to be reported. A reform to the election law introduced proportional representation for parliamentary elections, replacing a single nontransferable vote system that favored progovernment tribal elites over opposition-oriented political parties. The new law also redrew district lines in an attempt to mitigate malapportionment that has long placed urban voters at a severe disadvantage. However, even after the changes, rural and tribal voters, who make up the base of support for the regime, remain heavily overrepresented in the parliament. For example, a small number of eligible voters in one district elect several members of parliament, whereas a major city, which is dominated by [ETHNIC GROUP] citizens, has a large number of voters electing a smaller number of parliament members. The legal framework for elections is unstable, with major changes often introduced just a short time before polling day, causing confusion that can hinder campaigning and voting.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The head of state dominates policymaking and the legislative process. The appointed government submits all draft legislation to the parliament, which may approve, reject, or amend bills, though they require approval from the appointed upper chamber and the head of state to become law. Groups of 10 or more lawmakers can propose legislation, but the parliament must then refer it to the government before it can return to the chamber as a draft law. Among other prerogatives, the head of state unilaterally appoints the heads of the security forces, the intelligence service, and the national police.
The government has undertaken some efforts to combat widespread corruption, and a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] tasked with investigating allegations is active. However, successful prosecutions—particularly of high-ranking officials—remain rare. Anticorruption efforts are undermined by a lack of genuinely independent enforcement institutions and restrictions on investigative journalism and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] activism.
Laws governing access to government information are vague, lack procedural detail, and contain sweeping exceptions. Officials are not required to make public declarations of their income and assets. The parliament does not exercise effective or independent oversight of the government’s budget proposals. Activists and journalists who attempt to investigate state finances are subject to arrest on defamation and other charges.

