[COUNTRY] claims the territory and administers the area it controls by the same constitution, laws, and structures as in the rest of [COUNTRY], including laws that deal with religious freedom. [REBEL GROUP], an organization seeking the territory’s independence, disputes [COUNTRY]’s claim to sovereignty over the territory. According to the constitution, [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] is the religion of the state, and the state guarantees freedom of thought, expression, and assembly. The constitution also says the state guarantees to everyone the freedom to “practice his religious affairs.” The constitution states the head of state holds a prominent [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] title and that he is the protector of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and guarantor of the freedom to practice religious affairs in [COUNTRY]. It also prohibits political parties from being founded on religion and forbids political parties, the parliament, and constitutional amendments from denigrating or infringing on [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]. The law penalizes the use of enticements to convert a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] to another religion and prohibits criticism of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]. There were no reports of significant government actions affecting religious freedom in the portion of the territory administered by [COUNTRY].

Representatives of a Christian minority group said fear of societal harassment, including ostracism by converts’ families and social ridicule, were the main reasons leading them to practice their faith discreetly.

Foreign officials discussed religious freedom and tolerance with [COUNTRY] officials and also met members of religious minority communities during visits to the territory.

