## Overview

[COUNTRY] has claimed authority over [COUNTRY] for decades, but the United Nations does not recognize [COUNTRY]'s control, calling [COUNTRY] a “non-self-governing territory.” [COUNTRY] controls the most populous area along the coastline, more than three-quarters of the territory. While the United Nations (UN) brokered a cease-fire for many years, a long-promised referendum on the territory’s status has yet to be held. The [COUNTRY]-controlled area, which [COUNTRY] calls a certain region, is represented in the parliament. However, civil liberties are severely restricted, particularly as they relate to independence activism.

## Key Developments 

The [COUNTRY] government and the nationalist [ARMED GROUP] participated in two rounds of UN-backed talks, but made little progress. The UN’s envoy to [COUNTRY], the former president of a European country, resigned for health reasons, and the position remained unfilled. 
A major trade bloc finalized an agriculture trade deal with [COUNTRY] and a fisheries agreement; both of these agreements covered territory claimed by [ARMED GROUP], which did not participate in consultations on the deals. [ARMED GROUP] launched legal action against the agreements at a major court, which remained pending.
In recent months, a parliamentary committee voted in favor of measures to expand the country’s maritime borders to include waters claimed by [ARMED GROUP]. The full legislature did not vote on the proposal, however.

