*State Department Human Rights Report*

Harassment and discrimination against members of minorities continued throughout [COUNTRY], although not as frequently as in previous years. The Interreligious Council reported, for example, that the number of attacks against religious buildings continued to decrease, as they recorded only six cases during that year. Members of minority groups also continued to experience discrimination in employment and education in both the government and private sectors. While the law prohibits discrimination, human rights activists frequently complained that authorities did not adequately enforce the law. For example, that year, 121 hate crimes were recorded in [COUNTRY], but only two resulted in convictions. On a specific date, unknown perpetrators sprayed painted Nazi and nationalist symbols on a mosque in a major city. No perpetrators were identified, but the incident was widely condemned by government authorities.

Violence and acts of intimidation against ethnic minorities at times focused on symbols and buildings of that minority’s predominant religion. For more information, see the Department of State’s International Religious Freedom Report.

[CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported that [ETHNIC GROUP], and especially [ETHNIC GROUP] women, continued to be [COUNTRY]’s most vulnerable and discriminated group. They experienced discrimination in access to housing, health care, education, and employment opportunities, and nearly 95 percent of them remained unemployed. A significant percentage of [ETHNIC GROUP] were homeless or without water or electricity in their homes. Many dwellings were overcrowded, and residents lacked proof of property ownership. Approximately three-fourths lived in openly segregated neighborhoods.

In a recent census, [POPULATION FIGURE] persons registered as [ETHNIC GROUP], a number that appears to understate significantly the actual number of [ETHNIC GROUP] in [COUNTRY]. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported that a minimum of [POPULATION FIGURE] [ETHNIC GROUP] lived in [COUNTRY], which was similar to estimates from a regional human rights organization. Observers believed the discrepancy in the census figure was the result of numerous manipulations that occurred with the [ETHNIC GROUP] census registration.

Authorities frequently discriminated against [ETHNIC GROUP], which contributed to their exclusion by society. Many human rights [NGO] criticized law enforcement and government authorities for the failure and unwillingness to identify [ETHNIC GROUP] as victims of domestic violence and human trafficking, even though the majority of registered trafficking victims in recent years were [ETHNIC GROUP]. Consequently, many trafficking cases ended up as cases of family negligence, which are not criminally prosecuted.

[COUNTRY] has an established legal framework for the protection of minorities. The parliament had national minority councils that met on a regular basis but generally lacked resources and political influence on decision-making processes. A committee for [ETHNIC GROUP] continued to operate as a consultative body to the government, but with very limited influence.

[COUNTRY] does not have a comprehensive strategy on national minorities. The ministry in charge of human rights is responsible for implementing a law on national minorities, for which it annually allocates a certain amount of funds. [COUNTRY] has a council of national minorities, which is an advisory body to the government and is composed of one representative from each recognized national minority group. [COUNTRY] lacked human rights and antidiscrimination strategies, and the government does not have an effective system of collecting discrimination cases.

The government continued to implement an action plan to improve employment, housing, and health care for [ETHNIC GROUP] and a separate action plan on [ETHNIC GROUP] educational needs. That year, the government allocated funds for employment, healthcare, and housing of [ETHNIC GROUP]. At lower levels of government, these funds are regularly matched by additional funds from governmental and donor funds. Several local communities had local community plans to assist [ETHNIC GROUP].

Acts of Violence, Discrimination, and Other Abuses Based on Sexual Orientation and Gender Identity

While the law prohibits discrimination based on sexual orientation, authorities did not fully enforce it. Both the government and local authorities have laws that criminalize any form of hate crime committed on the basis of gender, sexual orientation, or gender identity.

Hate speech, discrimination, and violence against LGBTI individuals were widespread. An [NGO] reported that transgender persons were the most vulnerable LGBTI group. In its report, the [NGO] reported that every third LGBTI person in [COUNTRY] experienced some type of discrimination. The [NGO] believed the actual number of LGBTI persons who experienced some type of discrimination was much higher but that people were afraid to report it. That year, the [NGO] documented several discrimination cases, some of which involved workplace discrimination and others that involved discrimination in access to public services and goods. The [NGO] also documented cases of hate speech and crimes motivated by sexual orientation and gender identity. The perpetrators in all cases were family members. A survey by the [NGO] showed that two-thirds of transgender persons experienced some type of discrimination. The prosecution of assault and other crimes committed against LGBTI individuals remained delayed and generally inadequate. The [NGO] reported that, to date, the courts have never issued a final judgment that found discrimination had occurred on the basis of sexual orientation and gender-based identity.

LGBTI persons faced frequent harassment and discrimination, including termination of employment. [NGO] also reported that schools were increasingly hostile environments, where LGBTI persons regularly experienced harassment and violence. In some cases, dismissal letters from work explicitly stated that sexual orientation was the cause of termination, making it extremely difficult for those dismissed to find another job. In the face of such risks, LGBTI persons rarely reported discrimination to the police.

Prior to [COUNTRY]’s first LGBTI Pride March, numerous social media posts were directed against a foreign embassy and ambassador for supporting the right of the organizers to hold the march. Referring to LGBTI persons, a representative of [RULING PARTY] posted on social media: “I want these people isolated and moved as far as possible from our children and society.” A conservative [NGO] organized a counter demonstration that took place before the start of the Pride March. The stated goal was to demonstrate against the public expression of sexual orientation, which they deemed to be incompatible with traditional values. Participants carried banners with offensive messages against the LGBTI population but disbanded peacefully. The day prior to the Pride March, approximately [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals participated in a separate march. A spokesperson stated organizers wished to draw attention to what it called “traditional families.”

---

The constitution of [COUNTRY] and each of the country’s two entities – the [ENTITY] and the [ENTITY] – provide for freedom of religious thought and practice, prohibit religious discrimination, and allow registered religious organizations to operate freely. The [ENTITY] constitution declares religion to be “a vital national interest” of the constituent peoples. The [ENTITY] constitution establishes [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] as “the Church of the [ETHNIC GROUP] people and other people of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] religion.” The [COUNTRY] constitution reserves all positions in the presidency and one of two houses of the parliament and certain other government offices to members of the three major [ETHNIC GROUP] – predominantly [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]-member [ETHNIC GROUP], predominantly [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] [ETHNIC GROUP], and predominantly [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] [ETHNIC GROUP]. The human rights ministry issued new regulations allowing reporting of religious freedom abuses directly to the ministry, which is then charged with working with relevant authorities to correct the abuses. Religious groups in areas where they were a local minority reported continued government discrimination regarding denial of permits for construction or repair of religious properties, and in education, employment, and provision of social services. The presidency again failed to approve an agreement that would provide religious accommodations to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] workers. In a report, the [NGO] said a school threatened to punish [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] students if they did not make up classes missed during a religious holiday. The same report said the military served [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] soldiers pork over a period of time. The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], a nongovernmental organization comprising representatives of the country’s four major religious communities, again reported authorities moved unacceptably slowly to investigate and prosecute religiously motivated crimes. In an interview, the speaker of the [CITY] assembly said he did not mind that a local elementary school continued to be named for a World War II-era figure who glorified a notorious leader.

The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] registered [NUMBER] reported acts of vandalism against religious sites and one case of verbal abuse against a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] priest and said the actual number of incidents was likely much higher. The [NGO] reported receiving reports of [NUMBER] incidents of bias against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], [NUMBER] against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], and [NUMBER] against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]. The one incident of violence reported involved an assault and verbal insults against a [ETHNIC GROUP] man during a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] holiday. Anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] incidents included shots being fired at a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] site, theft, and vandalism against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] sites involving [desecration], broken windows, or graffiti. In the anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] incidents, vandals painted graffiti, including [hate symbols], on [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] housing. The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] continued to promote interfaith dialogue through conferences and projects with local governments.

Representatives of the [FOREIGN GOVERNMENT] embassy emphasized to government officials the need to promote respect for religious diversity and enforce equal treatment for religious minorities. In regular meetings with religious groups, embassy officials continued to urge these groups to improve interreligious dialogue to help develop a peaceful and stable society. The embassy continued to maintain regular contact with the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and to fund some of its interfaith activities.

