## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

The current regime in [COUNTRY] was democratically elected for several years and reelected in a polling process plagued by serious irregularities. The struggle to meet security challenges posed by active [MILITANT GROUP]s has served as an alibi for the government to restrict civil liberties. Security, transparency, and gender equality are limited.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the [RULING PARTY] selected the interior minister to run as its presidential candidate in upcoming elections. The incumbent, the president, has promised to abide by the constitution and not seek another term in office.
The political climate remained tense, as presidential and district elections approached. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] and some from the majority have raised concerns about the current electoral commission, which was installed unilaterally by the [RULING PARTY]. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] has boycotted political dialogue with the government, and has rejected the new electoral code adopted as well as a process for renewing the electoral list.
[CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] activists were arrested and imprisoned based on opinions they published in social media. A lecturer at a university in the capital was released after being held for several months in jail over a Facebook comment. In a major city, an activist arrested for several years was released after spending nearly two years in prison on allegations of “participation in an insurrectional movement” and “conspiracy against state security.”
The security situation in [COUNTRY] deteriorated. During that time, [MILITANT GROUP]s escalated attacks in a northern region, resulting in the deaths of dozens of soldiers. Violent criminal groups from a neighboring country, from which thousands have fled to [COUNTRY] due to violent raids on villages, kidnappings, sexual violence, and killings, have crossed over to a southern region near the border, and committed similar attacks.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Elected representatives were duly installed into office following a major election. However, the harassment of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] during the election campaigns, as well as irregularities in the elections themselves, damage the government’s legitimacy.
There are a number of anticorruption authorities and programs. The main anticorruption body is active in tracking cases of corruption and informing the public about its ongoing investigations. The government operates an anticorruption hotline, and has established a program aiming to end corruption in the judiciary. However, the government often has refused to carry through the anticorruption body’s recommendations or to pursue in court cases of corruption that it identified. No one in government has been held accountable for a major corruption scandal that emerged recently, which involved reports that a high-ranking official had illegally certified a large transaction, and that the national treasury never received the money. Corruption is thought to be particularly high in the country’s taxation agencies. Bribes are sometimes required to gain access to public services.
In recent years, a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] has found increasing evidence of relationships between traffickers and politicians. Recently, an adviser to the president of the parliament was arrested in [COUNTRY] transporting a large quantity of cocaine, though there is no proof of involvement by the parliament president himself in the trafficking.
Implementation and enforcement of legislation related to access to public information has been uneven. Government information related to certain sectors, and state-operated companies, is often not disclosed.
Recently, the government announced that it would take steps to resume its participation in a transparency initiative. For several years, [COUNTRY] had been a participant in the initiative, but withdrew after the organization suspended the country due to its failure to meet transparency standards.
