## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

While it is considered one of the most stable democracies in the region, [COUNTRY] has been dominated by a single party, the [RULING PARTY], for decades. Media freedom remains under threat. Certain ethnic minority communities, such as the [ETHNIC GROUP], as well as migrants, refugees, and LGBT+ people, face discrimination.

## Key Developments

The [RULING PARTY] secured a parliamentary majority in a major election, and the president was sworn in for a full term. While regional observers called the contest free and fair, the [OPPOSITION PARTY] claimed that it was rigged.
In recent years, a judicial panel ruled that a criminal statute banning same-sex relations was unconstitutional, though the government publicly vowed to appeal the decision. 
The former head of the intelligence service was arrested on suspicion of tax evasion. A trial against the former security chief was still pending at the end of the period.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

Freedom of expression is constitutionally guaranteed. However, journalists have endured harassment and intimidation under the president's government, and concerning incidents have also been reported under the current administration.
State-run media outlets dominate the broadcasting sector, and have exhibited a pro-government bias in their programming. However, election observers reported that state-run media provided unbiased coverage of the polls that year. A government ban on advertising in private media remains in place, and harms the competitiveness and economic viability of many outlets. Legislation passed that year established a statutory media regulatory body and mandated the registration of all media workers and outlets—including websites and blogs—with violations being punishable by a fine or prison time. A lawmaker from the [OPPOSITION PARTY] called for the legislation's repeal with a parliamentary motion, but it was defeated.
Journalists have faced harassment and scrutiny from the [INTELLIGENCE SERVICE], as well. A group of detectives and [INTELLIGENCE SERVICE] personnel, who were investigating a former [SECURITY CHIEF] on allegations that he unlawfully revealed the identity of intelligence agents earlier that year, raided the home of a political reporter for a news site, in the summer. The agents ordered her to surrender her mobile phone, admonished her for informing her editor of the raid, and threatened colleagues who visited her home with arrest.
Religious freedom is generally respected, though all religious organizations must register with the government.
Although academic freedom is generally respected, professors often practice self-censorship when addressing sensitive topics. In the past, foreign academics have been deported for publishing work that was critical of the government, contributing to cautiousness among many scholars.
Freedom of expression is constitutionally protected, but is restricted in practice, prompting self-censorship among members of the public. Insulting the president, a lawmaker, or public official is punishable by a fine. Legislation restricts the ability of public-sector workers to air their political views. In recent years, the [INTELLIGENCE SERVICE] has developed capacity to monitor private online communications.

