*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a vibrant representative democracy with competitive elections, lively media and civil society sectors, and unfettered public debate. Economic instability, corruption in the government and judiciary, and drug-related violence are among the country’s most serious challenges.

## Key Developments

The president and vice president were elected, defeating the incumbent president and running mate in a major election. The vice president was the driving force that brought the president to power, and had handpicked them to lead the ticket. The former president’s popularity, meanwhile, had been eroded by economic instability and the effects of an austerity program endorsed by a major international financial institution.
The country’s economic crisis continued, with gross domestic product (GDP) forecast to contract by approximately 3 percent, and inflation reaching more than 50 percent. The poverty rate rose from 25 percent to around 35 percent over a period of several years.
Later that year, the parliament approved a massive legislative package granting the president emergency powers over vast swaths of economic policy for a limited time. Among other powers, the president will be able to impose new taxes, determine wage and pension increases by decree, and renegotiate foreign debt.
During the electoral campaign and after taking office, the president publicly criticized trials against officials affiliated with the [RULING PARTY], deeming them politically motivated. Some detainees were released by judges immediately after the [RULING PARTY] administration returned to power, suggesting a high degree of politicization in the judiciary.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

The government respects citizens’ constitutional right to free travel both inside and outside of [COUNTRY]. People are free to change their place of education or employment.
Citizens generally enjoy the right to own property and establish private businesses, and the [RULING PARTY] administration has made some effort to reduce bureaucracy as a means of encouraging entrepreneurship. However, bureaucratic abuses and corruption continue to affect private businesses.
Approximately 70 percent of the [COUNTRY]’s rural [ETHNIC GROUP] communities lack titles to their lands, and forced evictions, while technically illegal, still occur. [ETHNIC GROUP] communities continue to struggle to defend their land rights against oil and gas prospectors, and to reclaim traditional lands.
Citizens enjoy broad freedom regarding marriage and divorce. Same-sex marriage and adoption by same-sex couples has been legal nationwide for several years. Legislation passed that year allows people to legally change their gender.
Violence against women remains a serious problem. Activists from [NGO] continue to hold highly visible protests and events aimed at drawing attention to the issue. According to official data, less than 5 percent of murder cases against women in recent years ended in convictions.
Access to abortion is legal only in cases where the mother’s life or health are in danger, or if the pregnancy is the result of rape; women in more remote parts of [COUNTRY] report difficulty in accessing an abortion even when these conditions are met. The president pledged to decriminalize abortion that year.
Some industries like garment and brick production profit from the forced labor of men, women, and children from [COUNTRY] as well as from neighboring countries; forced labor is also present in the agriculture sector and among domestic workers and street vendors. Exploitation is made easier by the prevalence of informal work: more than a third of the population works in the informal sector, without proper benefits or formal legal protections.
Men, women, and children are subject to sex trafficking. The government maintained the use of a hotline to facilitate investigations and has worked to identify more victims, deliver anti-trafficking trainings, and prosecute officials involved in trafficking, according to a report from [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP].

