a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedoms of expression and of the media, but the law limits these freedoms in the “interest of defense, public security or professional confidentiality, to the extent that the restriction is fair, reasonable, necessary and justifiable in a democratic society based on openness, justice, human dignity, equality and freedom.” The government continued to arrest, detain, and harass critics, and journalists practiced self-censorship.
Freedom of Expression : There were no official restrictions on individuals criticizing the government or discussing matters of general public interest. Authorities, however, remained sensitive to criticism in general, particularly when directed at the president. Persons accused of insulting the president and his office are charged under a law that undermines authority of or insulting the president, but this was contested in the supreme court on the basis that the section infringed on the right to freedom of expression. The court did not make a final determination on its constitutionality, however, and the law remained in force. On a certain date, [NGO] reported assisting [NUMBER] individuals charged under the law since the beginning of the year. Additionally, [NUMBER] activists or government critics were charged with violating other sections of the same law for attempting to subvert a constitutionally elected government or criminal nuisance.
In a certain month, a former finance minister and senior official of [OPPOSITION PARTY], the largest opposition party, was convicted and fined for unlawfully announcing that [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader had won a recent presidential election over [RULING PARTY] candidate. According to the decision, the announcement was “false and unlawful.”
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent newspapers and commercial radio stations were active and expressed a wide variety of views, although with some restrictions. State-sponsored media, however, were the most prevalent. The ministry of media exercised control over state-run media.
Independent newspapers continued to operate freely, although journalists reported practicing self-censorship.
On a certain date, police intentionally shot teargas canisters into the offices of a local media organization in the capital and then barricaded the doors, preventing staff from exiting. A reporter with the media outlet had been filming the police arresting vendors. Police officers chased the reporter to the media outlet’s offices and then fired a teargas canister directly at the reporter, striking them in the abdomen. The staff fled the offices through second floor windows. Police also confiscated a mobile phone used to record the attack.
On another date, police arrested a documentary filmmaker and charged them with “possession of offensive weapons at public gatherings.” Security officers claimed they found an empty tear gas canister in the journalist’s car when they arrived for a meeting between the president and civil society organizations in a major city.
The government used accreditation laws to monitor international media journalists’ entry into the country. The government required foreign journalists to obtain permits a certain number of days before traveling to the country in order to report from the country.
Foreign reporters paid more for permits and accreditation than did their local counterparts. The media commission charged a certain amount for a foreigner’s accreditation, while local journalists paid a smaller amount for a one-year accreditation.
On a certain date, state media criticized foreign media after the president stated they were “appalled” by a broadcast of security officials attacking a protester. A state-run newspaper reported authorities were worried about “surreptitious reporting” by a certain television station and alleged a foreign embassy was working with local media to besmirch the country and accused the television station of “manufacturing” stories about police brutality.
International media outlets continued to operate in the country.
Radio remained the principal medium of public communication, particularly for the rural majority. All urban commercial radio stations licensed in recent years were operating. Despite their perceived allegiance to [RULING PARTY], these stations included independent voices in their programming. The government did not license any community radio stations during the year.
The government-controlled broadcasting corporation, the country’s only domestically based television broadcasting station, operated one channel. International satellite television broadcasts were available through private firms but were too expensive for most citizens.
On a certain date, a high court judge ruled the state-controlled broadcasting corporation and a state-run newspaper group had, during a recent election campaign, “conducted themselves in material breach of section 61 of the constitution,” which governs freedom of expression and freedom of the media. The judge ordered the two organizations to produce impartial and independent broadcasts and ensure communications did not favor any political party or candidate over another.
Violence and Harassment : Security forces, officials, and supporters from the majority political party routinely harassed journalists.
On a certain date, police arrested and detained an editor with a local newspaper for reporting that members of the security forces assaulted delivery drivers as they left the capital to distribute the newspaper. The incident occurred after the government crackdown on the public, following protests in the capital and a major city.
On another date, police assaulted a local journalist for filming police officers dispersing protesters in the capital. Video footage showed officers in full antiriot gear charging toward the journalist and demanding that they stop filming. The journalist produced their accreditation card, but the officers took turns assaulting them with batons.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : The government maintained censorship through media registration and accreditation laws, although many provisions of the law are inconsistent with the constitution. The law provides the government with extensive powers to control media and suppress free speech by requiring the registration of journalists and prohibiting the “abuse of free expression.” Government-controlled media practiced self-censorship and bias in favor of [RULING PARTY].
In a certain month, an independent media research organization published a report alleging the country’s military takeover of the state-run broadcaster still had a chilling effect on its operations.
Libel /Slander Laws : The supreme court ruled the constitution prohibits criminal defamation. Civil defamation laws remained in force.
Newspapers exercised self-censorship due to government intimidation and the prospect of prosecution under civil libel laws.
On a certain date, police detained the leader of a small political party for undermining the authority of or insulting the president. On another date, the high court freed them on bail and ordered them to report regularly at a police station.
National Security : The law grants the government a wide range of legal powers to prosecute persons for political and security crimes that are not clearly defined. For example, a certain act criminalizes the divulging of any information acquired by government employees in the course of official duties. Authorities used these laws to restrict publication of information critical of government policies or public officials.

Internet Freedom

The law permits the government to monitor all communications in the country, including internet transmissions. Internet and mobile phone communication in the country was widely available. The government, however, regulated internet and mobile phone communication to curb dissent and increased its share of the information and communications technology market and international gateways.
In a certain month, the government used a certain act to suspend internet access for several days. On a certain date, a high court ruling declared the directive illegal. The government allowed internet service providers to increase fees, which limited internet access.
The government regularly monitored and interfered with use of social media. On a certain date, police arrested a human rights activist for tweeting a message calling for action against the government. The activist was charged with subverting a constitutional government. On another date, a high court justice freed the activist on bail with reporting conditions. The case remained pending.
The communications laws facilitated eavesdropping and call interception by state security personnel. The law allows law enforcement officers to apply to the responsible minister for a warrant authorizing them to intercept communications, including calls, emails, and other messages.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The government did not restrict academic freedom; however, the law restricts the independence of universities, subjecting them to government influence and providing disciplinary powers over staff and students to university authorities. The country’s president is the chancellor of all state-run universities and appoints their vice chancellors. The government has oversight of higher education policy at public universities through the ministry of higher education.
On a certain date, a university suspended a lecturer for criticizing the president, according to [NGO]. The lecturer was accused of “misconduct for allegedly denigrating the president by stating they had mismanaged the country’s economy, resulting in the payment of poor salaries to government workers.”
The censorship board approves scripts by playwrights. Artists who violated provisions of the censorship act received fines and prison sentences.
On a certain date, police raided the offices of a theater company; on another date, they arrested several artists after they showed a film without approval, allegedly in contravention of the censorship act. A magistrate freed each artist on bail and postponed the case repeatedly. The next hearing was set for a later date. A government official told media the film had “security implications for a foreign power.”

