Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The law provides citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : The parliamentary election held that year was generally considered free and fair, with seven new members elected to the [POPULATION FIGURE]-member parliament. Following the election, the parliament selected the prime minister.
Political Parties and Political Participation : There were no formal political parties. The parliament tended to divide itself between an ad hoc faction with at least the minimum votes required to form a government and an informal opposition faction.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No law limits participation of women in the political process, and they did participate. Nonetheless, participation by women in government and politics was limited. The [POPULATION FIGURE]-member parliament included one woman, who was also a cabinet minister. Women held a subordinate societal position, largely due to traditional perceptions of women’s role in society. There were no members of [ETHNIC GROUP] or other minority groups in parliament or the cabinet.

