## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a parliamentary democracy that holds regular, competitive elections. Civil liberties are generally upheld. Ongoing problems include a lack of antidiscrimination laws to protect women and LGBT+ people.

## Key Developments

The prime minister was elected in recent years. His victory ended the tenure of the previous prime minister, who had been in office for several years.
In recent years, the parliament livestreamed government proceedings on social media for the first time, with hopes of improving the government’s ability to inform its population, and the world, about policy changes.
Earlier, the supreme court determined that landowners on the main island, who had leased their land to the government for the island’s airport, had violated their lease agreement; the court ordered the security forces to arrest anyone interfering with flights. The landowners had blockaded the runway after they claimed the lease for the land had expired, sparking a dispute that had been ongoing for several years.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

There are no formal political parties, though no law bars their formation. Candidates typically run as independents and form loose, frequently shifting alliances once in office.
[COUNTRY] has an established pattern of democratic transfers of power. Individual prime ministers and governments have seldom lasted a full term in office for many years, with intense political rivalries often prompting no-confidence votes in the parliament. In recent years, the prime minister faced a motion of no confidence, although it was withdrawn a week later.
Traditional elders and the main [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] play an influential role in society, but they do not exercise undue control over the political choices of voters and candidates.
All citizens of [COUNTRY] aged 18 and over who are present in the country on polling day but not imprisoned are eligible to vote. While women formally have full political rights, in practice their participation is somewhat inhibited by discriminatory and widespread biases. Two women ran in the parliamentary elections that year, and one of them (an incumbent) won a seat. A move to add two reserved seats for women was considered as part of a constitutional review process, but no action appears to have been taken by the end of that period.

