*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Arbitrary Deprivation of Life and Other Unlawful or Politically Motivated Killings

There were several reports that the government or its agents committed arbitrary or unlawful killings. In general, the killings took place during law enforcement operations or were linked to other criminal activity by government agents. Civilian authorities investigated and arrested members of the security forces accused of human rights abuses. Impunity, however, remained a serious problem, with significant delays in some prosecutions and sources alleging corruption in judicial proceedings.
The relevant government body reported 307 arbitrary or unlawful killings by security forces, of which 34 cases were dismissed, 103 resulted in convictions, 87 resulted in fines, and 83 were still under investigation. The security forces' internal affairs division conducted internal investigations of security forces members in a continuation of the police purge begun several years ago.
On a specific date, a sub inspector assigned to a police checkpoint in a major city was arrested in connection with the killing of an individual who was allegedly murdered for working with authorities against the individuals who had killed his brother.
Authorities continued to investigate the deaths of [POPULATION FIGURE] protesters that occurred during civil unrest following a disputed election. On a specific date, the relevant government body's investigative agency executed an arrest warrant against a member of the security forces for his alleged involvement in the death of a postelection protester in a major city. The arrest was the second arrest of a member of the security forces allegedly involved in killings of election protesters.
The government continued to pursue legal actions against individuals allegedly involved in the killing of an environmental and indigenous activist. On a specific date, a court handed down sentences ranging from 30 to 50 years' imprisonment for several defendants, who had been convicted of planning and carrying out the killing of the activist and attempted killing of another activist. The legal process against one of the alleged intellectual authors of the killing was proceeding slowly, and the individual remained incarcerated. The trial resumed on a specific date. On another date, an appeals court annulled the criminal proceedings against two former security forces members, who had been accused of falsifying evidence in favor of the defendants accused of killing the activist. The relevant government body appealed that ruling on a subsequent date.
There continued to be reports of violence related to land conflicts and criminal activity. Near a mining project in a rural area, unknown gunmen killed two community leaders who supported the mine operated by a company. 
Organized-criminal elements, including drug traffickers and local and transnational gangs, committed killings, extortion, kidnappings, human trafficking, and intimidation of police, prosecutors, journalists, women, and human rights defenders. Major urban centers and drug trafficking routes experienced disproportionate rates of violence. A research institution reported that as of a certain point, [POPULATION FIGURE] individuals working in the transportation sector had been killed by criminal gangs.

---

b. Disappearance

There were no reports of disappearances by or on behalf of government authorities.
The government continued to make significant advances in combatting kidnappings by criminals. The security forces reported 11 kidnappings through October, a 40 percent decrease from the same period the previous year. The security forces secured the release of all 11 kidnapping victims, and investigations were underway in each case.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

Institutional weakness, corruption, violence, and impunity undermine the overall stability of [COUNTRY]. Journalists, political activists, and women are often the victims of violence, and perpetrators are rarely brought to justice. While [COUNTRY] holds regular elections, irregularities surrounding a recent presidential poll prompted election monitors to call the result into question.

## Key Developments

Antigovernment protests were held nationwide for much of the year, prompted by the president's announcement of unpopular health and education reforms. Protests continued despite their suspension, and authorities responded with excessive force; at least six people were killed in clashes by the end of the year.
Later that year, foreign prosecutors identified the president and his predecessor as coconspirators in a drug-smuggling operation led by the president's brother. The president's brother was convicted by a foreign jury, and his sentence was pending.
Towards the end of the year, legislators recommended against the renewal of a mission to support the fight against corruption and impunity in [COUNTRY], which is backed by an international organization. At the time, the government was negotiating with the international organization over the mission's mandate, which was to expire soon.

---

## F Rule of Law

Political and business elites exert excessive influence over the judiciary, including the supreme court. Judicial appointments are made with little transparency, with [NGO] reporting that the supreme court exerts excessive control over appointments. Judges have been removed from their posts for political reasons, and a number of legal professionals have been killed in recent years. Prosecutors and whistleblowers handling corruption cases are often subject to threats of violence.
In recent years, the parliament voted to remove several justices from the supreme court’s constitutional chamber after they ruled a security law unconstitutional. The legislature also granted itself the power to remove from office the president, supreme court justices, legislators, and other officials. It also curtailed the power of the supreme court’s constitutional chamber and revoked the right of citizens to challenge the constitutionality of laws. These moves laid the groundwork for a controversial constitutional change that allowed for the president’s reelection for several years.
Due process is limited due to a compromised judiciary and a corrupt and often inept police force, in which many officers have engaged in criminal activities including drug trafficking and extortion. The government has increasingly utilized the armed forces to combat crime and violence. Arbitrary arrests and detentions are common, as is lengthy pretrial detention. Authorities established several new courts in an attempt to address lengthy trial delays. Authorities in the armed forces have dishonorably discharged members accused of rights violations before their trials have taken place.
A court found several suspects in the killing of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] activist guilty of murder. Among those convicted were officials with a company and former members of the military. The defendants were handed sentences ranging from 30 to 50 years, over a year after their conviction.
The number of homicides rose to [POPULATION FIGURE] in recent years. Violent crime and gang violence remain serious problems, and have prompted large-scale migration out of [COUNTRY]. Many parents opt to send their children towards another country to avoid gang recruitment, and those who return to their neighborhoods are often targeted by gangs, and in some cases, killed for fleeing the community. In response to widespread violence, the government has empowered the security forces to combat security threats, and these units often employ excessive force when conducting operations.
Prisons are overcrowded and underequipped, and prison violence remains rampant due in large part to the presence of [ARMED GROUP]. Inmates in a high-security prison killed a drug smuggler who previously implicated a high-ranking official in his own confession. The prison’s warden, who was suspended after the death, was himself shot and killed. That period was also marked by two prison clashes that killed at least [POPULATION FIGURE] inmates; prison gangs were involved in both incidents.
Violence and discrimination against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] people and [ETHNIC GROUP] populations persist. A local [NGO] reported that at least [POPULATION FIGURE] people were killed in recent years, outpacing the previous year’s tally. While antidiscrimination laws are on the books, victims of such abuses have little practical recourse.
[COUNTRY] has among the highest rates of violence against women in the world, and these murders are rarely investigated. According to [NGO], [POPULATION FIGURE] women were murdered in recent years.
Asylum seekers from [COUNTRY] and other countries can find themselves held in [COUNTRY] due to an agreement signed with another country. As part of the agreement, [COUNTRY] agreed to house asylum seekers whose claims were either rejected by or unprocessed by the other country, even if they did not originate in [COUNTRY].

