*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY]’s democratic institutions took root at the start of its transition from communist rule for many years ago. Rapid economic growth and other societal changes have benefited some segments of the population more than others, contributing to a deep divide between liberal, pro-European parties and those purporting to defend national interests and “traditional” values. Since taking power in recent years, the populist, socially conservative [RULING PARTY] has enacted numerous measures that increase political influence over state institutions and threaten to reverse [COUNTRY]’s democratic progress.

## Key Developments

The ruling [RULING PARTY] won elections to the lower house of the parliament, taking a significant number of seats. [RULING PARTY] narrowly lost control of the upper house, which can delay and amend legislation but has few other powers.
Election observers from [NGO] raised concerns that a number of factors may have negatively impacted the fairness of the parliamentary elections, including the ruling party’s use of public media to influence voters.
Amid further infringement proceedings against [COUNTRY] by the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and a series of rulings by the supreme court against [RULING PARTY]’s judicial reforms, the supreme court issued a decision effectively invalidating parts of the reforms. In response, [RULING PARTY] pushed through legislation that would discipline judges who question the reforms.
The period saw a record number of marches advocating for equality for a certain minority group. However, they took place amid an intense campaign led by the ruling party and the church, and some events saw attempts at obstruction. A march in a major city was attacked by protesters, and the security forces made dozens of related arrests.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

People in [COUNTRY] typically enjoy freedom of travel and choice of residence, employment, and institution of higher education.
Citizens have the right to own property and establish private businesses. However, legislation passed that year imposed onerous restrictions on sale and ownership of agricultural land, ostensibly to protect small-scale farmers. State and religious institutions are not bound by the new restrictions.
Under the law, abortion is permissible through the 12th week of pregnancy if a woman’s health or life is in danger, if the pregnancy is a result of a criminal act such as rape, or if the fetus is severely damaged. Bills that sought to further restrict abortions, including the imposition of prison terms for illegal abortions, triggered a major protest and another large protest a couple of years later, prompting the parliament to back down. The president and senior [RULING PARTY] figures have made clear that they still intend to ban what they call “eugenic abortion,” in practice meaning cases of a congenital disorder of the fetus. A group of over 100 lawmakers, most from the [RULING PARTY], have asked the supreme court to rule on whether such abortions violate the constitution’s protection of human life and dignity.
In recent years, contraceptive pills have been available by prescription only, making [COUNTRY] one of only a few countries in which such a restriction is in place. A report by [NGO] found that in many, especially rural, parts of [COUNTRY], gynecologists are rare; many women must travel to obtain care, and reliable and timely access to contraception and other sexual-health services are thus more difficult to obtain.
Same-sex civil partnerships, marriage, and adoption are not permitted, as the constitution describes marriage as being between a man and a woman.
The law provides meaningful protections against abusive working conditions and child labor, especially in the formal sector. The authorities work to combat human trafficking, but women and children are still subjected to trafficking for sexual exploitation and foreign migrant workers are vulnerable to conditions amounting to forced labor.
State-owned entities have been expanding their already considerable presence in various sectors, such as banking, often by buying out foreign owners, as the government effectively renationalizes parts of the economy. Hiring for senior positions at such firms is often based on political loyalty or connections rather than merit, a longstanding issue under various governments that has grown more widespread under the current administration.

## On [COUNTRY]

See all data, scores & information on this country or territory.

---

## Functioning of Government

Freely elected officials generally determine and implement laws and policies without interference, though the chairman of the [RULING PARTY] continues to play a dominant role in the government despite not holding any official executive position. The [RULING PARTY] has also, throughout the party’s time in power, sought to limit parliamentary scrutiny of legislation through various means, such as making use of private-members bills that require no consultation or impact assessments; introducing legislation unexpectedly at the last minute, sometimes in the middle of the night; and limiting opportunities for the [OPPOSITION PARTY] to question or amend legislation.
Cronyism, a problem under all previous governments, appears widespread under the [RULING PARTY]. The government has altered, lowered, or simply removed many criteria for staffing of public institutions, allowing for appointments based on party loyalty and personal connections.
In recent years, a state watchdog has raised concerns about the misuse of public funds by the [RULING PARTY] government, occasionally prompting the party to take action such as donating scrutinized funds to charity. Upon the expiration of the previous head’s term, the [RULING PARTY] parliamentary majority appointed the finance minister as its new head. The finance minister immediately came under scrutiny for alleged irregularities in his property declarations and links to a [MILITANT GROUP], and took unpaid leave as a consequence.
Earlier that year, the chairman of the [RULING PARTY] was accused by a foreign businessman of defrauding him out of several million units of currency and of soliciting a bribe to be paid to a member of the clergy on the board of a foundation. Secret recordings of apparent discussions between the pair, published by a major newspaper, lent credence to the claim. The chairman denies any wrongdoing, and prosecutors have been reluctant to investigate the allegations.
The right to public information is guaranteed by the constitution and by legislation, but obtaining records and data from public institutions can be slow and difficult. The office of the parliament has refused to release lists of judges who supported controversial new appointees to the judiciary, despite a ruling from the supreme court ordering it to do so. The office cites personal data protection concerns to justify this failure.
The current government avoids consulting outside experts or [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUPS] on policy ideas and tends to introduce and pass legislation rapidly, with little opportunity for debate or amendment.

