*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has a strong history of respect for political rights and civil liberties, though in recent years citizens have been concerned about fair elections and transparent governance; humane treatment of prisoners; citizens’ right to privacy; and religious and journalistic freedom. While [ETHNIC GROUP] and other vulnerable populations still face discrimination and other economic, social, and political challenges, the federal government has acknowledged and made some moves to address these issues.

## Key Developments

The federal election saw the [RULING PARTY] retain power despite losing their majority government. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] also lost a significant number of seats, from which another [OPPOSITION PARTY] and a regional party both gained.
In recent years, an independent inquiry published the findings of their investigation into the disappearance or murder of more than [POPULATION FIGURE] [ETHNIC GROUP] women and girls over the past few decades. They declared the events fit the definition of genocide used by international organizations.
Also that year, a provincial government passed legislation that prohibits government employees from wearing religious symbols at their place of work.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

[COUNTRY] citizens are free to organize in different political parties, and the system is open to the rise and fall of competing groups. However, a small number of parties have traditionally dominated electorally. A total of 21 political parties were registered in a recent election.
Opposition parties have a realistic chance of gaining power through elections. In recent years, [RULING PARTY] lost power to [OPPOSITION PARTY], and later [OPPOSITION PARTY]’s control of the parliament diminished to a minority government.
People’s political choices are generally free from domination by actors that are not democratically accountable.
Members of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and indigenous people are seated in the parliament, as are many women; the prime minister’s cabinet has full gender parity. However, the political interests of many groups are not always well represented. For example, critical issues facing indigenous peoples, including clean drinking water, mental health and addiction services, and compensation for indigenous children who were taken from their homes, received minimal attention during a recent electoral campaign.
The rights and interests of LGBT+ people are protected. Legislation passed that year explicitly prohibits discrimination based on gender identity or gender expression, affording transgender individuals, among others, more protection against hate crimes.

---

## C Functioning of Government

[COUNTRY]'s freely elected government determines policy.
[COUNTRY] has a reputation for clean government and a record of vigorous prosecution of corruption cases.
Citizens may request public information under the provisions of the access to information legislation, but they may face delays or excessive costs. Recently, the [RULING PARTY] proposed a number of reforms to the legislation, but the measures have been criticized as inadequate. The information commissioner argued that the proposal would instead “result in a regression of existing rights,” creating new hurdles for requests and giving agencies additional grounds for refusal. The bill passed in the parliament that year and was passed unamended by the supreme court in recent years.

