*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has been ruled by the same [RULING PARTY] for decades, and authorities have systematically repressed political dissent. Corruption, due process violations, and abuses by the security forces all remain common. Since the president’s election, the government has taken steps to crack down on endemic corruption and eased restrictions on the press and civil society, but serious governance and human rights challenges persist.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the parliament adopted a new criminal code that no longer penalized same-sex relations, along with new antidiscrimination statutes.
Later, [COUNTRY] finalized an agreement with the Office of the UN High Commissioner for Refugees and a neighboring country allowing for the voluntary departure of migrants, a while after the government launched an operation that forcibly expelled over 520,000 people. 
Family members and officials who served under the former president faced corruption trials and investigations. A former minister received an 8-year sentence for fraud after being convicted, while the president’s relative was tried for embezzling public funds.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The country has been ruled by the [RULING PARTY] for decades, and the president is expected to consult routinely with the party’s political bureau. The former president retained his position as head of the [RULING PARTY] for a year after the president’s election. Recently, the former president was finally replaced by the president as party leader, enabling the new president to consolidate his authority.
Executive powers are broad and varied, leaving the parliament to act largely as a rubber stamp in approving the president’s policies. Like his predecessor, the president frequently adopts legislation by presidential decree.
After many years of [RULING PARTY] rule, corruption and patronage have become entrenched in nearly all segments of public and private life. The president stressed his willingness to fight endemic corruption during his election campaign, and high-profile former officials have been convicted of corruption in recent years. Recently, a former transport minister was handed a 14-year sentence for fraud, though that sentence was later reduced to 8 years. Later that year, a former head of the intelligence service was handed a three-year prison sentence for stealing confidential documents. However, a former vice president did not face trial that year. The former vice president, who is considered an ally of the president, was investigated for corruption by foreign authorities before they agreed to transfer his case to [COUNTRY].
The former president’s family also faced scrutiny recently. The former president’s son was tried for embezzling a large amount of public money over several years. The son, who was arrested a few years ago, pleaded not guilty; his trial was ongoing at the end of the year. A court in [COUNTRY] froze the assets of one of the former president’s daughters in recent years, after the government accused her of siphoning public funds when she was the head of a state-owned company.
Government operations are generally opaque, though the current administration has moved to improve transparency in the corrupt oil sector. Recently, the government announced the formation of a new agency to oversee the industry. However, its leadership has been sourced from a state-owned company; the agency head previously served as the company’s chief executive for several years.

