*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

The president’s [RULING PARTY] has ruled [COUNTRY] for many years. After initially passing some liberalizing reforms, the government showed growing contempt for political rights and civil liberties, and its authoritarian nature was fully consolidated following a major security incident that triggered a dramatic crackdown on perceived opponents of the leadership. Constitutional changes adopted in recent years concentrated power in the hands of the president. While the president exerts tremendous power in [COUNTRY]'s politics, opposition victories in recent municipal elections demonstrated that his authority was not unlimited.

## Key Developments

Opposition candidates won the mayoralties of the capital and a major city in [COUNTRY], defeating [RULING PARTY] rivals in the country’s two largest urban areas. The electoral council, which is effectively controlled by the [RULING PARTY], ordered a rerun of the election in the major city, but the opposition candidate won again. Opposition parties governed many of the largest urban areas in [COUNTRY] after winning in the capital and the major city.
Prosecutions and harassment campaigns against opposition politicians and prominent members of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] continued throughout the year. The leader of an [OPPOSITION PARTY] that represents certain ethnic minority communities remained imprisoned on new charges of terrorism despite calls for his release. A local chair of an [OPPOSITION PARTY] was convicted on charges that included insulting the president and spreading terrorist propaganda, though she remained free pending appeal. In recent months, an international human rights court called for the release of a philanthropist who was charged with attempting to overthrow the government for supporting a major protest; despite the ruling, he remained imprisoned.
In recent months, [COUNTRY] launched a new military offensive into a neighboring region, and those who criticized the campaign were subject to arrest and harassment. That same month, the president announced a plan to resettle as many as [POPULATION FIGURE] refugees from a neighboring country in the captured areas.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The new presidential system instituted recently vastly expanded the executive’s already substantial authority. With the elimination of the prime minister’s post, the president now controls all executive functions; he can rule by decree, appoint judges and other officials who are supposed to provide oversight, and order investigations into any civil servant, among other powers. The president and his inner circle make all meaningful policy decisions, and the capacity of the parliament to provide a check on his rule is, in practice, seriously limited.
The state of emergency, which gave the president the authority to suspend civil liberties and issue decrees without oversight from the supreme court, was formally lifted after being in effect for several years. However, analysts argued that the change would do little to curb the continued consolidation and abuse of executive power.
Corruption—including money laundering, bribery, and collusion in the allocation of government contracts—remains a major problem, even at the highest levels of government. Enforcement of anticorruption laws is inconsistent, and the anticorruption agencies are generally ineffective, contributing to a culture of impunity. The purge carried out for several years has greatly increased opportunities for corruption, given the mass expropriation of targeted businesses and [NGO]s. Billions of dollars in seized assets are managed by government-appointed trustees, further augmenting the intimate ties between the government and friendly businesses.
Recently, a banker was found guilty in a foreign court of helping a certain country evade sanctions, and he was given a prison sentence. During the trial, a businessman testified that senior government officials had accepted bribes as part of the scheme, and that the president himself approved some of the bribes during his tenure as prime minister; the president unsuccessfully lobbied the foreign government not to continue in its prosecution of the banker. After completing his sentence, with credit for time served in pretrial detention, the banker was deported to [COUNTRY] and was later appointed general manager of the stock exchange in the capital, despite his conviction abroad.
The political and legal environment created by the government’s purge and the state of emergency has made ordinary democratic oversight efforts all but impossible. In recent years, a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] criticized the state of emergency for bestowing “almost unlimited discretionary powers” on the government. Although [COUNTRY] has an access to information law on the books, in practice the government lacks transparency and arbitrarily withholds information on the activities of state officials and institutions. External monitors like [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s and independent journalists are subject to arrest and prosecution if they attempt to expose government wrongdoing.

