*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

The political system of [COUNTRY] is characterized by decentralization and direct democracy. The multilingual state is typically governed by a broad coalition that includes members from the four largest political parties represented in the parliament. The [POPULATION FIGURE] cantons that make up [COUNTRY] have considerable decision-making power, and the public is often asked to weigh in on policy matters through referendums. Civil liberties are generally respected in [COUNTRY], though laws and policies adopted in recent years have reflected a growing wariness of immigration and minority groups of foreign origin, which sometimes face societal discrimination.

## Key Developments

Parliamentary elections presented a minor shake-up of [COUNTRY]'s politics: The right-wing populist [RULING PARTY] maintained their plurality but lost a sizeable portion of their power. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] and another [OPPOSITION PARTY] recorded the strongest gains, becoming competitive opposition parties.
[COUNTRY] was added to the blacklist of the [NGO] of countries not offering enough protection to unionized employees. 
Also, the [intelligence service] was called out for leading investigations against several left-wing political activists and members of left-wing parties in a major city and the capital, despite not having legal grounds to do so.
In that year, [COUNTRY] was removed from a major economic bloc's “gray list” of countries not cooperating in the fight against tax evasion.

---

## C Functioning of Government

[COUNTRY]'s freely elected officials are able to determine and effectively implement national and local policy through a decentralized system of government.
The [POPULATION FIGURE] cantons have significant control over economic and social policy, with the federal government's powers largely limited to foreign affairs and some economic matters. Referendums, which are used extensively, are mandatory for any amendments to the federal constitution, the joining of international organizations, and major changes to federal laws.
Safeguards against corruption are generally effective. The trial against the former head of the cantonal government of a major city, who had accepted benefits from a foreign royal family, continued for several years even though the official still holds a seat as a state counsellor. Four employees of the state secretariat for economic affairs were indicted for procurement fraud. 
A law to improve whistleblower protection was rejected by the parliament but is currently under review. The reform came as a response to criticism by [NGO], which called out [COUNTRY] for failing to fully implement recommendations.
In recent years, [COUNTRY] was removed from a list of countries with questionable tax policies and support for tax avoidance, after [COUNTRY] had abolished tax policies that were not in line with international standards.
The government is generally transparent in its operations. In recent years, an increasing number of cantonal governments have passed transparency laws that make government data more accessible to citizens. Federal politicians were still debating an increase in lobbying transparency. A transparency referendum at the federal level was submitted and is likely to be voted on by the people. The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] announced it would halt its noncompliance procedure against [COUNTRY]: the upcoming transparency referendum and an anticorruption initiative put forward by the federal government address concerns.

