*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has enjoyed more than a decade of peace and stability since the second civil war ended. During this time, the country has made considerable progress rebuilding government capacity, reestablishing the rule of law, and ensuring the political rights and civil liberties of citizens, and that year saw the first peaceful transfer of power between leaders in many years. However, [COUNTRY] still faces serious issues with corruption, violence against women, and discrimination against LGBT+ people.

## Key Developments

The government partially restricted access to social networks and messaging services in recent years, ahead of an [OPPOSITION PARTY]-led rally calling for economic reform and an end to pervasive corruption. 
In recent months, the supreme court associate justice was removed by the parliament after he issued a writ in favor of petroleum dealers fighting a government levy on gasoline sales. The justice criticized the trial, saying it was conducted unconstitutionally, and appealed to a regional court; the case was under consideration at the end of the period.
A lower-house by-election was partially rerun after an [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidate alleged irregularities on the part of electoral officers; the candidate and her supporters were physically attacked twice during the election campaign, which ended with a victory for the [RULING PARTY] candidate.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

While some unofficial border checkpoints remain, at which border patrol agents sometimes attempt to extract bribes, people have enjoyed a gradual increase in the right to move about freely in recent years, since large-scale violence ended.
Conflicts over land remain pervasive. Many of these conflicts originated in the civil wars and subsequent displacement and resettlement. Others are the result of opaque concession agreements granting foreign corporations access to lands for mining, the production of timber, and the production of palm oil.
The parliament passed legislation that formalized community ownership of ancestral land, which has led to the signing of memorandums of understanding with numerous communities to control a significant amount of land under the new law. The relevant authority launched a public awareness program to educate residents on their rights under the legislation.
Customary law practices that prevail in large parts of the country disadvantage women in matters of land rights and inheritance.
While men and women enjoy equal legal rights under civil law, gender disparities are common in customary law, which remains dominant in many parts of the country and disadvantages women in matters involving child custody and other matters. Violence against women and children, particularly rape, is a pervasive problem. A decision by the parliament to make rape a bailable offense sparked protests by women’s rights activists. Despite this decision, rape remained a nonbailable offense.
The president signed the Domestic Violence Act into law, which mandates stricter punishment for those convicted of domestic violence, though restrictions on female genital mutilation (FGM) were not included.
Human trafficking for the purpose of forced labor and prostitution remains a problem, with most victims trafficked from rural areas to cities. Many trafficking victims are children, who can be found working in mines, agricultural operations, or as domestic laborers, or engaged in forced begging or prostitution.

