*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 4. Corruption and Lack of Transparency in Government

The law provides criminal penalties for corruption by officials, but the government did not implement the law effectively, and officials frequently engaged in corrupt practices with impunity.
Corruption : The penal code defines various corrupt acts and specifies penalties for them. The anticorruption law establishes a national council against corruption and an anticorruption unit to receive and investigate corruption complaints. The unit did not collaborate frequently with [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and was considered ineffective in combating official corruption. Instead, the unit focused on investigations of [OPPOSITION PARTY] figures, leading to a widespread perception the unit served the interests of the [RULING PARTY]. The unit has never investigated a high-level member of the [RULING PARTY], despite widespread allegations of corruption at senior levels of the party and government. For example, according to a report by [NGO], the director general of the country's taxation department violated foreign tax laws and evaded taxes, but authorities neither investigated nor prosecuted them. Civil servants must seek clearance and permission from supervisors before responding to legislative inquiries about corruption allegations.
Corruption was endemic throughout society and government. There were reports that the security forces, prosecutors, investigating judges, and presiding judges took bribes from owners of both legal and illegal businesses. Citizens frequently and publicly complained about corruption. Meager salaries contributed to “survival corruption” among low-level public servants, while a culture of impunity enabled corruption to flourish among senior officials.
A [NGO] report noted the judiciary remained the most corrupt sector of government for several years in a row, followed by law enforcement.
Financial Disclosure : The law requires public servants, including elected and appointed officials, to disclose their financial and other assets. The anticorruption unit is responsible for receiving the disclosures, with penalties for noncompliance ranging from one month to one year in prison. Senior officials’ financial disclosure statements were not publicly available and remained sealed unless allegations of corruption were filed. Only one financial disclosure statement has ever been unsealed, that of a senior official.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s political system has been dominated by the prime minister and the [RULING PARTY] for decades. While the country conducted semicompetitive elections in the past, a major election was held in a severely repressive environment. Since then, the government has maintained pressure on [OPPOSITION PARTY] members, independent press outlets, and demonstrators with intimidation, politically motivated prosecutions, and violence.

## Key Developments

The security forces interfered with public assemblies and demonstrations commemorating the anniversary of a prominent activist's murder. Three participants who visited the site of the death were arrested, and one was subsequently charged with incitement to commit a felony.
The government continued targeting key members of the [OPPOSITION PARTY], which was banned a few years ago. The interior minister's government charged several of its top officials, including the leader, with treason. The government also banned the leader from reentering the country; the leader had fled to a foreign country to avoid politically-charged charges several years prior. Another top member of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] was released from house arrest, but remained under indictment on charges of treason originally announced a few years ago.
Judges sitting in the supreme court, which oversees the trials of surviving members of a genocidal regime, reached an impasse over whether to hear the case of a defendant charged with committing genocide against a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The prime minister has increasingly centralized power, and figures outside of his close circle have little impact on policymaking. Analysts also believe the prime minister is grooming his son to succeed him. His son has gained several key posts during his father’s rule, including the deputy leadership of the prime minister's personal bodyguard and the top post in the defense ministry’s counterterrorism task force. For several years, the son has risen through the ranks, and was made commander of the armed forces. Recently, the prime minister publicly suggested his son could become his successor.

