*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedom of expression, including for members of the press. The government made progress in respecting media freedom and freedom of expression, but problems remained, including weak media independence, and violence toward and intimidation of journalists.
A report on [COUNTRY] noted the “overall situation and political climate for media continued to improve.” The report cited increased government efforts to support media through changes to legislation and financial subsidies for print media. The report also highlighted that professional organizations acknowledged the open dialogue and increased transparency of institutions.
A [NGO] report stated that “while the media and civil society are active, journalists and activists face pressure and intimidation.” The report noted the media landscape was “deeply polarized along political lines, and private media outlets were often tied to political or business interests that influenced their content. Some critical and independent outlets operated and were found mainly online.”
As of recent months, the government had not taken measures to address an open letter from media stakeholders expressing concern that legal changes to the electoral code would permit taxpayer money to be used for political campaigning in commercial media.
Freedom of Expression : The law prohibits speech that incites national, religious, or ethnic hatred and provides penalties for violations. Individuals may criticize the government publicly or privately.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : While outlets and reporting continued to be largely divided along political lines, the number of independent media voices actively expressing a variety of views without overt restriction continued to increase. Laws that restrict speech inciting national, religious, or ethnic hatred also cover print and broadcast media, publication of books, and online newspapers and journals.
A National Network against Hate Speech in Media was launched, led by the Media Ethics Council and supported by a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. The network is comprised of entities, including media and journalist associations, civil society, government, and other relevant stakeholders. An awareness campaign was launched under the motto, “Respect, Do Not Hate.” The government accepted these organizations and did not limit or restrict their activities.
In recent years, the government amended its Law on Audio and Audiovisual Media Services. A report noted implementing the law would require “strong political commitment to guarantee professionalism, respect for the principles of transparency, merit-based appointments and equitable representation.” A representative of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] welcomed the adoption of the amended law, saying it “is now in general accordance with international standards on audiovisual media.”
Government advertising on commercial channels is banned. A report noted concerns that legal changes that permitted public funding of a referendum campaign with commercial ad buys risked politicization of editorial policies.
The report also noted “further self-regulation efforts are required to improve professional standards and the quality of journalism.” The Media Ethics Council reported that a significant percentage of complaints received were for unethical reports or fake news in online portals.
A court in the capital issued a reprimand against a media outlet for violating electoral code ad campaign regulations during a presidential election by continuing with political advertising beyond the legal deadline. On a later date, an appeals court upheld the verdict reprimanding the media outlet for the violation.
Violence and Harassment : There were several cases of alleged threats and harassment against journalists.
The head of a journalists' association told a conference that impunity for cases of attacks on journalists remained a major problem and impeded freedom of expression. According to the association, the Ministry of Interior completed pending investigations of attacks on journalists, but no further action was taken except in one case. A court in the capital sentenced a member of [RULING PARTY] to probation for threats against a journalist. A report noted the government should “continue paying attention to the swift and effective follow-up by law enforcement and judicial authorities of all instances of physical and verbal attacks against journalists.”
As of recent months, no progress was reported regarding an investigation into a complaint by a former head of a journalists' association that he received threats against his and his family’s lives from an individual affiliated with [OPPOSITION PARTY].
Journalists reporting on poor infrastructure in a village said they received threats and verbal attacks from individuals reportedly linked to a local official. The journalists alleged individuals forcibly escorted them to the municipality building after they refused to delete their recorded interviews. A representative of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] condemned the intimidation, calling it a “blatant attack on freedom of the media.” 
On a certain date, a journalists' association and the Media Ethics Council strongly condemned the “explicit hate speech” against [ETHNIC GROUP] during a celebration in the capital. The association and the council expressed concern media failed to condemn the hate speech calling for violence and intolerance. A [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] condemned the inflammatory speech, calling for the perpetrators to be brought to justice.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : There were some reports the government pressured journalists into self-censorship. A report noted, “There was no progress on improving the labor and social rights of journalists whose working conditions are very poor. Consequently, journalists still practice self-censorship. 
Libel /Slander Laws : Persons found guilty of defamation, libel, and slander were subject to fines. A report noted “preliminary steps have been taken to reduce fines for defamation to a symbolic amount which is expected to improve the sense of balance between freedom of expression and protection of reputation.”
A high-ranking official of [RULING PARTY] announced plans to file slander charges against journalists and media for alleging some officials had abused state funds. A journalists' association reacted, urging politicians to refrain from filing slander charges against journalists. A spokesperson for a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] noted “threats of legal consequences for media for their reporting,” by political actors, reiterating a recommendation that [COUNTRY] should demonstrate “zero tolerance for physical and verbal harassment, and threats against journalists.”
A government official announced plans to file slander charges against journalists and media portals for spreading fake news. A journalists' association criticized the official’s “direct threats” against journalists as “pressure that may result in ‘self-censorship’.”

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content. There were no reports the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events.

