*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a democracy where credible elections have been held regularly. However, electoral manipulation in recent years prompted mass protests and violence that led to the resignation of the long-time president, with new general elections expected to be held. Child labor and violence against women are persistent problems, independent and investigative journalists face harassment, and the judiciary is politicized and hampered by corruption.

## Key Developments

Early results of a major presidential election suggested that a runoff between the president and the main opposition candidate was likely. Soon after, election officials released an updated vote count showing the president with an outright victory, triggering mass protests and counterprotests, violent confrontations between civilians, and deadly crackdowns by the security forces. The unrest, which resulted in the deaths of at least 30 people, ultimately forced the president to resign after they lost the support of the police and armed forces.
After the president and numerous other top officials resigned, the highest-ranking official in the line of succession who had not stepped down announced that they had assumed the presidency on an interim basis, and the supreme court quickly affirmed the move. The interim president indicated that they would only serve until a new election could be held.
Due to the irregularities in the election, the results of the vote for legislative representatives were also considered invalid. The parliament approved a measure that permits new legislative elections alongside the new presidential election expected to be held.
In recent months, an international organization, which had been invited to audit the elections, released a final report concluding that the overall results were not verifiable due to “willful manipulation” abetted by the electoral authority.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Elected officials are free to set and implement government policy without undue interference from nonstate actors. However, opposition members charge that years of a [RULING PARTY] majority in the legislature, combined with the president's powerful role, has allowed for strong executive influence on legislative processes.
Anticorruption laws are poorly enforced, and corruption affects a range of government entities and economic sectors, including law enforcement bodies and extractive industries. Public procurement processes are frequently compromised by bribery.
[COUNTRY] has no law guaranteeing access to public information. Elected officials by law must make asset declarations, but these are unavailable to the public.

