*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The transitional constitution provides for freedom of expression, including for the press. The government and its agents frequently violated these rights in the name of national security, however, and the downward trend in respect for these freedoms over the past several years continued.
Freedom of Expression : Civil society organizations must register with the government under legislation requiring [NGO] registration. The government regularly attempted to impede criticism by monitoring, intimidating, harassing, arresting, or detaining members of civil society who publicly criticized the government.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The government maintained strict control of media, both print and electronic. The government suppressed dissenting voices, forcing some civil society organizations and media houses to shut down or flee the country. Government officials or individuals close to the government regularly interfered in the publication of articles and broadcasting of programs, and high-level government officials stated press freedom should not extend to criticism of the government or soliciting views of opposition leaders.
Most organizations practiced self-censorship to ensure their safety, and authorities regularly censored newspapers, directly reprimanded publishers, and removed articles deemed critical of the government. Many print media outlets reported security forces forcing the removal of articles at the printing company (where all newspapers are printed), often leaving a blank spot where the article was originally meant to appear. For example, the security forces removed an article about a new government appointment from a major newspaper. On another occasion, the security forces removed an opinion article in a daily newspaper written by a former government official; there were a number of other similar cases of censorship during the year.
Since the outbreak of conflict, the government tried to dictate media coverage of the conflict and threatened those who tried to publish or broadcast views of the opposition. The media regulatory body advised international journalists not to describe conflict in the country in terms that could be perceived as divisive and described any such references as “hate speech.” The security forces regularly harassed, intimidated, and summoned journalists for questioning. The environment for media workers remained precarious throughout the year.
The media regulatory body announced its intention to shut down a radio station run by an international organization for “persistent noncompliance.” The media regulatory body stated it was not censoring the station, but rather monitoring for “hate speech and incitement.” Because the radio station’s transmitter is located within a secure compound, the government was unable to take it off the air, although the government continued to jam the station’s frequency to disrupt its broadcasts. The jamming affected areas within a mile of the national public service broadcaster’s compound in the capital. The radio station’s reporters were occasionally harassed when attempting to cover events outside of the secure compound and were not invited to government-sponsored media events.
Violence and Harassment : Security forces commonly intimidated or detained journalists whose reporting they perceived as unfavorable to the military or government. Security forces confiscated or damaged journalists’ equipment and restricted their movements. During the year journalists were interrogated, harassed, detained, and imprisoned. Security forces representatives frequently harassed journalists by detaining them at security forces headquarters or local police stations without formal charges. Government harassment was so pronounced that several journalists chose to flee the country. Journalists and media agencies that reported on news of the opposition could expect questioning and possibly closure. Journalists in the capital experienced threats and intimidation and routinely practiced self-censorship. On several occasions, high-level officials publicly used intimidating language directed toward media outlets and representatives.
There were multiple reports of abuses similar to the following example: A newspaper published a series of editorials describing a neighboring government as authoritarian and predicting its downfall. Subsequently, the editor began to experience a pattern of anonymous harassment and government restrictions. The editor and many other journalists were warned not to report on the situation in the neighboring country. A series of threatening anonymous telephone calls forced the editor into hiding, and he left the country for another country. The editor returned and resumed his life, although his newspaper was suspended, ostensibly for bureaucratic reasons. Later, as he was departing the capital for medical treatment, security forces officials at the airport boarded his plane and detained him, confiscated his passport, and ordered him to report to security forces headquarters for questioning. He was detained for several weeks without charges before being released. During his detention he did not have access to a lawyer, his family, or the medical treatment that prompted his attempt to travel from the capital.
There continued to be no credible investigation into the killing of a freelance journalist.

Internet Freedom

The government’s national communication authority frequently blocked access to certain websites, such as news websites and blogs, accused of disseminating messages considered not to be “in the best interest of peace building in this country.” There were credible reports the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority. The government also targeted and intimidated individuals, especially those outside of the capital, who were critical of the government in open online forums and social media.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The government restricted cultural activities and academic workshops. Security forces authorization is required for public events, including academic workshops, which particularly affected [NGO]s and other civic organizations. To obtain permission, the security forces sometimes requested a list of national and international staff members employed by the organizations and names of participants. Permission was often predicated upon the expectation that the security forces would be able to monitor the events. The government has [POPULATION FIGURE] people, and the restrictions on freedom of expression have been in place for many years, with the situation deteriorating over the past several years.

---

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The transitional constitution provides that every citizen has the right to participate in elections in accordance with the constitution and the law. Since a major referendum on self-determination, no elections have been held. Elected officials were arbitrarily removed, and others were appointed to take their place.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : Elections have been postponed several times over several years due to intense violence and insecurity. Since then, the president fired and appointed local government officials and parliamentarians by decree. The legislature passed amendments to the transitional constitution extending the terms of the president, the national legislature, and the state assemblies for three years. A peace agreement allowed for the extension of all terms for a transitional period. As of that time, constitutional amendments to reflect the agreement had yet to be passed by the legislature, and the transitional period had yet to commence.
An unfavorable environment for media and citizen expression hampered participation in political processes.
Political Parties and Political Participation : The [RULING PARTY] enjoyed a near monopoly of power in the government and continued to be the most broadly recognized political entity for many years. [RULING PARTY] membership conferred political and financial advantages, and there was great reluctance by opposition parties to shed the [RULING PARTY] name. For example, the main [OPPOSITION PARTY] was referred to as an offshoot of the [RULING PARTY] and most other political parties either were offshoots of the [RULING PARTY] or affiliated with it.
The peace agreement allows the government and opposition to appoint those to allocated seats in the parliament, the leadership of ministries, and the leadership of local governments; however, this had not taken place.
[OPPOSITION PARTY] parties complained that at times the government harassed party members. Legislation mandated specific requirements for those political parties that existed prior to the country's independence. Representatives of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] estimated the requirements affected approximately 25 parties.
Participation of Women and Minorities : The terms of a peace agreement forming a new unity government requires at least 35 percent female participation in the government at the national and state levels and specifies that one of the vice presidents should be a woman. The Local Government Act requires at least 25 percent of county commissioners and 25 percent of county councilors to be women. These conditions and laws were inconsistently implemented at both the state and national levels, and although women made gains in both the national legislature and the executive branch, they remained marginalized in the judiciary, local governments, and among traditional leaders. Representation was particularly poor at the local level, where there was little to no implementation of the act’s provisions. The current system also devolved substantial candidate selection power to political party leaders, very few of whom were women.
Traditional and cultural factors limited women’s participation in government. Women tended to be discouraged from assuming leadership positions because of the belief that such activities conflicted with their domestic duties.
Several [ETHNIC GROUP]s remained underrepresented or unrepresented in government, and the conflict exacerbated ethnic tensions and the imbalance in national and state-level political institutions.
The absence of translations of the constitution in local languages limited the ability of minority populations to engage meaningfully in political dialogue and contributed to low turnout for several consultations on a permanent constitution that took place around the country, including in the capital and a major city, affecting a [POPULATION FIGURE] people.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY], which gained independence from a neighboring country for many years, descended into civil war, when a rift between the president and a dismissed vice president triggered fighting among their supporters and divided the country along ethnic lines. A peace agreement reached in recent years further delayed overdue national elections, instituting an uneasy power-sharing arrangement among political elites who have presided over rampant corruption, economic collapse, and atrocities against civilians, journalists, and aid workers.

## Key Developments

The president and a rebel leader delayed the implementation of their peace agreement twice, extending their deadline. Government forces launched an offensive in a state in the center of the country in an effort to neutralize [ARMED GROUP] that opposed the peace deal over several months. Later that year, a UN mission reported that civilians suffered physical assaults, enslavement, rape, internal displacement, and the destruction of property during the fighting.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

The [RULING PARTY] dominates the political landscape, and most competition takes place within the movement, which splintered at the outbreak of the civil war. The president's hostility toward dissent within the [RULING PARTY] contributed to the conflict.
The agreement granted non-[RULING PARTY] parties representation in the parliament, but they lack the resources to operate effectively and the experience to formulate policy and set party platforms.
The agreement tasked a national committee with reviewing legislation to ensure that it meets international best practices. Recently, a [NGO] reported that the committee’s review was completed.
If fully implemented, the agreement would eventually provide an opportunity for opposition groups to contest long-overdue elections. However, the last elections featured violence and intimidation against opposition parties and [RULING PARTY] members whose loyalty to the president was in doubt.
The civil war has stifled ordinary politics and created a climate of fear. The security forces exercise an overbearing influence on political affairs and public life. The intelligence service also maintains a strong hold over the politics of [COUNTRY]. The activities of other [ARMED GROUP]s tied to partisan and ethnic factions have created an inhospitable climate for political participation by civilians.
Under the president's leadership, the [RULING PARTY] has sidelined citizens who are not members of a certain ethnic group, of which the president is a member. The exclusion of [ETHNIC GROUP]s has gone beyond the denial of political opportunities to include violent attacks, sexual exploitation, and the destruction of property.
In an attempt to address the chronic underrepresentation of women in political leadership positions, the agreement established a 35 percent quota for women in the planned government.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

[COUNTRY]'s transitional constitution guarantees freedom of the press, but this right is not respected in practice. According to [NGO], at least six members of the media have been killed in the course of their work in recent years. Defamation is prosecuted under criminal law, stifling free speech.
The government is particularly sensitive to coverage of events in neighboring countries and of the peace process. In late January, the editor in chief of a major newspaper temporarily fled the country after he was summoned by the media authority to explain the newspaper’s coverage of events in a neighboring country. In March, the authority suspended the newspaper’s publication. In late July, the security forces detained the editor and a radio journalist, who were separately traveling to another country; both were released without charge in August. In late October, the media authority revoked the accreditation of a foreign journalist who reported on the peace process for a major news agency, forcing her to leave the country.
In recent years, the UN reported that the security forces seized printing presses in an effort to restrict critical coverage of the government.
The interim constitution guarantees religious freedom, but houses of worship—used as places of refuge for civilians—have been attacked by gunmen seeking members of rival [ETHNIC GROUP]. In recent years, government forces attacked a religious institution in a major city, killing at least 10 people. Places of worship were looted and damaged during government-led offensives in the capital region.
There are no formal government restrictions on academic freedom. However, the education system was seriously disrupted by the civil war, with many schools closed or commandeered for military use. Some schools have reopened in recent years, but some teachers have not returned due to poor pay or delays in receiving their pay.
The security forces have extensive, unchecked powers to conduct surveillance, monitor communications, and infiltrate [NGO]. According to the UN, agents have used these powers to intimidate, detain, and murder journalists, opposition activists, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] representatives, members of certain ethnic minority communities, and members of faith-based organizations, forcing many to flee the country.
[COUNTRY]'s citizens have also been subjected to enforced disappearances in recent years. [NGO] recorded [POPULATION FIGURE] disappearances in a short period. A UN panel reported that two government critics who disappeared in another country several years ago were forcibly repatriated to [COUNTRY] and executed; later, a foreign government sanctioned several people it claimed were responsible for the deaths. In recent years, the UN reported that members of a youth-led movement went into hiding to avoid capture by security personnel.

