*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution and law provide citizens the ability to change their government through free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage. Voting is mandatory.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : The government held a free and fair federal parliamentary election. Voters re-elected the [RULING PARTY] government. The [RULING PARTY] won 77 seats in the [POPULATION FIGURE]-seat parliament; the [OPPOSITION PARTY] won 68 seats and others won six seats.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit participation of women or members of minorities in the political process, and they did participate.
[CERTAIN ETHNIC MINORITY COMMUNITIES] and other minorities generally were underrepresented in elective office relative to their share of the [POPULATION FIGURE]. Voters elected the first woman from an [ETHNIC GROUP] to the parliament for several years ago and the first woman from a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group to the parliament in the recent elections. The [COUNTRY]'s first [ETHNIC GROUP] cabinet minister was appointed recently.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] has a strong record of advancing and protecting political rights and civil liberties. Challenges to these freedoms include the threat of foreign political influence, harsh policies toward asylum seekers, discrimination against LGBT+ people, increasingly stringent checks against the press, and ongoing difficulties ensuring the equal rights of indigenous [ETHNIC GROUP].

## Key Developments

The prime minister won a new term in office when the [RULING PARTY] coalition won a federal election, despite expectations that the [OPPOSITION PARTY] would form the next government.
Later that year, a major broadcaster revealed that the intelligence service was investigating claims that a businessman connected to a foreign government attempted to plant an agent in the parliament. A major newspaper reported on a prominent politician’s ties to entities directed and supported by a foreign government that same month.
The security forces conducted two controversial raids against a newspaper editor and the public broadcaster, after they used leaked government documents as sources for stories in previous years. The editor, who was threatened with prosecution, took their case to the supreme court, which was considering the matter at year’s end. The broadcaster also sought judicial redress, and their case was still being weighed at the end of the year.
The supreme court restricted public servants’ ability to criticize the government when it ruled that a civil servant’s dismissal for criticizing immigration policy through a social media account was justified.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

Citizens of [COUNTRY] may organize political parties without restrictions. Registration and recognition as a political party requires a party constitution and either one member in the parliament, or at least 500 members on the electoral roll.
Power rotates between parties frequently, traditionally alternating between [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY]. Smaller left-leaning parties tend to ally with [OPPOSITION PARTY], while rural-oriented and conservative parties often ally with [RULING PARTY].
Political participation in [COUNTRY] is largely free from undue domestic influence. The monarch remains the head of state, but the monarchy’s power is strictly limited by the constitution and legal precedent.
Concerns about foreign interference in politics, particularly from a major world power, has persisted for several years. Foreign actors have allegedly funded candidates and parties, and a senator resigned due to their financial ties with companies linked to the foreign government. The government subsequently banned foreign donations to political parties, independent candidates, and other political campaign groups. Additionally, a transparency scheme, which came into force, requires persons who engage in political activities, such as lobbying, on behalf of a foreign government or other entity, to register publicly.
Foreign efforts to infiltrate [COUNTRY]'s politics continued in recent years. The security forces, the country’s intelligence agency, was revealed to be investigating claims that foreign intelligence attempted to plant an agent in the parliament. A businessman living in a major city claimed that a person tied to the foreign government offered him a large sum of money to run for a seat as a [RULING PARTY] member. The individual was found dead while awaiting trial on fraud-related charges. The security forces considered the story to be credible, and was investigating the individual who solicited them.
Connections to [RULING PARTY] lawmaker were also publicized in recent years. The lawmaker admitted that they were an honorary member of a chapter of a foreign-controlled organization. The press tied the lawmaker to a former head of a foreign company’s subsidiary in [COUNTRY]. The company donated a significant amount of money to [RULING PARTY] and relied on the lawmaker to win the government’s support for a project.
Political rights and electoral opportunities are granted to all citizens of [COUNTRY]. However, the interests of women are inadequately represented, and female members of the parliament have reported being bullied, intimidated, and harassed by their male colleagues. Female representation in the parliament remains fairly low; only a small percentage of the body is held by women. [OPPOSITION PARTY] employs an internal quota specifying that a certain percentage of its candidates must be women; this quota will rise in the future. [RULING PARTY] also aims for equal gender representation, but does not use a quota system.
Some voting restrictions—including requirements that voters hold a fixed address and a ban on voting by prisoners serving long sentences—disproportionately affect certain ethnic minority communities, who are also underrepresented in the parliament. However, politicians from these communities have recently entered the legislature; the first member from these communities won a seat several years ago. The government of [COUNTRY] has considered reforms to strengthen the political voice of these communities since they proposed a representative body that would advise the parliament on policy matters that affect them. The prime minister had dismissed the idea, but the government formed a panel to consider it, though the proposed body would not be constitutionally enshrined.
Representation of a religious minority group in national politics saw a breakthrough in recent years, when a lawmaker became the first member of the group to enter the parliament.
LGBT+ representatives have served in the parliament for many years, when a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] member became the first openly gay member of the upper house. The first openly gay member of the lower house was elected in a by-election several years ago.

[Reidentification] Actual: Australia (AUS) → CORRECT (top-1)
1. Australia
2. Canada
3. New Zealand
Reason: The country's strong record of advancing and protecting political rights and civil liberties, its system of mandatory voting, and the presence of a monarch as head of state with strictly limited powers, are key evidence pointing towards Australia as the top guess.

