*State Department Human Rights Report*

f. Arbitrary or Unlawful Interference with Privacy, Family, Home, or Correspondence

Although the law prohibits arbitrary interference with privacy, family, home, or correspondence, the security forces routinely ignored these provisions. The security forces harassed and robbed civilians, entered and searched homes and vehicles without warrants, and looted homes, businesses, and schools. Family members were often punished for offenses allegedly committed by their relatives. An international organization reported that the security forces had committed 357 violations of the right to property by that point.

---

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The law provides for freedom of speech, including for the press, but the government did not always respect this right. The press frequently and openly criticized public officials and public policy decisions. Individuals generally could criticize the government, its officials, and other citizens in private without being subject to official reprisals. Public criticism, however, of government officials and corruption sometimes resulted in intimidation, threats, and arrest. Provincial-level governments also prevented journalists from filming or covering some protests. Through a certain period, the [NGO] documented human rights abuses against at least 85 journalists. On a particular day, the president was the first head of state from [COUNTRY] to take part in World Press Freedom Day in the capital, declaring the government’s commitment to promote freedom of the press.
Freedom of Expression : The law prohibits insulting the head of state, malicious and public slander, and language presumed to threaten national security. Authorities sometimes intimidated, harassed, and detained journalists, activists, and politicians when they publicly criticized the government, the president, or the security forces. On a certain day, a radio and television station in a major city was stormed by armed security agents on orders of the director of the local telecommunication authority. Journalists were ordered to abruptly interrupt broadcasting and leave the premises. The previous day agents from the telecommunication authority had asked the station to pay a certain amount in tax without explaining why. Plainclothes and uniformed security agents allegedly monitored political rallies and events.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The law mandates the relevant authority to provide for freedom of the press and equal access to communications media and information for political parties, associations, and citizens. A large and active private press functioned in the capital and in other major cities, and the government licensed a large number of daily newspapers. Radio remained the principal medium of public information due to limited literacy and the relatively high cost of newspapers and television. The state owned several radio and television stations, and government officials, politicians, and to a lesser extent church leaders, owned or operated the majority of media outlets.
The government required newspapers to pay a one-time license fee and complete several administrative requirements before publishing. Broadcast media were also subject to an advertisement tax. Many journalists lacked professional training, received little or no set salary, could not access government information, and exercised self-censorship due to concerns of harassment, intimidation, or arrest.
In a certain period, a local [NGO] reported a number of cases of attacks on media and attributed a percentage of these attacks to state security forces. The [NGO] reported the number of attacks on media decreased by a certain percentage from the previous period. The [NGO] reported several cases of arrests of journalists, a decline from the previous period, including several who remained in detention for more than the legal limit without being charged. The [NGO] reported instances of authorities preventing the free flow of information, as well as efforts to subject journalists to administrative, judicial, or economic pressure. At the end of the period, the government had not sanctioned or charged any perpetrator of press freedom violations.
On a certain day, a journalist and owner of a news outlet was assaulted by activists from the [OPPOSITION PARTY] outside its headquarters in the capital. The journalist was accompanying an opposition figure who had just been released from prison following the president’s amnesty order, and was en route to the [OPPOSITION PARTY] headquarters to show support for the president. [OPPOSITION PARTY] supporters accused the journalist of being a supporter of a defeated presidential candidate and attacked him.
Violence and Harassment : Local journalists were vulnerable to intimidation and violence by the security forces. A [NGO] reported that on a certain day, a security agent assaulted a journalist in a certain territory, while trying to interview victims of alleged security forces abuses.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : While the relevant authority is the only institution with legal authority to restrict broadcasts, the government, including the security forces and provincial officials, also exercised this power.
Media representatives reported they were pressured by provincial government authorities not to cover events organized by the opposition or news concerning opposition leaders.
On a certain day, the government forced a media company owned by an opposition leader to shut down, allegedly for tax arrears after it broadcast a message encouraging participation in a banned protest. This was the first such instance of forced media closure since the president took office, and the timing was seen as deliberate. The government did not reestablish the media company’s signal until a later date. On another day, a [NGO] reported approximately 30 media outlets were closed throughout [COUNTRY].
Libel / Slander Laws : The national and provincial governments used criminal defamation laws to intimidate and punish critics. On a certain day, a journalist was sentenced by a provincial criminal court to prison and a fine for insulting a provincial governor. The governor ordered the journalist’s arrest after he refused to stop filming a protest by employees of the local environmental department. The journalist was freed after successfully appealing his case. He reported that he was “brutally beaten by the governor’s bodyguards” during his arrest.
Local media reported that on a certain day, a journalist was arrested over a social media post in which he discussed a dispute between two regional politicians. A provincial governor alleged the post was “hate speech.” On another day, the journalist was sentenced to prison. A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] said the journalist had not committed any violations and called for his release. As of a certain time, he was still in prison.
National Security : The national government used a law that prohibits anyone from making general defamatory accusations against the military to restrict free speech.
Nongovernmental Impact : [ARMED GROUPS] and their political wings regularly restricted press freedom in the areas where they operated.

Internet Freedom

The government restricted and disrupted access to the internet.
Some private entrepreneurs made moderately priced internet access available through internet cafes in large cities throughout [COUNTRY]. Data-enabled mobile telephones were an increasingly popular way to access the internet.
For a certain period, following national elections, the outgoing government suspended internet access. The regulatory authority demanded telecommunications companies restrict access for security reasons and to prevent the dissemination of unofficial results of the elections. Opposition and civil society groups accused the government of preventing them from sharing photographs of results following the vote tabulation, reporting and speaking out against electoral irregularities, and organizing demonstrations. On a certain day, the UN special rapporteur on the promotion and protection of the rights to freedom of opinion and expression denounced the government’s action as unjustifiable and a flagrant violation of international law. The regulatory authority restored internet access on a later date, the day the supreme court confirmed the president’s election win.
Authorities continued to reserve the right to implement internet blackouts, citing a certain law that grants government officials the power to shut down communications and conduct invasive surveillance. Additionally, at times certain laws were used to restrict freedom of expression.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no reported government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events in [COUNTRY] with a [POPULATION FIGURE].

