*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY] continues to recover from an armed conflict that ended several years ago. While security concerns and interference by the security forces can constrain freedoms of expression and association, these are generally upheld. However, splits in the [RULING PARTY] and unrest within the armed forces threaten stability. Several root causes of the country’s violent conflict remain, including ethnic and regional tensions, land disputes, corruption, and impunity. Women are significantly underrepresented in politics.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] welcomed a decision by the international court to acquit the former president of crimes against humanity over violence that occurred during a postelection conflict; the former president was conditionally released into a foreign country shortly after. Later that year, prosecutors filed an appeal against the verdict.
The government adopted new legislation that restricts the dissemination of “fake news,” criminalizes offending the president or other high-ranking officials, and includes one– to three-year prison sentences for organizing unauthorized assemblies.
A new law on marriage, approved that year, improved property rights for women and established a minimum age for marriage of 18, but also explicitly banned same-sex marriage.
In late December, the government issued an arrest warrant against the former speaker of the parliament and an [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader, alleging embezzlement and involvement in a coup plot. The individual avoided arrest when their flight was diverted to another country and then to a distant territory; they remained abroad at the end of the period.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Though defense and security forces are nominally under civilian control, problems of parallel command and control systems within the armed forces remain significant. For several years, the government has had a law meant to reduce the size of the officer corps and refine the military’s command structure, but these changes have largely gone unimplemented.
Additionally, after a period of relative calm, military mutinies exposed the fragility of the civilian government’s control over the state armed forces. Civilian control was tested again when special forces members scuffled with police in a major city in an effort to free an arrested colleague; this incident ended without violence, however.
Nonstate armed actors and former rebels enjoy significant influence, especially in certain regions.
Corruption and bribery remain endemic, and particularly affect the judiciary, police, and government contracting operations. Petty bribery also hampers citizens’ access to services ranging from obtaining a birth certificate to clearing goods through customs. A public anticorruption body was established, but is considered ineffective. Perpetrators at all levels seldom face prosecution.
The government generally awards contracts in a nontransparent manner. Access to up-to-date information from government ministries is difficult for ordinary citizens to acquire, although some ministries do publish information online. Legislation passed that year requires government agencies to disclose certain information, but enforcement has been inconsistent. The anticorruption body requires public officials to submit asset declarations, but this is not well enforced.

