*State Department Human Rights Report*

Executive Summary

[COUNTRY] is a constitutional republic and multiparty presidential democracy. In recent years voters re-elected the president in free and fair elections. Several years prior, voters elected representatives to the parliament in free and fair elections. The remaining seats are designated for [ETHNIC GROUP] and are left vacant.
The security forces enforce the law and combat criminal activity. The security forces report to the ministry of justice. The president appoints the security chief. Civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces.
Significant human rights issues included crimes involving violence or threats of violence targeting members of national and ethnic minorities.
The government took steps to investigate and prosecute officials who committed human rights abuses.

---

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The law provides for freedom of expression, including for the press, and the government generally respected this right. An independent press, an effective judiciary, and a functioning democratic political system combined to promote freedom of expression, including for the press.
Freedom of Expression : The law criminalizes incitement to hatred and violence based on race, color, religion, genealogical origin, national or ethnic origin, or sexual orientation. Such acts are punishable by up to five years’ imprisonment, a fine of up to [CURRENCY VALUE], or both. In recent months the attorney general ordered police to investigate whether public comments of a high-ranking [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] leader regarding homosexuals and women violated any laws. The attorney general concurred with the police’s finding that the leader’s remarks did not constitute hate speech nor an attempt to incite violence or hatred because of gender orientation or sexual identity.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views without restriction.
The law penalizes the use of geographical names and toponyms in [COUNTRY] other than those included in a government-published gazetteer. According to the law, anyone who publishes, imports, distributes, or sells maps, books, or any other documents in print or digital form that contain geographical names and toponyms on the island other than those permitted, commits an offense punishable by up to three years in prison, a fine of up to [CURRENCY VALUE], or both.

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content, and there were no credible reports that the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority.
The law criminalizes the use of computer systems to incite and promote racism, xenophobia, prejudice, racial discrimination, hate speech, and violence. Such acts are punishable by up to five years’ imprisonment, a fine of up to [CURRENCY VALUE], or both.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The government sometimes prevented visiting foreign academics and artistic groups from attending conferences or performing in [CITY], in accordance with laws that provide them the right to deny entry to visitors who declare a hotel in the area not originally owned by [ETHNIC GROUP] as the place of stay. In recent months immigration authorities at a major airport denied entry to a foreign academic invited by a university to deliver a series of lectures in the area. The academic reportedly boarded a flight back to another country and returned to [COUNTRY] via an airport in the area administered by [ARMED GROUP].

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Note

The numerical scores and status listed here do not reflect conditions in the northern part of the island, which is examined in a separate report.

## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a democracy that has de jure sovereignty over the entire island. In practice, however, the government controls only the southern, largely [ETHNIC GROUP]-speaking part of the island, as the northern area is ruled by a self-declared entity, recognized only by one country. Political rights and civil liberties are generally respected in [COUNTRY]. Ongoing concerns include societal discrimination against minority groups and weaknesses in the asylum system.

## Key Developments

In elections for [COUNTRY]’s seats in a major international parliamentary body, several parties won representation. One candidate, a member of [OPPOSITION PARTY], became the first [ETHNIC GROUP] to be elected to the body or to win office in [COUNTRY] in several decades.
[COUNTRY] continued to experience year-on-year increases in the number of arriving migrants and asylum seekers, putting additional strain on the government’s ability to house them and process their cases in a fair and timely manner.
In a major trial, a military officer received multiple prison sentences for the serial murders of several foreign women and some of their children. The murders prompted public discussion concerning the exploitative conditions faced by many migrant domestic workers in [COUNTRY].

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

Freedom of speech is constitutionally guaranteed, and media freedom is generally respected. A vibrant independent press frequently criticizes the authorities. Numerous private outlets compete with public media, and there are no restrictions on access to online news sources.
Freedom of religion is guaranteed by the constitution and generally protected in practice. Nearly 90 percent of the [POPULATION FIGURE] are followers of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group, and this group enjoys certain privileges, including religious instruction and some religious services in public schools. Non-followers of this group may opt out of such activities. The government recognizes institutions of a religious minority group and facilitates access to religious sites. This religious minority group has occasionally faced obstacles in the operation of their religious sites. Other religious minorities sometimes encounter discrimination.
Academic freedom is respected. However, state schools use textbooks containing negative language about [ETHNIC GROUP] communities, and there is some political pressure regarding schools’ treatment of sensitive historical issues.
People are generally free to engage in political and other sensitive discussions without fear of retribution or surveillance.

