*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

The repressive climate that followed a coup has eased since democratic elections were held in recent years. However, the [RULING PARTY] frequently interferes with opposition activities, the judiciary is subject to political influence, and military and police brutality is a significant problem.

## Key Developments

In recent months, video footage emerged of the prime minister assaulting a member of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] after a verbal altercation on the floor of the parliament. The prime minister received no punishment, while the member was suspended from the parliament for six months for refusing to apologize.
Military officers arrested a 16-year-old boy for posting an image on social media depicting graffiti insulting to the prime minister.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The executive branch under the prime minister determines the policies of government. With the [RULING PARTY] holding a strong parliamentary majority, the government has frequently pushed through bills and budgets with minimal scrutiny from the [OPPOSITION PARTY].
Safeguards against corruption are limited in their effectiveness. The anti-corruption agency has had limited success combatting institutional corruption. Corruption remains a serious problem. The anti-corruption agency has also allegedly pursued politically motivated corruption cases. Anticorruption lessons were introduced into the school curriculum that year.
Since the restoration of elective democracy for several years, government transparency and openness has improved. The government now organizes an annual briefing for [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] on the budget. Parliamentary sessions are broadcast live, and the official report of parliamentary proceedings is updated regularly. Although candidates for election are required to declare their assets, there is no law requiring public asset disclosures by members of parliament. [COUNTRY] lacks an access to information law, and requests for information from the media and the public are sometimes denied. In recent years, the [RULING PARTY] has used its parliamentary majority to rewrite parliamentary standing orders in a manner that limits debate on legislation and scrutiny of official statements.

