Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The law provides citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : Legislative, municipal, and presidential elections were completed that year. While there were isolated allegations of voter fraud, the elections were generally regarded as credible by international and domestic observers. Although voter turnout was low, citizens generally accepted the elections, and public demonstrations against the election results were muted, compared with previous years. Legislative and local elections scheduled for later that year did not take place and as of the end of the year had not been rescheduled.
Political Parties and Political Participation : Some [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY] exercised undue influence at the local level, including through threats to journalists and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. 
Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit the participation of women or members of [ETHNIC GROUP] or [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] in the political process, but social norms and the threat of electoral violence discouraged women from voting and, to a much greater extent, from running for office. The constitution requires that at least 30 percent of elected officials be women, but both chambers of the parliament fell well short of this quota (3 percent in the upper chamber, 2.5 percent in the lower chamber). Local elections, in which candidates run in groups where women must be at least 30 percent of the candidates on the ballot, did reach the quota. [NGO] noted female political candidates had little access to campaign financing and that female participation in politics was hindered by cultural norms rejecting female participation in politics.

