*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution and law provide for freedom of expression, including for the press. The government did not always effectively protect or respect these freedoms. Academics, journalists, [OPPOSITION PARTY] officials, and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported an atmosphere of intimidation and fear that restricted freedom of speech and press. Journalists expressed concern regarding government intimidation by the security forces.
Freedom of Expression : There were no official restrictions on the ability of individuals to criticize the government or on the discussion of matters of general public interest. Police imposed de facto restrictions on free speech and expression throughout the year. [OPPOSITION PARTY] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] members complained they could not freely criticize the government without fear of reprisal. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] accused the government of using the military and police to prevent its candidates from undertaking political activities.
Press and Media, Including Online Media : Media outlets and individual journalists regularly reported on a broad range of topics and criticized the government, the [RULING PARTY], and prominent political figures. The vast majority of critical articles did not result in retaliation from the government or the [RULING PARTY]. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and journalists, however, stated the government and [RULING PARTY] exerted substantial pressure on all forms of media and took retaliatory action when unspecified limits were crossed. During that year, the parliament passed legislation criminalizing photographing or recording video and audio of individuals without their consent. Conviction of violating this law is punishable by up to one year in prison.
Violence and Harassment : Journalists were subjected to violence, harassment, or intimidation due to their reporting. For example, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and journalists stated that authorities harassed journalists who reported on the involvement of the finance minister in a major financial scandal in which a large amount of money in government-backed loans was secretly contracted through a scheme that involved extensive bribery and kickbacks, including to sitting government officials.
In a major city, soldiers arrested a journalist as he was interviewing residents who were fleeing attacks by [ARMED GROUP]. He was reportedly held incommunicado in a military detention facility until his lawyers succeeded in obtaining his transfer to a civilian prison. Authorities stated he was suspected of terrorist activity and charged with violating state secrets. [NGO] stated mistreatment of the journalist while in detention included “physical aggression, forcing him to sleep handcuffed” and food deprivation. It concluded that this amounted “to cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment, or even torture.” After several months, the journalist was released, but his freedom of movement was restricted. Later, the public prosecutor charged him with “public instigation through the use of electronic media,” “slander against forces of public order,” and “instigation or provocation to public disorder.” As of the end of the year, the court had yet to accept the case.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : There were no official government guidelines for media. Journalists in the state-controlled and private media reported pressure to self-censor. Some journalists stated critical reporting could result in cancellation of government and [RULING PARTY] advertising contracts. The largest advertising revenue streams for local media came from ministries and state-controlled businesses. Domestic and international observers viewed the arrest and jailing of the journalist while interviewing persons displaced by violence in a major city as an example of de facto censorship.
National Security : Authorities cited antiterrorism and national security laws to arrest journalists who attempted to report on violence in a region affected by [ARMED GROUP] activity. A journalist was arrested, charged with using technology to violate state secrets, and jailed. He was released after several months. By the end of the year, he had yet to be tried.

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content; however, there were reports that the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority. For example, members of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported government intelligence agents monitored email and used false names to infiltrate social network discussion groups, and internet freedom advocates believed the intelligence service monitored online content critical of the government.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events; however, some academics reported self-censorship. Although the law provides for separation of party and state, in the capital and other areas, school principals and teachers were required to contribute money to the [RULING PARTY]’s election campaign. Teachers who refused to donate to the campaign were threatened with salary reductions. Some teachers were required to attend [RULING PARTY] election rallies and events. The population of [POPULATION FIGURE] was affected by these actions, which occurred for several years.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

The [RULING PARTY]'s unbroken incumbency before and since the introduction of multiparty elections has allowed it to establish significant control over state institutions. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] has disputed the results of recent elections, and its armed wing fought a low-level conflict against government forces that persisted until a truce was signed. [COUNTRY] also struggles with corruption, and journalists who report on it and other sensitive issues risk violent attacks.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the president of the [RULING PARTY] and the leader of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] signed a formal peace accord that paved the way for elections held that year. However, clashes between the national army and what police say were dissident [OPPOSITION PARTY] groups took place during the year, with at least seven deaths reported.
Presidential, legislative, and provincial elections were held. After a campaign marred by violence that mostly targeted [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidates and supporters, and which saw the assassination of a prominent independent election observer, the [RULING PARTY] overwhelmingly won the presidency and legislature and secured governorships in all provinces.
Domestic and international election observers and others decried the performance of the elections commission. The body, whose members are effectively appointed by the government, was sharply criticized over its stewardship of the vote in a major province, where it counted over 300,000 more registered voters than voting-age adults counted there during a previous census.
[MILITANT GROUP] continued to carry out attacks in the northern districts of a province, and voting was not possible in some areas there due to the lack of security.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

State-run outlets dominate the media sector, and authorities often direct such outlets to provide coverage favorable to the government. However, a number of smaller independent outlets provide important coverage.
Journalists frequently experience government pressure, harassment, and intimidation, which encourages self-censorship. The government is known to retaliate against journalists who criticize it by cancelling public advertising contracts. Journalists and political commentators appearing on television programs have been the targets of attacks and kidnappings in recent years. In late December there was an attempted kidnapping of the editor of a major newspaper. He escaped, but was severely beaten.
Religious freedom is generally respected, but government responses to attacks by [ARMED GROUP] have involved closing places of worship and detaining leaders of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], alarming human rights activists.
There are no legal restrictions on academic freedom. However, for several years, academics have been more hesitant to criticize the government, particularly after a law professor was murdered following a televised appearance in support of [OPPOSITION PARTY]. Indoctrination at primary schools has been reported, particularly in a major province, where some teachers have added [RULING PARTY] propaganda to their curricula.
[CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] claim that authorities monitor criticism of the government posted online. There have been reports of government intelligence agents monitoring the e-mails of [OPPOSITION PARTY] members.

