*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]’s democratic system holds competitive elections and has seen several transfers of power for many years. The country continues to struggle with political corruption and organized crime. The media sector is less pluralistic, as ownership concentration has considerably increased in recent years. Journalists encounter threats and even violence in the course of their work and are sometimes fired for not following the editorial line. Certain ethnic minority communities, particularly a minority group, face discrimination. Despite funding shortages and other obstacles, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s have been active and influential.

## Key Developments

That year, the parliament reinstituted the state subsidies for political parties, which had controversially been cut earlier. The amendment to the legislation governing political parties also lifted the ceiling on donations for political parties by private persons, businesses, and other organizations.
In recent years, the director general of a major public radio broadcaster removed a prominent journalist from a live-broadcast and suspended programming for an unprecedented five hours. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]’s strong reaction prompted the formation of a parliamentary committee to investigate the events. The broadcaster’s director was ousted shortly after.
That year, an outcry from right-wing political groups claimed the judiciary’s independence was threatened, after a foreign national, convicted of killing a law student several years prior, was granted parole. The groups called for the dissolution of a [NGO] due to its role in providing legal assistance to the convicted man.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

[COUNTRY]'s party system is competitive and quite volatile, featuring both long-term players like [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY], as well as cycles in which new parties emerge while others decline or disappear. [RULING PARTY] first won seats in the parliament for the first time in recent years, and a major election featured the emergence of a new alliance with 27 seats.
A controversial clause of a referendum—cutting state subsidies for political parties—was enacted by the parliament in recent years. The parliament also amended the Political Parties Act, removing the ceiling on donations to political parties by private persons, businesses, and other organizations, without introducing any further requirements on spending or reporting standards. A major [OPPOSITION PARTY] and other political experts criticized these changes, seeing their potential to undermine political pluralism and encourage corrupt practices. After an international organization voiced concerns about these amendments, the parliament voted to partially reinstate the state subsidy for political parties. However, no restrictions on private donations were reintroduced.
There have been multiple peaceful transfers of power between rival parties through elections for many years. In a recent parliamentary election, the [OPPOSITION PARTY], currently the main opposition party, gained 41 seats compared with the previous balloting. At a major international election, the [OPPOSITION PARTY] gained an additional seat.
Citizens of [COUNTRY] are generally free to make independent political choices. However, economic oligarchs dominate the major political parties and influence their platforms, a problem that is exacerbated by a lack of transparency in campaign finance law and the lack of limits to private donations for political parties that was lifted in recent years.
While marginalized groups generally have full political rights, the law dictates that electoral campaigns must be conducted in the dominant language, which hinders outreach to non-dominant language-speaking minority groups. A certain ethnic minority group is represented by a specific [OPPOSITION PARTY], but another [ETHNIC GROUP] is more marginalized. Small parties representing this [ETHNIC GROUP] are active, and many members of this group reportedly vote for the aforementioned [OPPOSITION PARTY], though none hold seats in the parliament. Members of far-right nationalist parties engage in hate speech against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] groups, [ETHNIC GROUP]s, and other groups, particularly during election periods, raising concerns about the normalization of xenophobia and discrimination.
There are currently 62 women in the [POPULATION FIGURE]-seat parliament, and the representation of women in politics remains an issue.
Further problems come from the influence of oligarchs on the vote in smaller municipalities and within marginalized groups in particular, an issue that is called the “controlled vote.”

