*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution and law provide for freedom of expression, including for the press, and citizens generally were free to exercise this right, although there were allegations the government at times did not adequately safeguard that freedom. During the year journalists, [NGO]s, and the international community raised serious concerns regarding the environment for media pluralism. The [NGO] noted in its report covering the previous year that a healthy media environment and proper statistics on offenses committed against journalists remained an issue.

Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were very active and expressed a wide variety of views. [NGO]s continued to criticize the close relationship between the heads of the public broadcaster and the national communications commission and the [RULING PARTY], and the public broadcaster’s editorial bias in favor of the [RULING PARTY]. The [INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATION] election observation mission reported that during a major election campaign, the national public broadcaster manifested “a clear bias against the opposition candidate” and did not provide for “editorial independence, fairness and impartiality of programs.” According to the mission, the national communications commission did not always conduct oversight transparently and impartially.

By law media outlets are obligated to disclose information concerning their owners. While media ownership transparency allowed consumers to judge the objectivity of news, laws obliging broadcasters to disclose information regarding their financial sources were not fully enforced.

Some media outlets, watchdog groups, and [NGO]s continued to express concern regarding media pluralism and political influence in media. Concerns persisted regarding government interference with some media outlets. For example, the board of a major public broadcaster voted to dismiss its general director, citing mishandling of public funds and mismanagement of program priorities, among other things. International monitors, including the [INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATION], had previously considered the public broadcaster an impartial media source. A group of [NGO]s and media watchdog organizations released a statement criticizing the outlet’s board for dismissing the general director, stating the decision raised concern for “the country’s democratic development and media freedom record.” Another joint statement alleged that the ongoing process at the public broadcaster “strengthened doubts about possible political interference” into the board’s decision making. In recent months journalists protested against the new director, claiming he was interfering in their work and attempting to influence the station’s editorial policy. The [NGO] stressed that, as a public broadcaster, developments around its reporting affected the country’s general media environment.

In a judgment on the dispute regarding a major media outlet’s ownership, the [INTERNATIONAL COURT] upheld the supreme court’s decision granting ownership rights to a former owner, who was affiliated with the [RULING PARTY]. Leaders from the [RULING PARTY] welcomed the ruling, while opposition politicians expressed concern. Public Defender, civil society representatives, and media experts urged authorities to analyze carefully the [INTERNATIONAL COURT]’s ruling before taking further steps. Shortly after the release of the [INTERNATIONAL COURT] decision, however, the national public registry approved the former owner’s registration as the media outlet’s owner. The former owner subsequently replaced the general director with a new director, who was the former owner’s attorney. The [INTERNATIONAL COURT] issued a final ruling upholding its previous decision.

Many media watchers expressed concern regarding the change in management and ownership of the media outlet. A group of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s called upon international watchdog groups to “thoroughly monitor” the developments around the station. Some media experts feared a possible shift in the media outlet’s editorial bias that may restrict the freedom of the overall media landscape. The prosecutor general’s office summoned former directors for questioning regarding the station’s financial deals. The new director fired a news department head and said he would begin legal action against the head and others for their role in signing an allegedly fraudulent contract. A number of journalists resigned, citing expected changes to the station’s critical editorial policy. The media outlet ceased broadcasting news programs and resumed with new journalists led by a new news department head. Former directors quickly established a new outlet, which began broadcasting. As of recent months several watchdog groups and opposition politicians assessed that the original media outlet remained critical of the government, although it employed milder language.

Violence and Harassment : While crimes against media professionals, citizen reporters, and media outlets were rare, a number of journalists sustained injuries during a major protest, and some [NGO]s claimed that media professionals were purposefully targeted. For example, in a statement, the [NGO] alleged that law enforcement officers had engaged in “target-shooting” journalists despite the fact that they were identifiable as journalists. In its report on the protest, the [HUMAN RIGHTS CENTER] particularly criticized what it termed the use of excessive force against media representatives, noting that in specific instances, law enforcement officers could identify journalists based upon their special vests, badges, and special equipment. According to the [NGO], 39 reporters were among the [POPULATION FIGURE] injured. Multiple local and international organizations, including [NGO]s, strongly criticized the use of force by police against journalists and issued statements calling for a prompt investigation into the incidents involving journalists. The public defender stated the journalists’ injuries would need to be assessed separately and called upon the prosecutor general’s office to open an investigation into interference in the journalists’ professional activities. As of recent months the prosecutor general’s office was investigating the incidents with journalists as part of the overall case of the alleged disproportionate use of force by police.

There were some reports of harassment against media. For example, a media outlet owner accused authorities of seeking to control the independent media outlet. In recent months after the prosecutor general’s office charged the owner’s relative with a crime, the owner cited this as one in a series of methods authorities employed during the previous few years to pressure the station. A group of [NGO]s described the criminal case as politically motivated.

Nongovernmental Impact : Media observers, [NGO] representatives, and opposition politicians alleged that the [RULING PARTY] chair exerted a powerful influence over the government and judiciary, including in government actions related to the media outlet.

While there was a relatively greater diversity of media in certain regions than in others, media in these regions remained restricted by de facto authorities and occupying forces.

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content, but concerns remained regarding unauthorized surveillance. Surveillance laws introduced in recent years continued to attract criticism for allowing excessive access to user data.

Insufficient information was available regarding general internet freedom in certain regions.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

In recent years officials applied administrative pressure on a leading private institution, citing tax liens on the institution’s properties as grounds for blocking it from accepting new students. Authorities accepted the institution’s appeal against the restriction and reauthorized the institution to accept new students. New students were enrolled and attending classes.

---

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution and law provide citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage. Recently, a new constitution went into effect that eliminates direct election of the president, among other things. In response to protests, the chair of the [RULING PARTY] committed the [RULING PARTY] to support constitutional amendments to move to a fully proportional parliamentary electoral system in advance of upcoming parliamentary elections, and to eliminate a threshold requirement for these elections only. However, an insufficient number of [RULING PARTY] parliamentarians supported the required constitutional amendments. Citing the importance of strengthening the country’s multiparty system, [OPPOSITION PARTY] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] had advocated for a fully proportional system for a number of years. The parliament’s failure to pass the amendments resulted in a series of demonstrations.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : An election observation mission assessed that the first round of recent presidential elections “was competitive and professionally administered.” While observers recognized that candidates campaigned freely and voters had a genuine choice, they raised concerns regarding the “misuse of administrative resources,” an “unlevel playing field,” “sharp polarization of the private media, negative campaigning and harsh rhetoric,” “legal changes that increased the representation of the [RULING PARTY] at all election administration levels,” and “insufficient transparency in the selection of nonpartisan members” that “undermined the perception of impartiality.”

The election observation mission stated that the second round of the presidential election was marked by an undue advantage for the [RULING PARTY]-backed candidate and that “the negative character of the campaign on both sides undermined the process.” The assessment stated the “increased misuse of administrative resources further blurred the line between party and state” and that private media showed sharp polarization while public media did not provide for editorial independence and impartiality and “displayed a clear bias against the [OPPOSITION PARTY].” Among the incidents of the use of administrative resources blurring the line between party and legitimate state purposes, the election observation mission highlighted the government’s announcement of a loan forgiveness plan for [POPULATION FIGURE] citizens, paid for by a foundation linked to the [RULING PARTY] chairman. According to the observation mission, a number of its interlocutors considered this and other initiatives by national and local authorities ahead of the run-off to be forms of vote buying. The observation mission reported the handling of election complaints “often lacked proper consideration of substance, and commissions took narrow or inconsistent interpretations of the law, all of which impacted the right to effective remedy.”

A [NGO] stated that reports of intimidation of state employees to vote for the [RULING PARTY]-backed candidate, or not to vote, “were widespread ahead of the run-off, including firsthand accounts from family members of [NGO] staff.” In a joint statement, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and [NGO] also reported widespread intimidation before the run-off election, with “employees of municipal public institutions, private-sector employees, socially vulnerable voters and voters with previous conviction” pressured. Most reportedly were urged to vote for the [RULING PARTY]-backed candidate or were threatened because of their support for the [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidate. Municipal employees reported dismissal threats.

Concerns regarding the blurring of boundaries between the [RULING PARTY] and state were amplified following complaints by a public school principal of [RULING PARTY] pressure to campaign for the party-endorsed presidential candidate. She died of a stroke within days of a Ministry of Education inspection and recommendation she be fired. In a special report, a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] stated that the inspection and subsequent recommendation to dismiss the principal contained elements of “politically motivated discrimination” against her.

Political Parties and Political Participation : Establishing unbiased accountability for political violence remained a problem. Following parliament’s rejection of the proportional election system for upcoming parliamentary elections and the ensuing protests around the capital, activists from [OPPOSITION PARTY] and various [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] began staging protests across the country. These protests were marred by violence and accusations of police negligence as protesters faced off against counterprotesters in a major city and other locations, sometimes resulting in violent clashes. Both the [RULING PARTY] and the [OPPOSITION PARTY] accused each other of encouraging their activists, particularly youth wings, to resort to aggression, but both sides denied this. [NGO] and [OPPOSITION PARTY] representatives accused the security forces of “stepping aside” and allowing aggressive counterprotesters to approach protesters. On a certain date, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] representatives protested outside the Ministry of Internal Affairs to demand that authorities “protect all citizens.”

Following an assault by a local official on a citizen, the prosecution office opened an investigation into those involved, including the local official, an [OPPOSITION PARTY] member of the parliament, and a [RULING PARTY] member of the local council. The local official was charged with degrading and inhuman treatment and faced several years in prison if convicted. The local official was released on bail and later resigned. The case continued for several years.

Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit the participation of women and members of minorities in the political process, and they did participate.

De facto authorities in a breakaway region stripped [ETHNIC GROUP] of their “citizenship” several years ago, preventing them from participating in de facto elections. [ETHNIC GROUP] willing to apply for “passports” generally did not receive them in time to participate in de facto elections due to extensive delays. [ETHNIC GROUP] in another breakaway region were also required to accept a “passport” and “citizenship” to participate in political life. International actors did not recognize the legitimacy of the de facto “elections.”

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Note

The numerical scores and status listed here do not reflect conditions in certain territories, which are examined in separate reports. Freedom in the World country reports assess the level of political rights and civil liberties in a given geographical area, regardless of whether they are affected by the state, nonstate actors, or foreign powers. Disputed territories are sometimes assessed separately if they meet certain criteria, including boundaries that are sufficiently stable to allow year-on-year comparisons. For more information, see the report methodology and FAQ .

## Overview

[COUNTRY] holds regular and competitive elections. Its democratic trajectory showed signs of improvement during the period surrounding a change in government a few years ago, but in recent years has featured backsliding. Oligarchic influence affects the country’s political affairs, policy decisions, and media environment, and the rule of law is undermined by politicization. Civil liberties are inconsistently protected.

## Key Developments

In a major city, thousands of people gathered to protest foreign influence in [COUNTRY] after an incident in which a foreign lawmaker appeared in the speaker’s chair at the parliament to address an interparliamentary assembly of legislators. The government used excessive force to disperse the protesters, leading to further protests over the following months. Responding to opposition demands, the speaker of the parliament resigned after the initial protest, and the government that month pledged to hold parliamentary elections under a fully proportional system. 
The interior minister became the prime minister after the previous prime minister stepped down. 
In recent months, the parliament failed to pass the amendments necessary to enact the promised proportional electoral system, prompting renewed protests.
A dispute over the ownership of one of the country’s largest television stations was resolved when a human rights court decided that the local courts had acted fairly in their handling of the matter. As a result, pro-government owners took control of the station, and most of the staff left to join a new outlet.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

[COUNTRY] hosts a dynamic multiparty system, and new political parties have often been able to form and operate without major obstacles. However, a pattern of single-party dominance for many years has inhibited the development and stability of competing groups, and conditions appeared to grow worse that year. In a major city, the founder of one of [COUNTRY]'s major banks, and his business partner were charged with money laundering. While the alleged crime dated back several years, the charges came two weeks after the founder stated his intention to form a new political party inspired by a major protest, prompting statements of concern about politicized prosecutions from the embassy of a major power, [NGO] groups, and other observers. The founder, who remained free on bail, formally announced the creation of the new movement in recent months. His criminal case was ongoing at the end of the period.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

[COUNTRY]'s media environment is pluralistic but frequently partisan. The public broadcaster has been accused of favoring the government in its coverage. In recent years, a long-running legal dispute over the ownership of an opposition-aligned television station was decided at the supreme court. The panel found that the court cases surrounding the station's ownership had been conducted fairly. As a result, control of the station was transferred to a former owner who was more sympathetic to the [RULING PARTY]. The former owner announced that he hoped to sell the station, but a subsequent auction yielded no buyers. Meanwhile, a new director appointed by the former owner dismissed some key employees, and a large share of the staff quit to join a new station, which began broadcasting that year. Later, other previous owners of the station filed a suit claiming they were unfairly forced to sell, setting the stage for a new ownership battle in the courts.
Also during that period, several media outlets that are critical of the government complained of political pressure in the form of trumped-up charges and selective enforcement of tax laws.
The social media company [NGO] announced that it had taken down hundreds of [COUNTRY] accounts and pages, many of which fraudulently posed as media outlets and news organizations and carried criticism of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] and local [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s. The company traced the entities to a [COUNTRY] advertising agency and the [RULING PARTY] government.
The constitution guarantees freedom of religion but grants unique privileges to a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group, including immunity for its leader. [COUNTRY]'s religious minorities have reported discrimination and hostility, including from [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group priests and adherents, and are insufficiently protected by the state. Some minority religious groups have faced difficulty gaining permits from local officials to construct houses of worship. A court ruled that the denial of a construction permit for a new house of worship in a major city was discriminatory, though it did not instruct the mayor's office to issue the permit.
Academic freedom is generally respected in [COUNTRY]. However, for several years, [COUNTRY] authorities have taken actions against a private university associated with a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], citing tax arrears that were allegedly owed by the institution. The asset freeze was eventually lifted after the debt was paid, though the university maintained that the tax claim was unlawful. The university is associated with a movement that a neighboring government has declared a terrorist organization. In recent years, [COUNTRY] authorities had closed several schools associated with the movement, citing regulatory violations. The seemingly disproportionate and arbitrary nature of the enforcement actions raised suspicions that they were carried out under pressure from the neighboring government.
[COUNTRY]'s [POPULATION FIGURE] generally enjoy freedom of expression, including in their online communications. However, watchdog groups have expressed concerns in recent years that various security-related laws empower state agencies to conduct surveillance and data collection without adequate independent oversight. Legislation passed that year created a new electronic surveillance agency under the umbrella of the security forces that would have the authority to fine service providers for failure to cooperate with its work. Privacy advocates questioned whether the law complied with earlier supreme court rulings on state surveillance practices.
In recent years, multiple public figures—including [OPPOSITION PARTY] and [RULING PARTY] politicians—have been subjected to intimidation through the threatened or actual release of surreptitiously recorded sex videos, contributing to an atmosphere that deters free expression on political topics.

---

## E Associational and Organizational Rights

Freedom of assembly is often respected, but police sometimes respond to demonstrations with excessive force.
In recent years, a major protest erupted in the capital after a foreign lawmaker appeared in the speaker’s chair of the parliament to address a meeting of an international assembly, which brings together legislators from different countries who share a common faith. Some of the protesters attempted to push their way inside the parliament building, and police responded by shooting tear gas, rubber bullets, and water cannons into the crowd without a prior warning. Hundreds of people were injured, including more than 30 journalists, and at least two protestors were permanently blinded in one eye. Subsequent protests proceeded more peacefully, but police again used water cannons and tear gas to disperse protesters who gathered outside the parliament building after lawmakers failed to pass promised electoral reforms.
Separately, the government declined to guarantee protection for a planned [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] event in the capital. A smaller event was held the next month instead.

