*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The law provides for freedom of expression, including for the press, but the combination of laws and regulations governing libel and media content as well as legal harassment, physical intimidation of individuals and media, and executive influence on the judiciary resulted in significant repression of these freedoms. National and international groups, such as [NGO], [NGO], [NGO], [NGO], [NGO], and [NGO], condemned the former regime's efforts throughout the year to restrict press freedom and create a climate of fear and self-censorship.
Freedom of Expression : The law makes insulting the president punishable by six to 30 months in prison without bail, with lesser penalties for insulting lower-ranking officials. The former regime gave final approval to legislation that stipulates prison sentences of up to 20 years. While the former regime stated the purpose of the law was to “promote peace and tolerance,” [NGO] observed the vaguely written law could be used to silence political parties, activists, and civil society leaders as well as media outlets and journalists. Comments exposing another person to public contempt or hatred are punishable by prison sentences of one to three years and fines. In recent years, [NGO] reported [POPULATION FIGURE] persons were arrested for online criticism of the regime.
On a specific date, members of the security forces arrested a musician for “instigating hate.” The musician posted tweets critical of the regime after losing her position with the national orchestra for signing a petition in opposition to the president. After being detained for several weeks, the musician was released from prison, although she was prohibited from leaving the country or making public statements.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The law provides that inaccurate reporting that disturbs the public peace is punishable by prison terms of two to five years. The requirement that media disseminate only “true” information was undefined and open to politically motivated interpretation.
The law prohibits all media from disseminating messages that incite or promote hate or intolerance for religious, political, gender-related, racial, or xenophobic reasons; incite, promote, or condone criminal acts; constitute war propaganda; foment anxiety in the population or affect public order; do not recognize legitimate government authorities; incite homicide; or incite or promote disobedience of the established legal order. Penalties range from fines to the revocation of licenses. The threat of nonrenewal of operating licenses systematically led to self-censorship on the part of several media outlets.
Despite such laws, the president and the [RULING PARTY] used the nearly [POPULATION FIGURE] former regime-owned or -controlled media outlets to insult and intimidate the political opposition throughout the year. The president of the [RULING PARTY] continued to use his weekly television program to denounce individual journalists and media outlets.
The law declares telecommunications a “public interest service,” thereby giving the government authority to regulate the content and structure of radio, television, and audiovisual production sectors. The law provides that the government may suspend or revoke licenses when it judges such actions necessary in the interests of the nation, public order, or security. The law empowers the government to impose heavy fines and cancel broadcasts for violations of its norms; the national telecommunications commission oversees the law’s application.
The former regime continued legal actions against high-profile independent media outlets. In recent years, the supreme court ordered a major media outlet to pay a significant fine to the president of the [RULING PARTY] for “moral damage and injury” for reprinting an article that indicated the president was under investigation.
[NGO] reported [POPULATION FIGURE] violations of freedom of expression between January and April, a significant increase compared with the same period in recent years and the second highest figure since the organization began tracking cases. The most common violations were aggressions against journalists and censorship. The former regime-owned and -influenced media provided almost continuous proregime programming. In addition, private and public radio and television stations were required to transmit mandatory nationwide broadcasts throughout the year, including a daily news broadcast that provided reports and summaries of former regime activities. Media reported the security forces regularly barred journalists from accessing the parliament to cover the legislative body’s debates and activities. [NGO] noted that the state-owned internet service provider also routinely blocked commercial streaming and web searches during opposition speeches and during weekly parliament sessions.
The former regime detained [POPULATION FIGURE] journalists in the first few months of the year, up from [POPULATION FIGURE] detentions in recent years, according to [NGO]. On a specific date, security agents detained a journalist and confiscated equipment, following his reporting on nationwide blackouts that struck the country. On his weekly television program, the president of the [RULING PARTY] accused the journalist of being involved in a conspiracy to sabotage the country’s electrical system. After being charged with “instigating crimes,” the journalist was released, although he was prohibited from leaving the country or making public statements.
The law requires practicing journalists to have journalism degrees and be members of the national college of journalists, and it prescribes jail terms of three to six months for those practicing the profession illegally. These requirements are waived for foreigners and opinion columnists.
Violence and Harassment : Senior national and state leaders of the former regime continued to harass and intimidate privately owned and opposition-oriented television stations, media outlets, and journalists by using threats, property seizures, administrative and criminal investigations, and prosecutions. The national journalists’ union reported [POPULATION FIGURE] attacks on journalists from January to June. The president and [RULING PARTY] officials used regime-controlled media outlets to accuse private media owners, directors, and reporters of fomenting antiregime destabilization campaigns and coup attempts. Former regime officials also harassed foreign journalists working in the country.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : [NGO] noted the former regime’s preference for using legal proceedings, financial sanctions, and administrative actions against unfavorable news outlets instead of shutting them down outright. Members of the independent media stated they regularly engaged in self-censorship due to fear of regime reprisals. This resulted in many journalists posting articles to their personal blogs and websites instead of publishing them in traditional media.
The former regime also exercised control over content through licensing and broadcasting requirements. The national telecommunications commission acted selectively on applications from private radio and television broadcasters for renewal of their broadcast frequencies. According to [NGO] reports, approximately [POPULATION FIGURE] percent of radio stations were in “illegal” status throughout the country due to the commission’s not having renewed licenses for most radio stations in recent years.
According to the local journalists’ union, print news outlets closed due to the former regime’s economic policies, which made it difficult for independent newspapers to access foreign currency, preventing many from purchasing critical supplies and equipment necessary for day-to-day business operations. National and regional newspapers went out of print for lack of supplies, especially newsprint.
The former regime controlled a large portion of the country’s businesses and paid for advertising only with regime-owned or regime-friendly media.
Libel /Slander Laws : Regime-aligned officials engaged in reprisals against individuals who publicly expressed criticism of the president or regime policy.
National Security : The law allows the government to suspend or revoke licenses when it determines such actions necessary in the interests of public order or security. The former regime exercised control over the press through a public entity, which is responsible for “compiling, processing, analyzing, and classifying” both regime-released and other public information with the objective of “protecting the interests and objectives of the state.”
During the year the president renewed the “state of exception” he first invoked for many years, citing a continuing economic emergency, and granted himself the power to restrict rights otherwise provided for in the constitution. The emergency decree allows the president to block any action he deems could “undermine national security” or could “obstruct the continuity of the implementation of economic measures for the urgent reactivation of the national economy.” The parliament continued systematically to refuse to ratify each renewal, and the supreme court annulled each refusal, reasoning that the assembly’s “contempt” status made its failure to endorse the renewal “unconstitutional.” According to [NGO], the “state of exception” negatively affected the rights to freedom of association and expression.
Nongovernmental Impact : Widespread violence in the country made it difficult to determine whether attacks on journalists resulted from common criminal activity or whether criminals or others targeted media members.

Internet Freedom

The former regime restricted or disrupted access to the internet and censored online content. The former regime exercised broad control over the internet through the state-run national telecommunications commission. [NGO], an organization focused on freedom of expression and social justice, reported that the commission supported monitoring of private communications and repression of internet users who expressed dissenting opinions online. According to media reports, users of social networks accused the commission of monitoring their online activity and passing identifying information to regime intelligence agencies.
The law puts the burden of filtering prohibited electronic messages on service providers, and it allows the national telecommunications commission to order service providers to block access to websites that violate these norms and sanctions service providers with fines for distributing prohibited messages. [NGO] reported that in recent years, private and regime-controlled internet providers following the commission’s orders blocked access to [POPULATION FIGURE] webpages. A significant percentage of the censored domains during this period belonged to social media platforms and news outlets.
The commission’s director reiterated the claims of his predecessors that the commission’s role is to enforce the law and prevent dissemination of illegal information or material unsuitable for children and adolescents. Nevertheless, the former regime continued to block internet sites that posted currency exchange rates differing from the former regime’s official rate. The former regime-owned internet service provider facilitated blockages. According to [NGO], the regime blocked websites during events of public interest. On a specific date, there were several internet cuts in a major city, affecting users of social media platforms and search engines in particular, combined with restrictions on access to social media platforms.
Regime-aligned intelligence agencies, which lacked independent oversight, conducted surveillance for political purposes. Courts relied on evidence obtained from anonymous sources to harass perceived opponents of the former regime, and senior former regime-aligned officials used personal information gathered by these sources to intimidate regime critics and human rights defenders. Users were arrested and criminally accused for actions such as tweeting information publicly available on webpages.
In recent years, proregime social media accounts published a database of opposition sympathizers’ personal data, which was the result of a former regime-linked operation.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no substantiated reports of former regime restrictions on cultural events, but the former regime imposed restrictions on academic freedom. [NGO], a local human rights organization focused on academic freedom, reported the former regime retaliated against opposition-oriented autonomous universities by providing insufficient funding and failing to adjust budgetary allocations to inflation. According to media reports, universities ran deep deficits, receiving less than [POPULATION FIGURE] percent of the funds they budgeted to cover operating costs. For many years, the national university council, the government’s regulating body for university education, relinquished its functions to the parliament, disregarding the law requiring university autonomy.
The former regime continued to increase its control over local universities, including the admissions process.
In recent years, the supreme court ordered a major university to hold university elections in several months. The ruling, which applied to several other public and private universities as well, stipulates that the elected candidate must win in at least three of the five electoral sectors and must receive an absolute majority of votes. Students and university leaders called the ruling an attack on university autonomy, in violation of the constitution, and said it would lead to the installation of regime-aligned sympathizers at the heads of universities.
The former regime continued its practice, announced in recent years, of educational financial incentives for holders of a social benefits card provided primarily to regime supporters.

---

b. Freedoms of Peaceful Assembly and Association

Freedom of Peaceful Assembly

The constitution provides for this right, but the [RULING PARTY] generally repressed or suspended it. The law regulates the right to assembly. [NGO] groups continued to criticize the law as enabling the [RULING PARTY] to charge protesters with serious crimes for participating in peaceful demonstrations. Ambiguous language in the law also allowed the [RULING PARTY] to criminalize organizations that were critical of it. Protests and marches require authorization from the [RULING PARTY] in advance and are forbidden within designated “security zones.” Citizens organized sporadic and often spontaneous small-scale protests throughout the year to demand basic goods and services such as water, gasoline, and electricity. The [OPPOSITION PARTY] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] organized marches to support the [OPPOSITION LEADER] and demand a transitional government and new presidential elections. The [NGO] documented [POPULATION FIGURE] protests in the first six months of the year, more than double the number in the same period in recent years. According to the [NGO], between January and May, a total of 66 persons died during protests; some of these incidents were marked by an alleged excessive use of force by the security forces and [ARMED GROUP]. The security forces detained more than 1,300 persons during protests between January and May, according to [NGO].
During a protest in a major city, a 16-year-old was blinded after police forces fired 52 rubber pellets at his face. According to media reports, a police investigation found that the security forces moved to repress the protest without warning when they fired rubber bullets into the crowd. The attorney general announced that authorities charged two police officers with cruel treatment in the case.

Freedom of Association

The constitution provides for freedom of association and freedom from political discrimination, but the [RULING PARTY] did not respect these rights. Although professional and academic associations generally operated without interference, a number of associations complained that the supreme court and the electoral council, which is responsible for convoking all elections and establishing electoral dates and procedures, repeatedly interfered with their attempts to hold internal elections.
Legislation passed that year called on the foreign ministry to suspend international funding to [NGO] when “it is presumed” that the funding is used with “political purposes or for destabilization.”

---

The constitution provides for freedom of religion on the condition its practice does not violate public morality, decency, or public order. [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] leaders stated the [RULING PARTY] and its aligned groups disrupted church services, attacked churchgoers, and destroyed church property. Representatives of [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and [NGO] said the government harassed, intimidated, and retaliated against their clergy and other members for continuing to call attention to the country's humanitarian crisis. On a specific date, the security forces arrested an evangelical Protestant pastor in [CITY] as he delivered a prayer calling for the “spiritual liberation” of [COUNTRY] during an annual religious event. The pastor, also an active duty officer, was reportedly arrested for disobedience and remained in detention for several years. Media reported [ARMED GROUP] aligned with the [RULING PARTY] attacked churches and their congregants during the year. According to a high-ranking [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] official, a group of [ARMED GROUP] forced their way into a church in a major city during a worship service. The [ARMED GROUP] attacked and injured approximately 15 worshippers inside, fired their weapons, defaced and destroyed church property, and confiscated items of value. The official said law enforcement officers stationed nearby did not intervene during the attack. Some members of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] community stated the [RULING PARTY] and those sympathetic to it used anti-Zionism to mask anti-Semitism. During the year, editorials in pro-[RULING PARTY] media outlets accused the leader of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] and nominated representatives, as agents or lobbyists of Zionism. Representatives of [NGO] said criticism of a foreign country in [RULING PARTY]-controlled or -affiliated media continued to carry anti-Semitic overtones, sometimes disguised as anti-Zionist messages. They said [RULING PARTY]-owned or -associated media and government supporters again denied or trivialized a major historical atrocity, citing reports of the [RULING PARTY] comparing sanctions against [COUNTRY] to persecution of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY].

The [NGO] representatives said many private citizens in addition to government officials continued to believe members of the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] community maintained direct lines of communication with a foreign government and placed that government's interests above those of [COUNTRY], which made them concerned their community could become targets of anti-Semitic acts.

A foreign government has no diplomatic relations with the [RULING PARTY] and recognizes the leader of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] as the legitimate leader. The foreign government's embassy suspended operations in the capital and continued to operate from a nearby city and its own capital through the end of the year. Prior to the suspension, [RULING PARTY] officials again did not respond to the foreign government's embassy requests for meetings on religious freedom and related issues. The embassy maintained close contact with a wide range of religious groups, including the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], [RELIGIOUS MINORITY], and [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] communities. Embassy representatives and these groups discussed the [RULING PARTY]'s imposed registration procedures and delays; harassment by its aligned and armed civilian gangs; anti-Semitic posts in social media and in [RULING PARTY]-controlled media; and other anti-Semitic acts, affecting a [POPULATION FIGURE] people.

---

d. Freedom of Movement

The constitution provides for freedom of internal movement, foreign travel, emigration, and repatriation; however, the former [RULING PARTY] regime did not respect these rights.
On a specific date, the former regime closed its borders with neighboring countries to prevent the entry of international aid. Media reported the borders with some neighboring countries were reopened a few months later and partially reopened with another neighboring country one month after that.
In recent years, the former [RULING PARTY] regime announced the deployment of a special migration police unit in a state on the border with a neighboring country. Although some [NGO]s expressed concern the former regime would use the unit to restrict international travel of select individuals, the former regime asserted the force would essentially be customs and border patrol units. The former regime declared the migration police would provide citizen security at migration points and established numerous points of control to monitor the border situation and dispel what it called myths regarding a supposed in-country migration crisis.
The security forces often used excessive force to control residents in states along the border with a neighboring country, with particular violence perpetrated by [ARMED GROUP]s against citizens of a specific state in late winter.
While no official statistics were available, a women’s shelter reported recurring problems with gender-based violence and trafficking of refugee women. [NGO]s reported individuals crossing through informal border crossings controlled by [ARMED GROUP]s faced significant protection risks, including gender-based violence. Individuals were often forced to pay a form of taxation at the informal border crossing or be indebted to those controlling them, exposing them to risks of exploitation, harassment, and sexual violence, as well as recruitment into drug trafficking and other [ARMED GROUP]s.
See the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]’s report on trafficking in persons.
In-country Movement : The former regime restricted the movement of certain [OPPOSITION PARTY] leaders, preventing them from traveling on regime-controlled airlines and refusing to allow them to board some domestic flights.
Foreign Travel : Obtaining a passport became increasingly difficult. Prospective applicants waited overnight in lines and often did not receive passports after [POPULATION FIGURE] years of delays. Some applicants reportedly paid several thousands of dollars to obtain a passport. The former regime repeatedly seized passports from journalists, members of the [OPPOSITION PARTY], and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] members at ports of entry without explanation as they attempted to depart the country.
Exile : In contrast with previous years, there were no cases of citizens denied the right to return.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]’s democratic institutions have deteriorated for many years, but conditions have grown sharply worse in recent years due to the continued concentration of power in the executive and harsher crackdowns on the opposition. The authorities have closed off virtually all channels for political dissent, restricting civil liberties and prosecuting perceived opponents without regard for due process. The country’s severe humanitarian crisis has left [POPULATION FIGURE] struggling to meet basic needs, and driven mass emigration. Government corruption is pervasive, and law enforcement has proven unable to halt violent crime.

## Key Developments

The supreme court swore in the president in January, following his victory in a snap election that failed to meet minimum international standards and was widely condemned as illegitimate.
In January, [COUNTRY]’s democratically elected parliament declared its head to be [COUNTRY]’s interim president as a constitutional response to the president’s reelection in the previous year’s flawed poll. The interim president has since received the backing of more than 50 countries. The president, who has consolidated power over most of [COUNTRY]’s institutions, and is backed by the country’s powerful military and international patrons, has refused to relinquish power.
The first half of the year saw mass demonstrations by those who backed the interim president’s proclamation, notably in January and May. The security forces and allied [ARMED GROUP]s responded with violence, and dozens of protest-related deaths were reported. Mass arbitrary detentions of demonstrators took place at the larger protests.
Later that year, the [RULING PARTY] announced that it would end a three-year boycott of the parliament, and that its legislators would take their seats. Analysts suggested that the move was a sign that the president might seek to win control of the body by calling for new congressional elections. The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]-controlled parallel legislative body continued to function as a parallel legislative body serving to empower the president.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

[COUNTRY]'s independent journalists operate within a highly restrictive regulatory and legal environment, and risk arrest and physical violence in connection with their work. By the end of that year, most of the country's independent newspapers had shut down or moved to an exclusively digital format, where they are subject to frequent blocking, often at times of heightened political tension. (Journalists have pointed to the government's control over newsprint as hastening the decline of print publications in the country.)
Restrictions on online news outlets are mainly implemented by the state-run telephone and internet service provider that owns most communications infrastructure, but also by private internet service providers (ISPs). Independent media outlets such as [NGO] are among those recently subject to blocking.
Obstruction, intimidation, physical attacks, confiscations of equipment, and detentions and arrests of media workers continued. There were more than 35 arbitrary detentions of journalists in the first two months alone, according to the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. Among those arrested was a journalist, arrested for “instigation to commit crimes” in an investigation related to severe blackouts the country suffered. The journalist was released under the condition to appear before court every eight days.
Legislation passed that year mandates fines and up to 20 years’ imprisonment for anyone who disseminates information deemed “intolerant” via traditional or social media. The law also allows authorities greater legal grounds to block digital content deemed to be in violation.
The government maintains a state communications infrastructure used to propagate its political and ideological program. By that time, the president had made numerous televised appearances, accumulating more than [POPULATION FIGURE] since taking office. (In contrast, live-streamed speeches by the opposition leader were frequently blocked.)
Constitutional guarantees of religious freedom are generally respected, though tensions between the government and a religious minority group remain high. Government relations with another religious minority group have also been strained at times.
Academic freedom has come under mounting pressure for many years. A school curriculum developed by the [RULING PARTY] government emphasizes socialist concepts. Budget cuts and other funding problems have undermined universities’ autonomy and prompted an exodus of academics from the country. The number of students abandoning their studies has increased in turn. The government refused to transfer funds to pay the teachers of public universities if their institutions failed to formally recognize the president.
In recent years, the supreme court suspended the head of a major university, changed the voting rules, and ordered elections at several public universities considered [OPPOSITION PARTY] strongholds. Under the directive, if elections are not held within six months of the decision, the court will choose the new authorities. The same period, the president assigned a close ally to lead the cabinet bureau responsible for university education; the ally was tasked with putting the education system “at the service of the greater interests of the nation.”
Students and faculty perceived as holding antigovernment views face harassment and attacks. When the government started to collect signatures against foreign intervention and in support of the [RULING PARTY], a group of students were threatened with expulsion if they failed to sign. A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] reported at least 15 attacks, including threats and instances of physical assault, against students and professors who sought to defend human rights at their institutions. The body of a student was found after the student had denounced corruption at a university, where many faculty members are government supporters. A [NGO] reported that the body showed skull trauma and a nasal septum fracture, and some teeth were missing.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

The [COUNTRY]’s currency controls and other economic policies, combined with an enormous decline in the number of flights to and from [COUNTRY], and periodic border closures, have made it extremely difficult for citizens to travel abroad. Nevertheless, citizens of all social classes continued to flee the [COUNTRY] in massive numbers in recent years. At one point, the UN Refugee Agency and the International Organization for Migration announced that the total number of emigrants had surpassed [POPULATION FIGURE], with [POPULATION FIGURE] of those having left over the course of several years.
Property rights have been affected by years of price controls, nationalizations, overregulation, and corruption. Accusations of mismanagement, underinvestment, graft, and politicized hiring practices within state-owned enterprises are common.
Individuals enjoy broad freedom in their interpersonal and romantic relationships and in their personal appearance.
The politically driven economic collapse has reduced the availability of reproductive health care. Maternal and infant mortality has increased due to a lack of skilled birth attendants and medical supplies, and to poor hospital conditions. While data on the matter is limited, a contraceptives shortage is likely related to an increase in pregnancies among young women and teenage girls. Due to restrictive legislation on abortion, many women and girls resort to clandestine abortions that are frequently unsanitary and unsafe. Those with means must travel abroad to access legal and safe abortions.
Women relatives of political prisoners are subjected to sexual and gender-based violence and humiliation during visits in detention centers, security operations, and house raids. Women who have been political prisoners have referred to attacks by the security forces including sexual violence, threats of rape, and forced nudity.
Legislation passed to combat violence against women has been in place for many years, but domestic violence and rape remain common and are rarely punished in practice. Same-sex marriage still is not legal in the [COUNTRY].

