*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a democracy that regularly holds competitive elections and upholds civil liberties. Challenges include official corruption and a lack of government transparency, discrimination against lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, and related communities, violent crime, and poverty.

## Key Developments

The parliament altered the penal code to eliminate the mandatory death penalty for murder, which a ruling by the supreme court had declared unconstitutional. However, the attorney general indicated his ongoing support for capital punishment, which remains legal.
After a spate of homicides and other nonfatal violence, the prime minister augmented police patrols with up to 80 soldiers and increased surveillance. Forty-nine people were murdered that year, up from 28 the previous year.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The prime minister and members of the parliament are largely unimpeded in their ability to craft and implement policy, notwithstanding the powerful role played by labor unions and the demands of international creditors.
[COUNTRY]'s government has failed to implement key anticorruption measures. [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s, business figures, and the attorney general have complained of serious incidences of corruption, but no major officials have faced arrest under the current administration. Potential whistleblowers fear costly defamation suits.
The [legislation aimed at strengthening protections for whistleblowers]—which would strengthen protections for whistleblowers, require members of the parliament to declare their personal wealth, and create a new anticorruption investigative unit—was unveiled, but is yet to be made law. The bill was brought to the parliament for debate after a significant delay.
[COUNTRY] is one of just a few countries in the region to have neither signed nor ratified a key regional convention on mutual assistance in criminal matters. [COUNTRY] is also yet to ratify a major international convention against corruption, having signed the treaty many years ago, despite promises by the prime minister to do so recently. However, [COUNTRY] ratified a regional convention against corruption, having signed it for many years.
In [NGO]'s Global Corruption Barometer published recently, perceived corruption was relatively low, with 37 percent of respondents believing that corruption had risen in the past year, and 55 percent believing that the current administration is doing a good job in fighting corruption.
The government largely operates with transparency, however, [COUNTRY] lacks key laws to ensure this openness persists. Notably, a long-promised freedom of information act and a measure that would require public officials to disclose income and assets have yet to be enacted. Information on the country's national budget is difficult to obtain.
The newly elected [RULING PARTY] government initially demonstrated an inclination towards greater transparency and scrutiny by the press. The government's information service has dramatically improved the functionality and accuracy of its web portal, which contains information about government policies. Recently, the government revealed the full extent of the country's debt burden and financial liabilities.
However, the current administration has been criticized for not updating the parliament more actively about the country's foreign debt, its decision to default on external credit for an entire year, and for paying a significant amount to an advisory firm contracted to restructure [COUNTRY]'s debt.

