*State Department Human Rights Report*

Approximately 90 percent of the [COUNTRY]'s residents were noncitizens, more than half of whom originated from [REGION]. Societal discrimination against noncitizens was prevalent and occurred in most areas of daily life, including employment, education, housing, social interaction, and health care.
The law allows for criminalizing commercial disputes and bankruptcy, which led to discrimination against foreigners. Authorities enforced these laws selectively and allowed citizens to threaten noncitizen businesspersons and foreign workers with harsh prison sentences to assure a favorable outcome in commercial disputes.

Acts of Violence, Discrimination, and Other Abuses Based on Sexual Orientation and Gender Identity

Both civil law and [LEGAL CODE] criminalize consensual same-sex sexual activity. Under [LEGAL CODE] individuals who engage in consensual same-sex sexual conduct could be subject to the death penalty. The penal code of a major city in the [COUNTRY] allows for up to a 10-year prison sentence for conviction of such activity, while the penal code of the capital allows for up to a 14-year prison sentence. There were no reports of arrests or prosecutions for consensual same-sex activity.
The law does not extend antidiscrimination protections to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individuals on the basis of their sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or sex characteristics. There were no government efforts to address potential discrimination.
By law wearing clothing deemed inappropriate for one’s sex is a punishable offense. The government deported foreign residents and referred the cases of individuals who wore clothing deemed inappropriate to the public prosecutor.
The law permits doctors to conduct sex reassignment surgery when there are “psychological” and “physiological” signs of gender and sex disparity. The penalty for performing an unwarranted “sex correction” surgery is three to 10 years in prison. In recent years, the supreme court denied a request for legal gender recognition by three local transgender persons who sought legally to change their names and update their gender on official documents. The appeals court upheld the lower court’s ruling, and the supreme court rejected their final appeal.
Due to social conventions and potential repression, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] organizations did not operate openly, nor were pride marches or rights advocacy events held.
There were reports of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] persons being questioned in national airports on the basis of appearance and behavior.

---

The constitution designates [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith as the official religion. It guarantees freedom of worship as long as it does not conflict with public policy or morals. It states all persons are equal before the law and prohibits discrimination on grounds of religious belief. The law prohibits blasphemy, proselytizing by non-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individuals, and conversion from [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith. An antidiscrimination law includes prohibitions on religious discrimination and criminalizes acts the government interprets as provoking religious hatred or insulting religions. Local press reported that a court in the capital convicted a foreign national of blasphemy and sentenced them to three months imprisonment followed by deportation and a fine of [POPULATION FIGURE] units of currency. In recent years, authorities in a major city charged two residents with engaging in extramarital sex, in violation of local interpretation of religious law. A woman initially convicted of charges related to practicing witchcraft was acquitted after appeal in a major city. The government's religious authority continued to provide weekly guidance for the content of sermons in [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] mosques. Some [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] imams chose to follow the government's approved guidance, while a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]-based council, charged with management of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] affairs, issued additional instructions to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] mosques. Christian churches and [ETHNIC GROUP] temples serving the noncitizen population operated on land donated by the ruling family. The government granted licenses, and thereby formal legal status, to several houses of worship, including Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant churches and the country's first traditional [ETHNIC GROUP] temple. Individuals belonging to non-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] faiths otherwise reported they could worship in private without government interference but faced some restrictions on practicing their religion in public. Government-controlled internet service providers blocked access to websites critical of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith or supportive of views the government considered religiously extremist. The government prohibited the dissemination of literature it perceived as supporting religious extremism. During the year, construction was underway on multiple houses of worship. Regulatory requirements sometimes limited the ability of religious organizations to rent space for worship and limited certain charitable activities. The government announced construction of the first official synagogue, with construction slated to begin soon. The head of a major [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] held a public ceremony in the capital for a large group of followers as part of a historic visit. The government hosted conferences and meetings with religious minority leaders throughout the year to promote interfaith tolerance both domestically and internationally.

According to non-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] religious community representatives, there was a high degree of societal tolerance for minority religious beliefs and traditions, particularly for those associated with officially recognized houses of worship, although conversion from [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith was strongly discouraged. Conversion to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith was encouraged, however. A government-backed [NGO] posted a video online featuring new converts to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith and the faith's role in promoting tolerance and forgiveness. Local newspapers published stories portraying conversions to [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith positively. In some cases, organizations reported that hotels, citing government regulatory barriers, were unwilling to rent space for non-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] religious purposes, such as weekly church services. Local media reported on difficulties in obtaining bank loans to cover construction costs for new religious spaces, including for registered religious organizations.

The [COUNTRY]'s ambassador at large for religious freedom spoke at a conference in the capital on the subject of interfaith tolerance and education. The ambassador also met with local officials, including the interior minister and other senior government counterparts. In meetings with senior government counterparts, the ambassador, other embassy and consulate general officers, and visiting officials reviewed ways to promote respect among faith groups and freedom for minority groups to practice their religions, as well as government initiatives to foster religious tolerance and counter what it considered extremist interpretations of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]'s dominant faith. Embassy and consulate general officials also engaged with a broad range of minority religious groups. The embassy and consulate general hosted interfaith events to encourage and support religious freedom and tolerance.

