*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY] is a federation of several emirates led in practice by the capital, the largest by area and richest in natural resources. Limited elections are held for a federal advisory body, but political parties are banned, and all executive, legislative, and judicial authority ultimately rests with the hereditary rulers. The civil liberties of both citizens and noncitizens, who make up an overwhelming majority of the [POPULATION FIGURE], are subject to significant restrictions.

## Key Developments

A major religious leader undertook a historic visit to [COUNTRY] and the region, as government officials sought to burnish the country’s reputation for religious tolerance.
In recent years, media circulated video images of the president meeting well-wishers during a major religious holiday. The president had rarely appeared in public since suffering a serious health issue several years prior; their relative and designated heir remained the country’s de facto ruler.
Nonpartisan elections for half of the seats on the advisory council were held, featuring a somewhat expanded pool of eligible voters and greater participation by women candidates, though turnout remained low.

---

## G Personal Autonomy and Individual Rights

[COUNTRY] nationals face no apparent restrictions on freedom of movement within [COUNTRY] or on their ability to change their place of employment, but under the country’s sponsorship system, migrant workers’ legal status is tied to their employers’ sponsorship, meaning they can be punished or deported for leaving employment without meeting certain criteria. Stateless residents’ freedom of movement is limited by their lack of travel documents; under a government program, many stateless people have received passports from a foreign country that ease travel and other activities but do not confer full citizenship. Societal norms sometimes restrict a woman’s ability to travel without the consent of her husband or father. Nationals of a neighboring country have been barred from [COUNTRY] for several years.
[COUNTRY] has enacted reforms in recent years to ease procedures for establishing and operating businesses. However, the government and ruling families exercise considerable influence over the economy and are involved in many of [COUNTRY]’s major economic and commercial initiatives, limiting the space for genuinely private business activity.
Women generally receive smaller inheritances than men under [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] law, and women are excluded from state benefits aimed at supporting home ownership.
[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] women are forbidden to marry non-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] men, while [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] men may marry women from other religious groups. Women are generally at a distinct disadvantage under laws governing marriage and divorce. All sexual relations outside legal marriage are criminal offenses, which deters victims from reporting rape. No laws protect against spousal rape or domestic violence.
Foreign workers are often exploited and subjected to harsh working conditions, physical abuse, and withholding of passports with little to no access to legal recourse. A series of ministerial decrees issued that year aimed to give migrant workers more flexibility to terminate employment under certain conditions. Foreign household workers were not covered by those decrees or by labor laws in general, leaving them especially vulnerable. Legislation passed that year guaranteed such household workers basic protections and benefits including sick leave and daily rest periods, though they were inferior to those in the national labor law, and household workers would still be unable to leave their employers without a breach of contract.
A competitive rivalry between the capital and a major city for eye-catching development projects masks deeper sensitivities in relations between these two regions and the less affluent areas. Economic disparities also persist among [COUNTRY] citizens across the country and between citizens and the noncitizen majority.

