## B Political Pluralism and Participation

Political parties are banned, and all electoral candidates run as independents.
For several years, the government has aggressively cracked down on opposition activists, particularly if they are suspected of belonging to a group that advocates for democratic reform. The government has accused members of this group of being foreign agents of a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group intent on overthrowing the regime, and designated this group as a terrorist organization. A neighboring country's support for this [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group has been a factor in efforts by [COUNTRY] and its regional allies to isolate that country in recent years. Dozens of activists, [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] leaders, academics, and students remained imprisoned as part of the broader crackdown.
The political system grants the hereditary rulers a monopoly on power and excludes the possibility of a change in government through elections.
The political choices available to eligible voters are severely limited in practice, and the alignments of both voters and candidates are heavily influenced by tribal networks.
Approximately 90 percent of the [POPULATION FIGURE] consists of noncitizens who lack political rights and electoral opportunities, including thousands of stateless residents. There is no clear process for obtaining citizenship without [COUNTRY] parentage or marriage to a [COUNTRY] man; children of [COUNTRY] mothers and foreign fathers must apply for naturalization.
Women make up about 50 percent of the electoral college, and approximately 180 women ran as candidates in recent elections, more than double the number who ran a few years prior. Women won several of the elected seats on the council, and the authorities appointed a number of women in keeping with a pledge to ensure equal representation in the council. In practice, however, ordinary women have little opportunity to organize independently and advance their interests through the political system.

