*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The law provides for freedom of expression, including for the press. Nonetheless, the government’s interpretation and implementation of the law and other provisions of the constitution limited freedom of speech and expression, and restricted access to the internet as described below.
Freedom of Expression : Although the law provides for freedom of speech, under the law the government may limit the expression of ideas that promote or incite the activities of “antistate” individuals or groups. During the year, prosecutions under the law for speech that allegedly supported or praised [COUNTRY]'s government continued. Two persons were charged under the law for praising or supporting [COUNTRY]'s government from January to July. There were nine such cases in recent years and one in the preceding year.
[NGO] contended the government maintained “unreasonable restrictions on freedom of expression,” citing the use of defamation laws, the law, and other laws.
In August a district court upheld a professor’s six-month prison sentence for defamation after he told his class that some women “probably knew exactly what they were signing up for” when they “volunteered” to be comfort women (women subjected to sexual servitude for [COUNTRY]'s military during a major conflict). The court also upheld [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]'s decision to fire him. The professor said he did not intend to defame the women but was trying to provoke an academic discussion of the historical issue in his class.
Under the election law, the government may limit the expression of ideas that the election commission deems to be false.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views, within the constraints cited above.
In March the spokesperson of the [RULING PARTY] criticized a journalist for her article that called the president the “top spokesman” of [COUNTRY]. The spokesperson also called out another journalist the following day for expressing a similar opinion. The spokesperson later apologized and had the journalists’ names removed from transcripts of his statements.
[NGO] expressed concerns about criminal libel and national security laws that invoke severe penalties for the dissemination of sensitive information, especially when it involves [COUNTRY]. Conservative politicians complained that the government placed political pressure on media outlets.
Libel /Slander Laws : The government and individual public figures used libel and slander laws, which broadly define and criminalize defamation, to restrict public discussion and harass, intimidate, or censor private and media expression. The law allows punishment of up to three years in prison for statements found to be “slander” or “libel,” even if factual, and up to seven years for statements found to be false. The law punishes defamation of deceased persons as well; the maximum punishment if convicted is two years’ imprisonment. [NGO] and human rights attorneys noted several cases of politicians, government officials, and celebrities using the libel laws to deter victims of workplace sexual harassment from coming forward or to retaliate against such victims. In January a film director asked prosecutors to investigate journalists under the nation’s defamation laws for reporting allegations that he sexually and physically abused actresses working under his direction. Prosecutors ultimately rejected the director’s request. Subsequently, the director filed a civil libel suit seeking damages from a news agency and one of the actresses. As of September, that case had not been resolved.
National Security : The law criminalizes actions interpreted to be in support of [COUNTRY] or otherwise against the state. The government used this law to arrest and imprison civilians and to deport foreigners. The supreme court ruled the law constitutional for many years.
In July a district court overruled the conviction of a migrant for recruiting individuals to join [MILITANT GROUP]. The man had been living in the country for more than 10 years on a temporary humanitarian stay permit after the government denied his asylum application. According to a local [NGO], when he traveled to the region for the birth of his child, investigators assumed he was meeting with [MILITANT GROUP]. Prosecutors accused him of having [MILITANT GROUP] recruitment material on his phone; the man said the material automatically downloaded from his social media feed. The district court found that the prosecutors failed to prove that the defendant encouraged others to join [MILITANT GROUP] or proposed a way to join the group. Nevertheless, the court rejected his request to determine the constitutionality of the law. Prosecutors appealed the decision to overturn the verdict and the case was pending.

Internet Freedom

There were some government restrictions on internet access, and the government monitored email and internet chat rooms with wide legal authority.
The communications standards commission, a government body, blocked [POPULATION FIGURE] websites it deemed harmful from January to September. The vast majority of blocked sites involved gambling, illegal food or drugs, and pornography. The commission also blocked [COUNTRY] propaganda on social media platforms. Although viewing websites praising [COUNTRY]'s regime is lawful, disseminating information about those websites, including posting links to those sites, is illegal under the law. Other blocked sites included those promoting illegal trade of internal organs, forgery of documents, violating intellectual property rights, or encouraging suicide.
The commission determines whether posts made on social networking sites or in chat rooms contain unlawful content, defined as harmful or illegal speech. If the government finds prohibited materials, it has the authority to warn the user. If the prohibited content is not removed, the user’s account may be blocked.
Although persons may use a false name when making online postings to large websites, the election campaign law requires real names for internet postings about upcoming elections.
[NGO] assessed the country’s media as generally free and competitive.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

Teachers are subject to the same law on political activities that applies to civil servants. The ministry of education monitors song lyrics and may ban content it considers obscene. The communications standards commission governs and maintains ethical standards in broadcasting and internet communications.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]’s democratic system features regular rotations of power and robust political pluralism, with the largest parties representing conservative and liberal views. Personal freedoms are generally respected, though the country struggles with minority rights and social integration. Legal bans on pro–[ARMED GROUP] activity affects legitimate political expression. Members of the press can also come under undue scrutiny by the government as they report on, or commentate on, relations with neighboring entities. [COUNTRY] is also affected by severe corruption, which has ensnared successive governments and executives from the country’s largest companies in recent years.

## Key Developments

Investigations into the corruption of high-ranking government officials, judges, and business executives continued. The president’s justice minister was indicted on charges including bribery, embezzlement, and document fraud, after being forced from their post. A former president and a major company’s vice-chairman faced renewed trials over acts of corruption and collusion, after they were respectively convicted in previous years. A former chief justice was put on trial for manipulating high-profile cases to curry favor with the former president.
The government renewed its campaign against “fake news,” using the country’s media regulator, police, and prosecutors to pressure journalists and commentators who criticize its policies. The country’s internet censor also gained new powers to more closely track [COUNTRY] citizens who visit secure web pages.
[COUNTRY] returned two [ARMED GROUP] fishermen who requested resettlement after they were accused of killing fellow crew members before traveling to [COUNTRY], despite fears that they would be tortured upon their return. They were the first defectors to be returned to their home entity since a major conflict ended decades ago.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The news media are generally free and competitive, reporting aggressively on government policies and allegations of official and corporate wrongdoing. However, a defamation law authorizes sentences of up to seven years in prison, encouraging a certain degree of self-censorship, and journalists at major news outlets have faced political interference from managers and the government.
News coverage or commentary that is deemed to favor [ARMED GROUP] can be censored and lead to prosecution under the National Security Law, and access to [ARMED GROUP] media is banned. The government also exerts pressure on journalists covering relations with [ARMED GROUP] outside of these strict laws. In recent years, a spokesperson for the [RULING PARTY] accused a journalist of authoring “traitorous content,” after she wrote an article portraying the president as a “spokesperson” for the leader of [ARMED GROUP]. That same period, the spokesperson used similar language to criticize another journalist, after she authored similar content. The [RULING PARTY] retracted those statements after receiving fierce criticism from the foreign correspondents’ club in the capital.
The government has continued its fight against what it calls “fake news”. The prime minister originally ordered police forces, prosecutors, and the media regulator to investigate and punish those who spread it, saying practitioners were risking the country’s national security. In practice, the government has reacted most strongly to public criticism of its policies; the prime minister’s instructions came after he was derided by conservative commentators for attending a funeral of a foreign leader. The security forces subsequently investigated false stories that appeared on social media platforms, some of which originated from right-wing outlets or users.
The president reiterated his commitment to this policy after receiving online criticism over the country’s ongoing trade dispute with a neighboring country and his policy towards [ARMED GROUP]. The media regulator reiterated the same stance, saying that the need to halt fake news outweighed the risk of violating freedom of expression.
Freedom of religion is guaranteed by the constitution and generally respected in practice. However, the military conscription system made no allowances for conscientious objection, and hundreds of men—nearly all of them members of a religious minority—were imprisoned each year for refusing military service. For several years, the supreme court ruled that the government must provide alternative forms of service. Later, the supreme court acquitted a man who refused military service due to his religious beliefs.
After those rulings, the justice ministry began offering parole to conscientious objectors; the final objector was paroled recently. The parliament passed legislation allowing conscientious objectors to perform alternative service at correctional facilities for 36 months.
Academic freedom is mostly unrestricted, though the National Security Law limits statements supporting [ARMED GROUP]. An anticorruption law subjects teachers and administrators to the same tight restrictions as public officials. Certain portrayals of sensitive historical issues—such as the wartime sexual enslavement of women from [ETHNIC GROUP]—can be subject to government censorship or prosecution under the country’s defamation laws.
Recently, a professor portrayed victims of wartime sexual enslavement as voluntary sex workers in a lecture, and was suspended after his remarks were reported by the press. The professor also resigned his membership from [OPPOSITION PARTY] after its ethics committee prepared to discipline him. He became the target of a police probe over his remarks.
Private discussion is typically free and open, and the government generally respects citizens’ right to privacy. However, the government also maintains a robust surveillance apparatus.
An antiterrorism law granted the intelligence service expanded authority to monitor private communications, and the measure’s vague definition of “terrorism” raised concerns that it would enable the agency to track government critics, particularly online. The security forces, prosecutors, and investigative agencies can also access metadata without a warrant; this includes internet users’ national ID numbers, postal addresses, and telephone numbers.
The government also uses invasive information-gathering techniques when considering the requests of conscientious objectors; recently, prosecutors announced they were examining the phone records of conscientious objectors to see if they had played “online shooting games,” which would have undermined their claims. It remains unclear how prosecutors gained access to those records.
The government body that monitors online content is another part of this apparatus; it evaluates online content found by its staff, along with material referred to it by other government agencies, and censors such content according to vaguely defined standards. The body’s abilities were bolstered recently, when it began employing Server Name Indication (SNI) filtering to closely monitor user access of secure web pages.
The National Security Law restricts speech that is considered pro-[ARMED GROUP]. However, the law was not strictly enforced amid the diplomatic process with [ARMED GROUP] in recent years; concerns about potential constraints on free expression shifted to those who opposed or could complicate rapprochement with [ARMED GROUP], including defectors and human rights activists from [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP].

