*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The Interim National Constitution provided for freedom of expression, including freedom for the press “as regulated by law,” but the former regime heavily restricted this right. The constitutional declaration provides for the unrestricted right of freedom of expression and for freedom of the press as regulated by law, and the transitional government reportedly respected these rights.
Freedom of Expression : Individuals who criticized the former regime publicly or privately were subject to reprisal, including arbitrary arrest. The former regime attempted to impede such criticism and monitored political meetings and the press. There were no reports of this occurring under the transitional government.
According to the [NGO], between late [TIME PERIOD] and mid-[TIME PERIOD], the former regime arrested 90 journalists. All journalists have been released.
The former regime also curtailed public religious discussion if proselytization was suspected and monitored religious sermons and teachings (see the [GOVERNMENT BODY]'s International Religious Freedom Report).
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The Interim National Constitution provided for freedom of the press, but the former regime prevented media from reporting on issues they deemed sensitive. From [TIME PERIOD] through [TIME PERIOD], the former regime restricted coverage of the protests, resulting in the arrest of numerous journalists and near-daily confiscations of entire newspaper print runs. The security forces declared news of the protests a “red line” topic and increased precensoring of newspapers to prevent publication of newspapers reporting on the protests. Journalists responded by staging peaceful demonstrations, and several newspapers ceased operations in protest against the escalating censorship. The former regime attempted to control reporting by staging pro-regime demonstrations and planting bogus news stories that blamed civil unrest on [ARMED GROUP].
The former regime influenced radio and television reporting through the permit process as well as by offering or withholding regime payments for advertisements, based on how closely affiliated media outlets were with the regime.
The former regime controlled media through the [GOVERNMENT BODY], which administered mandatory professional examinations for journalists and oversaw the selection of editors. The [GOVERNMENT BODY] had authority to ban journalists temporarily or indefinitely. The registration of journalists was handled primarily by the [NGO], which estimated there were [POPULATION FIGURE] registered journalists in the country, although fewer than 200 of them were believed to be actively employed as journalists. The remainder were members of the former regime and security forces working on media issues who received automatic licenses.
The former regime arbitrarily arrested journalists, detaining them and holding them incommunicado, sometimes for weeks.
The transitional government reportedly respected press and media freedoms.
Violence and Harassment : The former regime arrested, harassed, intimidated, and abused journalists and vocal critics of the regime. The security forces required journalists to provide personal information, such as details on their ethnic group, political affiliation, and family. On [DATE], the security forces stormed the office of the international news channel in the capital and arrested correspondents. The security forces subjected them to verbal and physical abuse before releasing them the next day. There were no reports of the transitional government using these tactics.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : The former regime practiced direct prepublication and prebroadcast censorship of all forms of media. Confiscations of print runs was the censorship method most frequently used by the security forces. This was an incentive to self-censorship. There were no reports of government censorship or print confiscations under the transitional government.
Former regime authorities used the [COURT], which specialized in media issues and “newspaper irregularities” and established under the [LEGISLATION], to prosecute “information crimes.”
Following the protests that began in [TIME PERIOD] and continued throughout the first few months of the year, media censorship under the former regime tightened, resulting in the arrests of several journalists and near-daily confiscations of entire newspaper print runs. The security forces declared news on the protests a “red line” topic and then precensored newspapers to stop the publication of news on the protests. For example, on [DATE], the security forces forced editors of a newspaper to remove a columnist’s articles from the newspaper and remove her name and photo from all locations on the newspaper’s website due to her critical reports on the former regime. The security forces refused to allow the newspaper to refer to the column as “banned.”
Libel/Slander Laws : The law holds editors in chief potentially criminally liable for all content published in their newspapers.
National Security : Under the former regime, the law allowed for restrictions on the press in the interests of national security and public order. It contained loosely defined provisions for bans on encouraging ethnic and religious disturbances and incitement of violence. The criminal code, [LEGISLATION], and emergency laws were regularly used to bring charges against the press. Human rights activists called the law a “punishment” for journalists.
Under the former regime, the security forces initiated legal action against journalists for stories critical of the former regime and security services.
Actions to Expand Freedom of Expression, including for the Media : The constitutional declaration provides for freedom of expression and the media, and the transitional government took measures to respect these rights.
At the [INTERNATIONAL BODY] on [DATE], the prime minister underscored, “Never again in the new [COUNTRY] will a journalist be repressed or jailed.” He also declared, “A free press is an important pillar in promoting democracy, good governance, and human rights.”
The transitional government extended entry to foreign journalists, including the return of an international news channel, which had been banned earlier in the year. Foreign journalists from several international news channels have returned to the country.

Internet Freedom

The former regime and the security forces restricted and disrupted access to the internet and censored online content, and there were credible reports the former regime and the security forces monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority. The transitional government generally respected internet freedom.
The former regime regulated licensing of telecommunications companies through the [GOVERNMENT BODY]. The agency blocked some websites and most proxy servers judged offensive to public morality, such as those purveying pornography. The security forces shut down internet access on [DATE], the same day security forces violently dispersed peaceful demonstrators from the sit-in in front of the [GOVERNMENT BUILDING]. Internet service to the country was restored on [DATE].
[NGO] continued to rank the country as “not free” in its annual internet freedom report. According to the report, arrests and prosecutions under the [LEGISLATION] grew during the year, reflecting a tactical shift in the government’s strategy to limit internet freedom. The report noted many journalists writing for online platforms published anonymously to avoid prosecution, while ordinary internet users in the country had become more inclined to self-censor to avoid government surveillance and arbitrary legal consequences.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

The former regime restricted academic freedom, determined the curricula, and appointed vice chancellors responsible for administration at academic and cultural institutions. The former regime continued to arrest student activists and cancel or deny permits for some student events. Youth activists reported some universities discouraged students from participating in antigovernment rallies and gave [RULING PARTY] students preferential treatment. Some professors exercised self-censorship. In [TIME PERIOD] the former regime arrested at least nine prominent academics, many from a major university, for criticizing the regime. On [DATE], the former regime closed all public and private universities in the country to suppress political criticism. In [TIME PERIOD] the security forces ordered universities to reopen, but not all did. After the transitional government was established, however, all major universities reopened.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

The military leaders and civilian protesters who ousted the repressive regime of the former president and his [RULING PARTY] in recent years are uneasy partners in a transitional government that—if successful—will be replaced by an elected government in the near future. Civic space is slowly opening to individuals and [OPPOSITION PARTY] groups, but security personnel associated with the abuses of the old regime remain influential, and their commitment to political freedoms and civil liberties is unclear.

## Key Developments

The president, who came to power in a coup d’état for many years, was overthrown by the military after a protest movement placed growing pressure on the government. The military initially attempted to rule without the input of civilian protesters, who originally demonstrated against rising commodity prices and pervasive economic hardship before calling for the president’s resignation. 
Security forces killed 127 protesters in the capital in a major crackdown, sparking a backlash that forced the short-lived junta to include civilian leaders in a new transitional government as part of a power-sharing agreement reached later that year.
The former president was arrested by the military junta and charged with corruption by the succeeding transitional government. The former president was convicted and was sentenced to a prison term. As the year came to a close, prosecutors also questioned the former president over their role in the coup that brought them to power. The transitional government remained undecided on whether to surrender the former president to the international court, which had charged them with crimes against humanity, war crimes, and genocide over their government’s efforts to quell a rebellion in a region with a [POPULATION FIGURE] population, which has been affected by conflict for several years.
The transitional government made progress in resolving conflicts in border regions, which have collectively displaced nearly two million people from [COUNTRY]. By the end of the year, the government had secured peace deals with [NUMBER] [REBEL GROUP]s, and committed to a roadmap to end the conflict in the region.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The interim constitution agreed recently guarantees freedom of the press, but the government of [COUNTRY] has historically been hostile to journalists. When a major protest began, national newspapers were repeatedly closed, and journalists were detained without charge. The security forces interrogated a journalist at a major newspaper after antigovernment material was posted on its Facebook page. The ruling authorities were similarly repressive during their time in power, closing the [COUNTRY] bureau of a major international news organization; it was reopened later. The ruling authorities also detained the president of the [NGO] and editor in chief of a major newspaper for several days; no reason was given for the arrest.
The current government has refrained from the most aggressive tactics used by the previous government, though journalists have criticized its activities. The [NGO] and the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] condemned the government’s decision to close trade union organizations associated with the old regime, including the [NGO], recently.
The [ARMED GROUP] has remained active in its targeting of journalists since the rise of the current government. Recently, it filed a complaint against the editor in chief of a major newspaper over her column. The editor in chief was subsequently charged with violating legislation related to information crimes.

