*State Department Human Rights Report*

f. Arbitrary or Unlawful Interference with Privacy, Family, Home, or Correspondence

The constitution prohibits such actions, and there were no reports the government failed to respect these prohibitions.
The federal and state offices for the protection of the constitution continued to monitor political groups deemed to be potentially undermining the constitution, including left-wing extremist groups inside [OPPOSITION PARTY], which has seats in the parliament, and the right-wing extremist [OPPOSITION PARTY]. Monitoring requires the approval of state or federal interior ministries and is subject to review by state or federal parliamentary intelligence committees.
In recent years the office for the protection of the constitution announced it would monitor a loose network within the far-right [OPPOSITION PARTY] because it aims “at the exclusion, disparagement, and extensive deprivation of rights” of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and “violates the guarantee of human dignity as well as the principles of democracy and the rule of law.” Additionally, the office asserted that members of the network have links with members of extremist organizations.
All office activities may be contested in court, including the supreme court. Following a ruling by the supreme court, the government stated the office would no longer monitor members of the parliament.
The education ministry of a major city confirmed that it had ordered a local high school to remove left-wing extremist paraphernalia based on a complaint through the [OPPOSITION PARTY]’s reporting tool.

---

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

Freedom of Expression : While the government generally respected these rights, it imposed limits on groups it deemed extremist. The government arrested, tried, convicted, and imprisoned a number of individuals for speech that incited racial hatred, endorsed extremist ideologies, or denied historical atrocities (see also section 6, Anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] Sentiment).
In recent years, a major social media platform announced it had removed a large number of “fake profiles” after a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] identified them as sources of targeted misinformation. A [RULING PARTY] politician claimed her posts had been deleted or blocked in the past, which deprived her of her freedom of expression.
The government of a major state approved a law that makes it illegal for judges and state prosecutors to wear religious symbols openly during public trials. This includes headscarves, crosses, and kippas. Similar laws already existed in several other states, while others imposed more vague limits on religious attire for judges and state prosecutors.
A host of a left-leaning political TV program on a public broadcaster received a death threat by mail after he made critical comments about the [RULING PARTY] on a specific date. The broadcaster has filed charges against the unknown perpetrator, and several journalists expressed solidarity with the host in an ad in a local newspaper. After the threat, the host requested stronger protection for freedom of speech and press. The threatening letter appeared to have the same author as similar letters sent to the mayor of the capital and to another mayor. The [security chief] assumed that an individual with an extremist background was responsible. The police were investigating.
Press and Media, including Online Media : The constitution provides for freedom of expression, including for the press, and the government generally respected this right. An independent press, an effective judiciary, and a functioning democratic political system combined to promote freedom of expression, including for the press. The law bans extremist propaganda, denial of historical atrocities, and fomenting racial hatred.
Violence and Harassment : During a demonstration of a far-right movement in a major city, a journalist from a local daily was threatened by protesters. Instead of defending the journalist’s right to cover the demonstration, police forced him to delete his pictures and afterwards expelled him from the demonstration site. Later, police released a statement saying it was a “misunderstanding.” The movement is a right-wing organization which the [intelligence service] monitors to evaluate whether it should be banned.
In recent years, representatives of an anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] movement and the [RULING PARTY] protested the president's visit to a major city. A demonstrator (an off-duty police employee) claimed privacy laws prohibited a camera team from filming him, and he filed a complaint with police on the spot. Police held the camera team for 45 minutes, reportedly to verify their identities. The president issued a statement in support of press freedom and noted that demonstrators should expect they may be filmed. The police commissioner apologized to the journalists, and the police employee was transferred to a different position. The employee later sued the media outlet for violating media law and his personal rights. The case was ongoing.

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content, with one notable exception, and there were no credible reports that the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority. The exception is that the law permits the government to take down websites that belong to banned organizations or include speech that incites racial hatred, endorses extremist ideologies, or denies historical atrocities. Authorities worked directly with internet service providers and online media companies to monitor and remove such content. As of recent years, authorities monitored several hundred websites and social media accounts associated with extremist groups.
In recent years, the interior minister announced the creation of a central office for severe cases of politically motivated hate speech on the Internet, such as death threats against politicians on social media.
For several years, the government has been working on a project to combat hate speech on the internet. Through recent years, it received a number of offense reports, leading to investigation procedures and the identification of defendants. Other contributors to the initiative include the [interior ministry], the police, and media outlets.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were government restrictions on academic freedom and cultural events supporting extreme right-wing ideologies.

