*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution provides the majority of citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage. Certain regions have political systems that differ from the rest of [COUNTRY], and neither have representation in the national parliament.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : In recent years, [COUNTRY] held direct elections that resulted in a [RULING PARTY]-majority national government led by the prime minister. International observers assessed voting was “well-conducted and transparent” but noted “counting was sometimes problematic.” [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] and political parties raised concerns regarding pre-election interference, including restrictions on freedom of expression, allegedly creating an uneven electoral playing field.
Later that year, the Electoral College (made up of the members of both houses of the parliament, and of the provincial assemblies) held presidential elections and selected a [RULING PARTY] member to succeed the previous president of the [OPPOSITION PARTY]. Following the passage of legislation merging a former region with the rest of a province, the government held special elections. These elections gave residents of the former region representation in the provincial assembly for the first time in their history. Politically, the only remaining hurdle for full integration of the former region with the province is elections for local leaders.
Political Parties and Political Participation : There were no reports of restrictions on political parties participating in elections, except for those prohibited due to terrorist affiliations. Judges ordered media regulatory agencies to enforce constitutional bans on content critical of the security forces or the judiciary, compelling media to censor politicians’ speeches and elections-related coverage deemed “antijudiciary” or “antimilitary.” Organizations that monitor press freedom reported direct pressure on media outlets to avoid content regarding possible security force influence over judicial proceedings against politicians, and to refrain from reporting on [OPPOSITION PARTY] leaders in a positive way. In most areas there was no interference with the right of political parties and candidates to organize campaigns, run for election, or seek votes. In a major city, however, there were reports security agencies and separatist groups harassed local political organizations.
Participation of Women and Minorities : Legislation stipulates special measures to enhance electoral participation of women, religious minorities, transgender persons, and persons with disabilities. By law women must constitute a certain percentage of party tickets, and if less than a certain percentage of women vote in any constituency, authorities may presume that the women’s vote was suppressed, and the results for that constituency or polling station may be nullified. The government enforced the law for the first time in a region when the election commission canceled the district’s general election results after women made up less than the required percentage of the vote.
Cultural and traditional barriers in tribal and rural areas impeded some women from voting. Authorities used quotas to assure a minimum level of participation of women in elected bodies. There are a certain number of seats in the national assembly and a certain number of seats in the senate reserved for women. Authorities apportioned these seats based on total votes secured by the candidates of each political party that contested the elections. Women and minorities also may contest directly elected seats, but both women and minorities have struggled to be directly elected outside of the reserved seats. Authorities reserved a certain number of seats for women in provincial assemblies and a proportion of the seats on local councils. Women participated actively as political party members, but they were not always successful in securing leadership positions within parties, apart from women’s wings. Women served in the federal cabinet.
The law provides for mail-in voting for persons with disabilities. It requires expedited issuance of identification cards (which also serve as voter identification cards) for non-Muslims, transgender persons, and persons with disabilities.
The government requires voters to indicate their religion when registering to vote. A religious minority group is required to either swear a certain oath and denounce their movement’s founder, or declare themselves as non-Muslims, in order to vote. This group considers themselves Muslims, and many were unable to vote because they did not comply.

