*State Department Human Rights Report*

b. Freedoms of Peaceful Assembly and Association

The law provides for the freedoms of peaceful assembly and association, and the government of [COUNTRY] generally respected these rights.

---

The constitution guarantees freedom of conscience, religion, thought, belief, opinion, expression, and the right to equal protection and benefit of the law without discrimination based on religion. The government does not require religious groups to register, but some registered groups may receive tax-exempt status. The court of appeal ruled that a certain group did not qualify as a religion for purposes of obtaining charitable status. In recent years, the provincial government passed and implemented a law prohibiting certain categories of provincial government employees from wearing religious symbols while exercising their official functions, while requiring individuals seeking certain provincial government services to do so with the “face uncovered.” Observers said the legislation targeted [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] women and would also effectively exclude some [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individuals from positions of authority, including positions in the parliament, education, the courts, and law enforcement. [NGO], [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], and an individual plaintiff filed a legal challenge to the law in the superior court. A court dismissed the appeal of physicians who objected on religious and/or moral grounds to a provincial policy requiring them to refer patients for certain medical services. In conjunction with a new strategy addressing all forms of discrimination, including based on religion, the government adopted a definition of anti-Semitism. The court of appeal heard the appeal of a long-standing case concerning whether the province could fund non-[RELIGIOUS GROUP] students to attend [RELIGIOUS GROUP] schools. The appeal process continued.

Reports continued of anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and anti-[RELIGIOUS MINORITY] activity, including cases of violence, hate speech, harassment, discrimination, and vandalism. Statistics on hate crimes showed the number of police-reported religiously motivated hate crimes was approximately 24 percent lower than the previous year, dropping to a total of 639; reported crimes against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individuals decreased by 50 percent, while those against [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] individuals decreased by 4 percent. [NGO] reported in its annual audit that there were 11 cases of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] violence nationwide, 221 reports of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] vandalism, and 1809 occurrences of harassment, approximately 90 percent of which reportedly occurred online. [NGO] received a total of 2,041 reports of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] cases, compared with 1,752 reports in the previous year and 1,728 cases the year before that. A judge sentenced a man to a minimum term of 40 years after he pled guilty to multiple counts of murder for killing worshippers at a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] cultural centre. A taxi driver was arrested and charged with assaulting a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] man wearing a [RELIGIOUS SYMBOL], who reportedly wanted to take a photograph of the taxi to file a complaint about the taxi driver’s [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]-targeted comments. [NGO] released the results of a survey on [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]-targeted views of the [POPULATION FIGURE] adult population. It said 8 percent harbored [RELIGIOUS MINORITY]-targeted views, down from 14 percent in its previous survey.

The ambassador, embassy and consulate officials, and other government officials raised respect for religious freedom and diversity with the national and provincial government. They also raised how they might partner to promote religious freedom around the world, better support individuals persecuted for their religion, and counter rising threats to religious freedom. Embassy officials discussed strategies to combat religious intolerance through engagement with religious leaders, [NGO], and [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] groups. The embassy sponsored and participated in public programs and events encouraging interfaith dialogue and freedom of religion. The consul general in a major city held a breakfast with faith leaders to discuss interfaith dialogue and cooperation. The embassy amplified these activities through social media.

---

Section 5. Governmental Attitude Regarding International and Nongovernmental Investigation of Alleged Abuses of Human Rights

A wide variety of domestic and international human rights groups, including [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], generally operated without government restriction, investigating and publishing their findings on human rights cases. Government officials, including the parliament and the government, were cooperative and responsive to their views.

Government Human Rights Bodies : National and local human rights commissions enjoyed government cooperation, operated without government or [RULING PARTY] interference, and had adequate resources. Observers considered the commissions effective. Parliamentary human rights committees operated in the parliament. The committees acted independently of the government, conducted public hearings, and issued reports and recommendations to which the government provided written, public, and timely responses. Most government departments and some government agencies employed ombudsmen. Several regions also employed an ombudsperson.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

