*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s democracy is dominated by the [RULING PARTY]. Corruption is decreasing but remains widespread, while organized crime, environmental destruction, and systemic discrimination damage the rights of [ETHNIC GROUP] populations. Poverty and gender-based discrimination also limit the rights of women and children.

## Key Developments

Between August and October of that year, 13 officials, including the foreign minister, the justice minister, and the interior minister, were removed by the president or resigned amid various political and corruption scandals. These personnel changes attested to the public’s general priority to fight corruption and raise concerns about a lack of transparency and meritocracy in key appointments.
In recent years, the parliament declared itself “pro-life and pro-family,” withdrawing materials related to sex education from the school system and reflecting the growing influence [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] groups have over public life in the nominally secular nation.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

Constitutional freedoms of expression and the press are unevenly upheld. Direct pressure against journalists, including threats by criminal groups and corrupt authorities, encourages self-censorship, and violent attacks against journalists take place occasionally.
In recent years, two journalists were injured by rubber bullets fired by police while covering a protest in the capital, prompting condemnation by press freedom groups.
Diverse religious groups generally worship freely. However, the cultural dominance of a major religious group has spread further into public and private life, sometimes to the detriment of individual rights. Religious groups unaffiliated with this dominant group claim the government disproportionately subsidizes schools affiliated with it. There have also been concerns from human rights groups that holidays of certain ethnic minority communities are not respected by employers of other religious backgrounds.
Earlier that year, the parliament declared itself “pro-life and pro-family” and opened its session with a prayer.
A religious group, which claims to have more than 100,000 members, accused the relevant government ministry of rejecting two requests to register as a religious entity.
Although academia is generally independent, student elections and professional advancement often depend on affiliation with the [RULING PARTY] and [OPPOSITION PARTY].
Recently, academic groups expressed concern after a crowd threw projectiles at and lit fires outside the home of a historian in a rural town over her opposition to urban redevelopment plans.
It emerged that a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] had been awarded a significant amount of state funding over several years for delivering classes discouraging the use of condoms and promoting controversial therapies in public schools, in which religious instruction is legally prohibited.
Citizens can, for the most part, engage in free and open private discussion, though the presence of [ARMED GROUP] in some areas can serve as a deterrent.

