*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedom of expression, including for the press. However, for several years, the government has carried out a sustained campaign to eliminate independent news media and dissenting voices in [COUNTRY] and enacted ever-greater restrictions on free expression; many individuals and institutions reported widespread self-censorship.
Freedom of Expression : The constitution grants freedom of expression except where it adversely affects public security. The constitution also declares the head of state is “inviolable,” and a ministry directive implementing the criminal defamation law reiterates these limits and prohibits publishers and editors from disseminating stories that insult or defame the head of state, government leaders, or public institutions.
Election laws require [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s to remain “neutral” during political campaigns and prohibit them from “insulting” [POLITICAL PARTY]s in the media. Although campaign laws require news outlets to give equal coverage to each [POLITICAL PARTY] participating in an election, there was no evidence of the law’s enforcement during a major election; news outlets gave significantly greater coverage to the [RULING PARTY] than to other [POLITICAL PARTY]s.
The government used the penal code to arrest and prosecute citizens on disinformation and incitement charges, which carry a maximum sentence of three years’ imprisonment. Judges also can order fines, which may lead to jail time if not paid. Police and courts interpreted “incitement” broadly, leading to more than 40 arrests for statements posted to social media during the year.
The government adopted a new law that led to the arrest of at least three citizens. On a specific date, an individual was sentenced to three years in prison for social media posts deemed insulting to the head of state. The government used criminal defamation laws to pursue perceived opponents. The interior minister used criminal defamation laws to pursue perceived opponents. In a certain month, a self-exiled former [OPPOSITION PARTY] leader was charged with public defamation and incitement to commit felony when he accused the prime minister of using the head of state as a hostage and a puppet.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : The government, military forces, and the [RULING PARTY] continued to own or otherwise influence newspapers and broadcast media; there were few significant independent sources for news. The largest pro-[RULING PARTY] newspapers did not criticize the government for politically motivated acts or human rights issues. The government shuttered numerous FM radio frequencies across several provinces, affecting stations relaying independent news [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP].
The National Election Committee code of conduct for a major election established a maximum fine for reporters who interviewed any voter near a polling station or who published news that could affect political stability or cause the public to lose confidence in the election.
Violence and Harassment : Threats and violence against journalists and reporters remained common. On a specific date, a reporter for a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] was beaten unconscious while reporting on illegal activities in a major city. On another date, a group of men stoned and beat a journalist to death. The motive was unknown, but the journalist had been working for a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], covering social issues.
As of a certain point, two former [NGO] journalists arrested on charges of treason (charges which observers said were politically motivated), to which authorities later added charges of distribution of pornography, were awaiting the conclusion of their trial after several court hearings. [NGO]s and observers argued that the case against the two journalists was politically motivated and pointed to the prolonged trial and the confiscation of their passports as proof of government intimidation of the media.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : The law prohibits prepublication censorship, and no formal censorship system existed. The government, however, used other means to censor media, most notably through its control of permits and licenses for journalists and media outlets not controlled directly by the government or the [RULING PARTY]. Private media admitted to practicing some degree of self-censorship, in part from fear of government reprisal. Reporters claimed that newspaper editors told them not to write on topics that would offend the government and have also reported self-censoring due to the chilling effect of recent criminal cases against journalists.
Libel/Slander Laws : The government used libel, slander, defamation, and denunciation laws to restrict public discussion on issues it deemed sensitive or against its interests. A [POLITICAL PARTY] leader was convicted of libel and ordered to pay a significant amount in damages to the prime minister after publicly accusing the prime minister of accepting bribes. The leader has been living in exile for several years, when he fled [COUNTRY] to avoid previous libel charges filed against him.
National Security : The government continued to cite national security concerns to justify restricting citizens’ rights to criticize government policies and officials.

Internet Freedom

There were credible reports that government entities monitored online communications.
The telecommunications law was widely criticized by leading [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]s and human rights activists, who stated it provides the government broad authority to monitor secretly online public discussion and communications using private telecommunication devices. The law gives the government legal authority to monitor every telephone conversation, text message, email, social media activity, and correspondence between individuals without their knowledge or consent. Any opinions expressed in these exchanges that the government deemed to violate its definition of national security could result in a maximum 15 years’ imprisonment.
The government has the authority to shut down any social media page or website that publishes information leading to “turmoil in the society that undermine[d] national defense, national security, national relations with other countries, the economy, social order, discrimination, or national culture or tradition.” For example, a few days before a major election the government ordered local telecommunication companies to block several independent news websites, including [NGO] websites.
A “cyber war team” in the government’s Press and Quick Reaction Unit was responsible for monitoring and countering “incorrect” information from news outlets and social media. The prime minister has threatened that within a short time his cyber experts could identify, to within a short distance, the telephone of anyone who posted a defamatory social media post.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no formal or overt government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events, although scholars tended to exercise caution when teaching political subjects due to fear of offending politicians. Many individuals in academia resorted to self-censorship or expressed their opinions anonymously in [COUNTRY], which has a [POPULATION FIGURE] of inhabitants.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s political system has been dominated by the prime minister and the [RULING PARTY] for decades. While the country conducted semicompetitive elections in the past, a major election was held in a severely repressive environment. Since then, the government has maintained pressure on [OPPOSITION PARTY] members, independent press outlets, and demonstrators with intimidation, politically motivated prosecutions, and violence.

## Key Developments

The security forces interfered with public assemblies and demonstrations commemorating the anniversary of a prominent activist's murder. Three participants who visited the site of the death were arrested, and one was subsequently charged with incitement to commit a felony.
The government continued targeting key members of the [OPPOSITION PARTY], which was banned a few years ago. The interior minister's government charged several of its top officials, including the leader, with treason. The government also banned the leader from reentering the country; the leader had fled to a foreign country to avoid politically-charged charges several years prior. Another top member of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] was released from house arrest, but remained under indictment on charges of treason originally announced a few years ago.
Judges sitting in the supreme court, which oversees the trials of surviving members of a genocidal regime, reached an impasse over whether to hear the case of a defendant charged with committing genocide against a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The government uses lawsuits, criminal prosecutions, massive tax bills, and occasionally violent attacks to intimidate the media. There are private print and broadcast outlets, but many are owned and operated by the [RULING PARTY].
In recent years, the government has engaged in an intense crackdown on independent media. An independent English-language newspaper closed after it was issued an onerous tax bill. It has since been relaunched as an online news aggregator. The following year, a major independent newspaper was sold to a foreign investor with links to the prime minister, and many of its editors and reporters quit or were fired following the sale.
Foreign investments also influence the press output in [COUNTRY]. A pro-government news site, which has accepted outside investment, has distributed content from foreign state media. The government also collaborated with a foreign investment group when launching a television station; this station broadcasts from a studio within the interior ministry and maintains a close working relationship with foreign state media.
While pro-government media organizations operate freely, foreign media groups operate with more severe restrictions or are forced out of [COUNTRY] altogether. A foreign radio station, which reported on forced evictions and corruption, was forced to close its bureau in the capital in recent years. Two of its journalists were arrested and charged with espionage soon after. Their trial began and remained in session at the end of the period.
A leader of a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] was convicted of incitement to discriminate and given a prison sentence; they had cooperated with a foreign state-operated television network on a documentary covering the exploitation of children in [COUNTRY].
Two staff members of the independent newspaper remained on trial as the period ended, after proceedings were delayed. They were originally charged with incitement to commit a felony after writing about local elections. Both journalists subsequently fled [COUNTRY]; one is seeking asylum abroad, while the other has remained outside [COUNTRY] for fear of being placed in pretrial detention.
The majority of the population of [COUNTRY], which is approximately [POPULATION FIGURE], are [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] and can practice their faith freely, but societal discrimination against religious and ethnic minorities persists.
Teachers and students practice self-censorship regarding discussions about the politics and history of [COUNTRY]. Criticism of the prime minister and his family is often punished.
The state generally does not intervene in people’s private discussions, though open criticism of the prime minister can result in reprisals. In recent years, the prime minister and other government leaders warned ahead of an election that criticism of the government would be punished severely.

