*State Department Human Rights Report*

e. Denial of Fair Public Trial

The constitution provides for an independent judiciary, and the government generally respected judicial independence and impartiality. An extreme backlog of criminal cases, however, led to the denial of a fair public trial for thousands of citizens.
Delays were often due to procedural requirements, although the office of the director of public prosecutions began to use recent legislative amendments to expedite certain cases. The need for legislative changes limited the benefits from admitting evidence using new technologies such as teleconferencing witness testimony and videos recorded from a telephone. Consequently, criminal proceedings could extend for years. The government’s statistical office reported the legal system failed to convict in approximately 50 percent of murder cases. During that year courts continued their efforts to address the case backlog by further developing regional justice centers and closely monitoring case throughput to the ministry of justice.

Trial Procedures

The constitution provides for the right to a fair and public trial, and an independent judiciary generally enforced this right. The law provides defendants a presumption of innocence, the right to counsel, and the ability to confront witnesses. Defendants have the right to be informed of the charges against them, and the right to a trial within a reasonable time. Defendants had ample time to prepare their defense. They cannot be compelled to testify or confess guilt. They have the right to appeal. Legal aid attorneys (public defenders) were available to indigents, except to those charged with certain serious offenses. Duty counsels were available to everyone, regardless of charges, from when the person was taken into custody up to their first appearance in court. The government provided free assistance of an interpreter as necessary. The supreme court tries serious criminal offenses, which include all murder cases.

Political Prisoners and Detainees

There were no reports of political prisoners or detainees.

Civil Judicial Procedures and Remedies

There is an independent and impartial civil judiciary process. Complainants may bring human rights abuse cases to the courts for civil remediation, but awards were difficult to collect. The government is required to undertake pretrial negotiations or mediation in an attempt to settle out of court. Plea bargains were rarely offered by the prosecution and even more rarely accepted by defendants. When there were settlements, the government often lacked the funds to pay, resulting in a backlog of awards.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]


## Overview

[COUNTRY]’s political system is democratic, and features competitive elections and orderly rotations of power. However, corruption remains a serious problem, and long-standing relationships between officials and organized crime figures are thought to persist. Violent crime remains a concern, as does harassment and violence against LGBT+ people.

## Key Developments

In recent years, the former education minister was arrested and accused of corruption, fraud, and misappropriation of funds along with the president of a publicly run university. The cases against the former minister, who lost their post over the allegations, and the university president were ongoing. 
A national identification system, introduced a few years ago, was ruled unconstitutional by the supreme court for infringing on citizens’ privacy. Later, the prime minister vowed to introduce revised legislation to relaunch the system.

---

## F Rule of Law

Judicial independence is guaranteed by the constitution, and while the judiciary is widely considered independent, corruption remains a problem in some lower courts.
A large backlog of cases and a shortage of court staff at all levels continue to undermine the justice system. Trials are often delayed for many years and at times cases are dismissed due to systemic failures. In order to reduce the backlog, the government passed legislation that increased avenues for the resolution of cases outside of trial. Since its passage, prosecutors, judges, and government officials have noted an unwillingness from some defendants to consider plea deals; the chief justice vowed to launch an information campaign to persuade defendants to consider plea agreements.
Killings by police remain a serious problem. According to the independent commission of investigations, [POPULATION FIGURE] people were shot and killed by security personnel that year. This represents a decline in reported shooting deaths by security forces; [POPULATION FIGURE] people were killed the previous year, and [POPULATION FIGURE] the year before that.
Gang and vigilante violence remain common. The capital's insular communities remain the epicenter of most violence and serve as safe havens for criminal groups. [COUNTRY] is a transit point for cocaine, and much of the violence is the result of warfare between drug-trafficking organizations. A range of initiatives to address the problem have been undertaken by successive governments, but crime and violence remain deeply entrenched.
States of emergency were declared in several areas for much of that year, as part of the government's efforts to address violent crime. Despite these measures, the government reported a national tally of [POPULATION FIGURE] murders, an increase from the previous year.
Harassment of and violence against LGBT+ people remains a major concern and is frequently ignored by the police. Anti-LGBT+ discrimination is pervasive. A report published by [NGO] found that a large majority of survey respondents felt that same-sex relationships were immoral; these results represented an increase over the results of a previous survey.
While unequal treatment of LGBT+ people remains common, progress has been made in recent years. Legislation against sodomy, which is punishable by up to [POPULATION FIGURE] years in prison with hard labor, was challenged in court. The claimant withdrew after receiving death threats, but their representative, an LGBT+ advocate, launched a new suit; they testified to the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] that the government repeatedly moved to delay its consideration. A parliamentary subcommittee proposed a national referendum on repealing the anti-sodomy law, but the move was criticized by LGBT+ activists who felt legislators should scrap the law themselves; the referendum remained under consideration.
The government added a provision to a law that criminalized the production, recording, or distribution of audio or visual materials promoting violence against any category of persons, including LGBT+ individuals.
[COUNTRY]'s first public pride event took place a few years ago; while subsequent events have grown larger, pride events are still met with government reticence. A pride event scheduled in a major city was cancelled after local officials denied the organizers access to a cultural center. Some high-profile politicians have also spoken out publicly in support of [NGO] in recent years.
Women enjoy the same legal rights as men but suffer employment discrimination and tend to earn less than men for performing the same job.

---

## C Functioning of Government

The elected prime minister and national legislative representatives determine the policies of the government. However, powerful criminal groups, as well as corruption in politics, can affect democratic policymaking.
Long-standing links between officials and organized crime figures persist. Government bodies continue to pursue corruption investigations, and cases frequently end in convictions. However, there are criticisms in the media and from [NGO] that authorities are reluctant to pursue some cases. Government whistleblowers are not well protected.
New legal efforts to fight corruption have been mounted in recent years. These include the approval of legislation that requires lawmakers and public officials to disclose their income, liabilities, and assets; the legislation also streamlined anticorruption laws and empowered a single commission to monitor compliance. The commission began its work recently. In a recent year, the commission announced that it sent six investigative reports to the parliament and supervised [POPULATION FIGURE] government projects in its first year of operation, but no prosecutions were launched.
Legislation approved recently mandated the establishment of an independent anticorruption agency. This agency previously existed, but was affiliated with the security forces; the current body is autonomous. Recently, the agency arrested several security force members, and some were later charged with extortion and breaches of corruption laws.
Major anticorruption investigations continued throughout recent years. In a report to the parliament, the commission warned that managers at a state-owned company spent a significant amount of money on unnecessary expenses, and that the company was unable to justify major outlays. The report was forwarded to the director of corruption prosecution, and remained under review. Recently, the agency accused several security force members of corruption for allegedly soliciting a bribe; their cases remained pending.
Recently, a former government minister, relatives, and a university president were arrested on suspicion of corruption, fraud, and the misappropriation of a significant amount of public funds for their personal use. While a parliamentary committee subsequently declined to launch new probes into the case, the university president and the former minister, who was forced to vacate their post and parliamentary seat as the allegations surfaced, remained in custody.
An access to information law has been in effect for many years, though it contains a number of exemptions. Legislative processes are often opaque.
Recently, the government resisted calls to allow a parliamentary committee, which was chaired by an [OPPOSITION PARTY] lawmaker, to discuss an audit of a state-owned company that showed evidence of serious corruption and mismanagement. The audit was published and concluded that the company's contracts violated procurement rules.

