*State Department Human Rights Report*

Section 4. Corruption and Lack of Transparency in Government

Although the law provides criminal penalties for conviction of official corruption, the government did not implement the law effectively, and government officials frequently engaged in corrupt practices with impunity. Massive, widespread, and pervasive corruption affected all levels of government, including the judiciary and the security services. The constitution provides immunity from civil and criminal prosecution for the president, vice president, governors, and deputy governors while in office. There were numerous reports of government corruption during that year.
Corruption : The [NGO] holds broad authorities to prosecute most forms of corruption. The [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] writ extends only to financial and economic crimes. The [NGO] led a raid that resulted in the arrest of 37 federal road safety officers and five civilian employees on charges of extortion. As of September the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] had secured 834 convictions during that year.
Although [NGO] and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] anticorruption efforts remained largely focused on low- and mid-level government officials, following a major election, both organizations started investigations into and brought indictments against various active and former high-level government officials. Many of these cases were pending in court. According to both the [NGO] and the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], the delays were the result of a lack of judges and the widespread practice of filing for and granting multiple adjournments.
[CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] arrests and indictments of politicians continued throughout that year, implicating a significant number of [OPPOSITION PARTY] political figures and leading to allegations of partisan motivations on the part of the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP].
Financial Disclosure : The law requires public officials, including the president, vice president, governors, deputy governors, cabinet ministers, and legislators, to declare their assets to a government body before assuming and after leaving office. The constitution calls for the government body to “make declarations available for inspection by any citizen on such terms and conditions as the parliament may prescribe.” The law does not address the publication of asset information. Violators risk prosecution, but cases rarely reached conclusion.
In a major case, the chief justice was convicted of falsely declaring assets for failing to reveal money held in several foreign bank accounts. The chief justice was banned from holding public office for a significant period and ordered to forfeit the money in the accounts. The president had suspended the chief justice over the charges of failing to disclose assets several weeks before a major election. The president did not receive support for the chief justice’s removal from the required majority of the parliament or from the judicial council as the law requires. The timing and process of the chief justice’s suspension led many [OPPOSITION PARTY] candidates, lawyers, and [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] leaders to accuse the president of meddling with the independence of the judiciary.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

While [COUNTRY] has made significant improvements to the quality of its elections since the transition to democratic rule for many years, the presidential and legislative elections, which saw the president reelected and the [RULING PARTY] regain its legislative majority, were marred by consistent irregularities. Corruption also remains endemic in the petroleum industry, a key sector of the economy. Security challenges, including the ongoing insurgency by [MILITANT GROUP], kidnappings, and communal and sectarian violence in a restive region, threaten the human rights of millions of people. The response by the military and law enforcement agencies to the widespread insecurity often involves extrajudicial killings, torture, and other abuses. Civil liberties are also undermined by religious and ethnic bias, and discrimination against women and certain minority groups remains pervasive. The vibrant media landscape is impeded by criminal defamation laws, as well as the frequent harassment and arrests of journalists who cover politically sensitive topics.

## Key Developments

The president of the [RULING PARTY] won reelection in a contest marred by violence, the intimidation of electoral officials, and a delay in the poll that weakened voter confidence. Turnout in the presidential election stood at 35.7 percent, the lowest ever recorded. The [RULING PARTY] also secured a majority in both houses of the parliament in a contest held concurrently with the presidential election.
[MILITANT GROUP], an Islamist militant group, intensified its activities in a region throughout the year, leading to at least 1,000 civilian deaths in [COUNTRY] by the end of the year.
A [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group calling for a religious government was classified as a terrorist organization and banned, after followers clashed with security forces in the capital. 
Efforts to reform the vital oil sector remained in limbo, after the president’s veto of legislation the previous year. The reform bill, which was first introduced in the parliament several years ago, remained under consideration by legislators as the year closed.

---

## C Functioning of Government

Elected officials generally make and implement policy in [COUNTRY], but their ability to do so is impaired by factors including corruption, partisan conflict, poor control over areas of the country where [MILITANT GROUP]s are active, and the president’s undisclosed health problems, which have caused him to seek treatment outside the country in recent years.
In addition, the president has demonstrated a willingness to obstruct government bodies while in office. In a major move, the president appointed an economic advisory council that superseded a constitutionally mandated economic management body chaired by the vice president. Later, media reported that the president dismissed many of the vice president’s aides, and the vice president had been bypassed on several presidential decisions.
The government has attempted to reduce corruption in public and private institutions, but the practice remains pervasive, particularly in the oil and security sectors. A whistleblower policy introduced a few years ago, which rewards citizens who provide information on government corruption, led to the recovery of [POPULATION FIGURE] in stolen funds, according to the finance ministry.
Politicians have been locked in an effort to curb corruption in the oil sector for many years, since legislators first considered expansive legislation. Since then, legislators have split the massive bill into components in an effort to secure its passage. The parliament passed the first of these items, a long-awaited bill, but the president refused to sign it, objecting to large budgetary allocations for a proposed regulatory commission. The parliament passed the bill again, removing the budget mechanism in an effort to resolve the impasse. Efforts to pass the bill were still ongoing at the end of the year.
Anticorruption groups have voiced concern over the president’s commitment to keep corrupt individuals from the cabinet after the appointment of a former [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] leader as a deputy minister. The individual, a former governor of a major state, was arrested by the security forces on charges of fraud several years ago. The security forces alleged the individual stole a large sum of money, but the case was dismissed, with the supreme court ruling that the individual was denied due process.
Customs officials have also been susceptible to corruption, allowing smuggled goods to enter the country through porous customs checks in return for bribes. In response, the government took the step of closing its borders with neighboring countries. A major [NGO] criticized the decision and called for a campaign to improve customs practices instead. A regional economic community, which counts all the countries as members, also objected to the move, warning that the border closure would hamper the region’s free trade agreement. The land border remained closed at the end of the year.
Legislation guarantees the right to access public records, but [NGO]s have criticized government agencies for routinely refusing to release information sought through the law. The law has also encountered resistance in some areas. A court in a major city ruled that the federal act did not apply in areas that did not adopt it. In another instance, a state declined to make its budget public in response to a freedom of information request.

