*State Department Human Rights Report*

a. Freedom of Expression, Including for the Press

The constitution provides for freedom of expression, including for the press, and the government generally respected these rights. An independent press, an effective judiciary, and a functioning democratic political system generally combined to promote freedom of expression, including for the press.
Press and Media , Including Online Media : Independent media were active and expressed a wide variety of views without restriction, although some self-censorship occurred.
Censorship or Content Restrictions : Media outlets reported on political developments and high-profile court cases, but privately owned media exercised self-censorship regarding high-profile individuals. The board of a state-owned broadcasting commission directed that board-appointed censors review all programming prior to broadcast.

Internet Freedom

The government did not restrict or disrupt access to the internet or censor online content, and there were no credible reports the government monitored private online communications without appropriate legal authority. Workplaces and internet cafes provided internet access, but most homes did not have internet access.

Academic Freedom and Cultural Events

There were no government restrictions on academic freedom or cultural events.

---

b. Freedoms of Peaceful Assembly and Association

The constitution provides for the freedoms of peaceful assembly and association, and the government of [COUNTRY] generally respected these rights.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

[COUNTRY]'s constitutional monarchy has featured a prime minister backed by a mostly elected parliament for several years. However, the monarch retains important powers, including the authority to veto legislation, dissolve the parliament, and appoint judicial officials. While civil liberties are generally protected, ongoing problems include political pressure on the state broadcaster and land laws that discriminate against women.

## Key Developments

The prime minister died in office; the prime minister was succeeded by the finance minister, who was formally appointed later that year.
Earlier that year, [COUNTRY] signed a memorandum of understanding with neighboring countries to cooperate on fighting organized crime in the region. Later, one of these countries publicly committed to a support program to benefit the [COUNTRY] police in their efforts to fight transnational crime.

---

## D Freedom of Expression and Belief

The constitution guarantees freedom of the press, and a variety of news outlets operate independently, including online. However, politicians have a history of exerting pressure on the media in response to critical coverage. While the prime minister repeatedly complained about reporting by the state-run broadcasting commission, accusing the radio and television outlet of becoming “an enemy of government” that year, a series of leadership changes at the broadcasting commission followed, raising widespread concerns among press freedom advocates.
Constitutional protections for religious freedom are generally upheld in practice. Religious groups are not required to register, but those that do receive various benefits. There are some restrictions on commercial activity on Sundays in keeping with a constitutional recognition of the [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] sabbath. The broadcasting commission’s policy guidelines bar broadcasts of preaching outside the “mainstream [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] tradition,” though this has reportedly not been strictly enforced.
Academic freedom is generally unrestricted. While there have been reports of self-censorship to avoid friction with the government in the past, no incidents of political interference have been reported in recent years. [COUNTRY] hosts one of the regional campuses of [NGO] as well as a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] institute, which offers tertiary courses. In recent years, a university, which is owned by a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] group, became [COUNTRY]’s first locally owned university to be registered and accredited.
There are no major constraints on [COUNTRY] citizens’ ability to discuss politics and other topics in person or on social media. The government is not known to monitor personal communications.

---

## E Associational and Organizational Rights

The constitution protects freedom of assembly, and demonstrations, though rare, generally remain peaceful. Political protests a few years ago degenerated into violent riots, prompting the government to declare a state of emergency that lasted for several years. However, there have been no similar incidents in recent years.
Nongovernmental organizations ([NGO]) have not reported harassment or other restrictions by the authorities. A number of different laws govern the registration processes for civil society groups, but they are not considered onerous.
Workers have the legal right to organize in trade unions, but implementing regulations have never been issued, meaning the [COUNTRY]'s various de facto unions generally operate as associations. [COUNTRY] joined the International Labour Organization in recent years, though it has yet to ratify the organization’s conventions on labor standards.

