*State Department Human Rights Report*

b. Freedoms of Peaceful Assembly and Association

The government of [COUNTRY] restricted freedoms of peaceful assembly and association.

Freedom of Peaceful Assembly

The constitution of [COUNTRY] provides for freedom of assembly “according to notification regulated by law.” The demonstrations law includes an expansive list of prohibited activities, giving a judge the authority to prohibit or curtail planned demonstrations after submitting an official memorandum. Domestic and international [NGO]s asserted the law did not meet international standards regarding freedom of assembly. In recent years, a local [NGO] filed a lawsuit challenging a long-standing law, arguing that it was passed by an incompetent body and repealed many years ago. The court was expected to issue a ruling in the case. The government imposed an exclusion zone of 2,600 feet (790 meters) around vital governmental institutions in which protests are prohibited.

There were protests throughout the year, mostly small, and some occurred without government interference. In most cases the government rigorously enforced the law restricting demonstrations, in some cases using force, including in cases of small groups of protesters demonstrating peacefully.

In recent months, local [NGO]s reported police arrested more than 4,000 individuals after small protests erupted in several cities over accusations of corruption against the president. Many of the individuals detained reportedly had no connection to the protests and happened simply to be in the vicinity of the protests. Police and prosecutors subsequently released more than 2,500 of those detained. Local [NGO]s claimed that, in some instances, detainees were tortured or subjected to other abuses.

The cumulative number of persons arrested under the protest law was not publicly available. The security forces arrested a political activist, who the police had surveilled for several years. On another day, the security forces arrested another political activist, the brother of a prominent activist, who co-founded a major [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP]. Both remained in pretrial detention pending charges of “colluding with a [REBEL GROUP].”

On the final day of voting for a major referendum, the security forces arrested an engineer and member of a [OPPOSITION PARTY] after he raised a sign saying “No to the constitutional changes” outside a polling station in the capital. According to local media, the security forces arrested several members of the [OPPOSITION PARTY] after they reportedly voiced objection to the proposed constitutional amendments.

According to a local [NGO], thousands of persons whom the security forces arrested during a period of unrest several years ago due to their participation in demonstrations (some of which were peaceful) remained imprisoned; however, the authorities released others who had completed their sentences and some through presidential pardons. The authorities reportedly held such individuals under charges of attending an unauthorized protest, incitement to violence, or “blocking roads.”

[NGO]s claimed the authorities inflated or used these charges solely to target individuals suspected of being members of groups in opposition to the government or those who sought to exercise the rights to free assembly or association.

The interior minister conditionally released a prominent activist, whom a court convicted of participating in a protest several years ago. The conditions of the sentence require the activist to report to a police station from 6 p.m. to 6 a.m. each day for the next five years. The activist was appealing the nightly sentence and requesting that he be allowed to fulfill its terms from home. Later, the police detained the activist as he prepared to leave after spending the night in the police station and charged him with belonging to a [REBEL GROUP], funding a [REBEL GROUP], spreading false news to undermine national security, and using social media to commit a publishing offense. Local [NGO]s reported he was tortured or subjected to other abuses while in custody.

Since their release from prison after completing sentences for violating the protest law, several activists remained on probation with terms requiring them to reside in a police station from 6 p.m. to 6 a.m. each day. The security forces rearrested one of the activists and released him a few days later on charges of beating a citizen and damaging his car outside a police station. The security forces also rearrested another activist in another case. An administrative court ruled that the order to compel one of the activists to spend every night inside a police station as part of his probation was invalid.

The security forces arrested several high school boys who protested in front of the Ministry of Education building regarding the repeated failures of new electronic systems in their schools; they were released the following day.

Freedom of Association

The constitution of [COUNTRY] provides for freedom of association. The law governing associations, however, significantly restricts this right.

The president signed a new [NGO] law, which replaced a previous law that local and international [NGO]s stated could make it impossible for them to operate independently. According to a [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], the new law includes noteworthy improvements from the previous law in several respects. However, the [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] also assessed that the new law preserves the former law’s overall restrictive regulatory approach and continues to impose significant barriers to civil society activity.

Pending the promulgation of implementing regulations for the new law, the Ministry of Social Solidarity continued to apply the previous [NGO] law on international and domestic organizations receiving international funding. [NGO]s reported fewer incidents of security services ordering cancellation of planned training programs or other events. The supreme court ruled unconstitutional several articles of the previous [NGO] law, which gives the minister of social solidarity the right to dissolve [NGO]s.

The penal code criminalizes the request for or acceptance of foreign funds, materiel, weapons, ammunition, or “other things” from states or [NGO]s “with the intent to harm the national interest.” Those convicted may be sentenced to life in prison (or the death penalty in the case of public officials) for crimes committed during times of war or with “terrorist purpose.”

A local [NGO] announced that the security forces arrested a lawyer after he announced that he intended to go on a hunger strike and begin a sit-in to protest the arrests and alleged abuse of a journalist, an activist, and his attorney. As of the end of the period, the lawyer remained in detention pending investigations on charges of “colluding with a [REBEL GROUP],” “publishing fake news,” and “misusing social media to spread false information.”

The security forces arrested the founder of a [NGO] that supports the families of disappeared persons. The founder was traveling to an international event to participate in a working group. The charges against him included “communicating with a foreign body to harm the national interest.” A [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP] stated that it “remains concerned” that the measures against the founder “constitute acts of reprisals against him for cooperating with the working group.” Later, a court ordered the founder’s release. However, the founder appeared in front of the State Security Prosecution accused in a new case of “belonging to a [REBEL GROUP]” and “funding a [REBEL GROUP].”

Following the acquittal of several foreign [NGO] workers sentenced for operating unlicensed organizations and receiving foreign funding without government permission, a court acquitted the remaining defendants. A [REBEL GROUP] and its affiliated [NGO] remained illegal, and the [REBEL GROUP] was listed as a designated terrorist organization.

The authorities continued investigations of local [NGO]s that received foreign funding under a case originally brought several years ago. A court postponed a motion to lift the travel bans imposed on several defendants in the case, including the founder of a [NGO] that focuses on feminist studies, accused of receiving foreign funding to harm national security in connection with her [NGO].

A court case brought by a [NGO] that provides rehabilitation services to victims of violence, challenging a closure order, remained pending an expert report ordered by the court. The [NGO] asserted the closure was politically motivated, targeting the [NGO] because of its work investigating torture, deaths in detention, and impunity for these crimes. The [NGO] continued to operate in a limited capacity, serving a [POPULATION FIGURE] of people.

---

Section 3. Freedom to Participate in the Political Process

The constitution provides citizens the ability to choose their government in free and fair periodic elections held by secret ballot and based on universal and equal suffrage. Constraints on freedom of expression, association, and assembly, however, limited citizens’ ability to do so.

Elections and Political Participation

Recent Elections : [COUNTRY] held a presidential election, resulting in the re-election of the president with 92 percent of the vote. The president’s sole opponent received 3 percent of the vote, less than the number of spoiled ballots. The opponent registered their candidacy on the last possible day to register, and until the day before, they were a member of a campaign supporting the president for a second term. Prior to the elections, authorities arrested some potential candidates for allegedly violating military prohibitions for public office and reportedly pressured others against running in the elections; some candidates remained in detention, as did journalists arrested based on their coverage of the elections. Authorities were still holding the chief editor of a now-blocked news site, a satirist, and a former leader of [OPPOSITION PARTY] in pretrial detention. Authorities arrested them with other bloggers, researchers, and students in cases on charges including spreading false news and joining a banned group. According to [NGO], authorities arrested the chief editor after the news site published a translation of an article that claimed authorities gave bribes to citizens to vote during the presidential elections. According to local media, authorities arrested the former leader after they made statements about the presidential elections. Domestic and international organizations expressed concern that government limitations on association, assembly, and expression severely constrained broad participation in the political process.
International news media alleged that, in some instances, voters were paid to vote. The media regulatory council fined some news outlets publishing critical coverage of the presidential election and also referred several journalists to the journalists' syndicate for investigation (see section 2.a.).
Parliamentary elections were held in recent years. Domestic and international observers concluded that government authorities professionally administered these elections, while also expressing concern about restrictions on freedom of peaceful assembly, association, and expression and their negative effect on the political climate surrounding the elections.
In recent years, a national referendum approved constitutional amendments extending the president’s current term and allowing the president to run for a third term. The amendments expand the role of the security forces to include “safeguarding the constitution and democracy” and the role of the president to include appointing the heads of judicial bodies and chairing the supreme council for judicial bodies and entities. They also limit the state council’s authority to review laws. The amendments also add a second chamber to the parliament and allow the president to appoint one or more vice presidents.
Multiple domestic and international organizations and press reported interference by the government in the referendum process: arrests of independent and partisan individuals who opposed the constitutional amendments publicly on social media accounts; distribution of food packages and cash as incentives to vote yes; a large presence of banners and media promoting the amendments and a lack of opposition banners and media; a government ban on websites opposing the amendments, including those gathering “no vote” signatures; the lack in some polling stations of a list of the proposed amendments; progovernment supporters mobilizing near and in polling stations; and allowing out-of-district voters to vote in all polling stations, which allowed for the possibility of casting multiple votes.
Political Parties and Political Participation : The constitution grants citizens the ability to form, register, and operate political parties. The law requires new parties to have a minimum of 5,000 members from each of at least 10 regions. The constitution also states, “No political activity may be practiced and no political parties may be formed on the basis of religion or discrimination based on gender, origin, or sectarian basis or geographic location. No activity that is hostile to democratic principles, secretive, or of military or quasi-military nature may be practiced. Political parties may not be dissolved except by virtue of a court judgment.”
The political wing of [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] remained banned. Authorities did not ban other [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] parties. Separate military courts sentenced a former chief of staff of the security forces to prison for violating military discipline by announcing their intention to run for president and for forgery. The former chief of staff was released from detention by military prosecution order.
Participation of Women and Minorities : No laws limit participation of women or members of minorities in the political process, and they did participate. Social and cultural barriers, however, limited women’s political participation and leadership in most political parties and some government institutions. Voters elected a record number of women, [ETHNIC GROUP] members, and persons with disabilities to parliament during recent parliamentary elections, a substantial increase compared with previous parliaments. The parliament law outlines the criteria for the electoral lists, which provides that the parliament must include at least a certain number of women, [ETHNIC GROUP] members, and persons with disabilities. The constitutional amendments introduced a quota in the parliament for women and a requirement to better represent workers, farmers, youth, [ETHNIC GROUP] members, and individuals with disabilities. The president appointed additional members of parliament, including women and [ETHNIC GROUP] members. The parliament law grants the president the authority to appoint parliament members, not to surpass a certain percentage of the total number of elected members. If the president opts to use this authority, a certain percentage of the appointments must be women, according to the law. Parliament included women and [ETHNIC GROUP] members.
Several women led cabinet ministries. There were [ETHNIC GROUP] members among the appointed governors of the regions. Authorities appointed a [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] woman governor of a region, making her the [COUNTRY]’s second female governor. No women were on the supreme court. The supreme judiciary council promoted female judges to higher courts; legal experts stated there were approximately a certain number of female judges serving in courts; that total was less than a certain percentage of judges. Several senior judges were [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] members.
A member of parliament told press that parliament referred them to an ethics committee for posting a video criticizing the president.

g. Stateless Persons

Of the [POPULATION FIGURE] stateless persons known to [NGO], most were [ETHNIC GROUP] displaced for many years. According to a local [CIVIL SOCIETY GROUP], the number of stateless persons in [COUNTRY] was likely higher than the number recorded by [NGO]. The government and [NGO] lacked a mechanism for identifying stateless persons, including those of disputed nationality and those of disputed [ETHNIC GROUP] nationality. A majority of the [POPULATION FIGURE] [ETHNIC GROUP] refugees were stateless.

---

*Freedom House Freedom in the World*

## [COUNTRY]

## Overview

The president, who first took power in a coup, has governed [COUNTRY] in an increasingly authoritarian manner for many years. Meaningful political opposition is virtually nonexistent, as expressions of dissent can draw criminal prosecution and imprisonment. Civil liberties, including press freedom and freedom of assembly, are tightly restricted. The security forces engage in human rights abuses with impunity, and physical security is further undermined by terrorist violence centered in a major region.

## Key Developments

Constitutional amendments that were adopted in a tightly controlled referendum further concentrated power in the hands of the president and authorized the president to remain in office for several more years. The amendments also undermined the independence of the judiciary and strengthened the military’s role in civilian governance.
In recent years, small protests broke out in several cities, including the capital, to express opposition to the regime’s corruption and economic mismanagement. In response, the authorities detained thousands of people and censored online speech.

---

## A Electoral Process

The president is elected by popular vote for up to two terms. The president, who originally seized power while serving as the defense minister and armed forces commander, won election and reelection with 97 percent of the vote. The balloting did not offer voters a genuine choice. In recent years, legitimate opposition candidates were pressured to withdraw, and the president ultimately faced an approved challenger, who had campaigned on the president’s behalf before entering the race. The vote was also marred by low turnout, the use of state resources and media to support the president’s candidacy, voter intimidation, and vote buying. The electoral commission threatened nonvoters with fines in an attempt to increase participation.
Constitutional amendments added two years to the president’s current term, extending it for several more years, at which point the president would be allowed to seek an additional term.
The amendments to the constitution reestablished the parliament as a bicameral body. The upper house was abolished for a time, but was restored as a [POPULATION FIGURE]-member Senate. Members will serve five-year terms, with two-thirds elected and one-third appointed by the president. The amendments reduced the number of seats in the lower house. Elections for both chambers were scheduled to take place.
The last elections for the lower house took place in two stages, featuring low turnout, intimidation, and abuse of state resources. A progovernment coalition won all bloc-vote seats. Independents won a majority of the constituency seats, and the coalition parties’ candidates generally outpolled their rivals in the remaining districts. Just a few parties outside the coalition won more than 10 seats. Many parties—including moderate [RELIGIOUS MINORITY] parties and liberal and leftist factions—boycotted the elections and voiced serious reservations about their fairness, accusing the security forces of harassment and intimidation. In recent years, the parties associated with the coalition formed a parliamentary bloc, which controlled a majority of the chamber.
Elections for local councils had yet to be held as of recent years. The last councils were elected many years ago and dissolved after the ouster of the longtime authoritarian leader. Since then, government-appointed officials have controlled local governance.
The constitution was not drafted in a fair or transparent manner, and the referendum through which it was adopted was tightly controlled, with little opportunity for public debate or an opposition campaign. The drafting and passage of the constitutional amendments featured direct intervention by leaders of the intelligence service, including a relative of the president. A referendum, held over several days, was marred by reports of vote buying and other irregularities, and no organized opposition was permitted to challenge the well-resourced “yes” campaign. Nearly 89 percent of participants backed the amendments, according to official results.
While the electoral laws themselves provide some basis for credible elections, electoral authorities largely fail in practice to ensure an open and competitive campaign environment. The president signed a law creating an electoral commission, as envisioned in the constitution. The commission’s board consists of senior judges drawn from the highest courts to serve six-year terms. However, the legislation phases out direct judicial supervision of elections, which critics argue will damage the integrity of elections and reduce public trust in the results.

---

## B Political Pluralism and Participation

Political parties are legally allowed to form and operate if they meet membership thresholds, pay fees, and comply with other requirements. However, in practice there are no political parties that offer meaningful opposition to the incumbent leadership.
Parties formed on the basis of religion are forbidden. While some Islamist parties still operate in a precarious legal position, a religious minority group was outlawed as a terrorist organization, and its political party was banned. Since then, authorities have systematically persecuted its members and supporters. The former president, who was overthrown in a coup, died in a courtroom in the capital while on trial; like many other prisoners, he had been denied medical care and otherwise mistreated during his years in custody. A leader of the banned group has received multiple sentences of life in prison and death sentences on various charges.
Activists, parties, and political movements that criticize the government continued to face arrests, harsh prison terms, death sentences, extrajudicial violence, and other forms of pressure. Following a series of small protests, the regime carried out thousands of arrests, targeting not only protesters but also political activists and politicians, among others.
The persecution of potential challengers in a presidential election and of political activists illustrated the regime’s determination to eliminate any opportunity for a peaceful change in leadership. By tightly controlling the electoral process, intimidating presidential candidates into withdrawing, and denying credible opposition parties the space to function effectively, the government makes it nearly impossible for the opposition to gain power through elections. The extension of presidential term lengths and limits only exacerbated the problem.
Since the coup, the military has dominated the political system, with most power and patronage flowing from the president and his allies in the armed forces and security agencies. Most of [COUNTRY]'s provincial governors are former military or police commanders. Vaguely worded constitutional amendments further strengthened the legal underpinnings of the military’s political influence, calling on it to “protect the constitution and democracy, and safeguard the basic components of the state and its civilian nature, and the people’s gains, and individual rights and freedoms.”
The constitution and laws grant political rights to all citizens regardless of religion, gender, race, ethnicity, or any other such distinction. However, women, Christians, a religious minority group, people of color, and LGBT+ people face indirect forms of discrimination that limit their political participation to varying degrees.
A religious minority group, who account for some [POPULATION FIGURE] percent of the [POPULATION FIGURE], were allocated seats in the unicameral parliament. Some members of the group were elected, and some were also among the lawmakers appointed by the president. Thanks in large part to quotas, women won seats in the parliament, and some were also appointed by the president.
Under constitutional amendments, at least a quarter of the seats in the new parliament will be reserved for female representatives. As with the old parliament, some seats will also be set aside for Christians, workers and farmers, people under 35, people with disabilities, and citizens living abroad.

---

## E Associational and Organizational Rights

According to the constitution, freedom of assembly should not be restricted. However, legislation passed that year, as amended a few years later, allows the interior ministry to ban, postpone, or relocate protests with a court’s approval. Among other restrictions, unauthorized gatherings of 10 or more people are subject to forced dispersal, protests at places of worship are prohibited, and protest organizers must inform police of their plans at least three days in advance. Thousands of people have been arrested under the legislation, and some jailed protesters have received death sentences. The severity of the crackdown on assembly rights has made protests extremely rare.
In recent years, small protests erupted after videos posted online alleged government corruption and urged people to take to the streets. The regime responded with a sweeping nationwide crackdown; more than 4,000 people were arrested, including protesters, human rights lawyers, journalists, and politicians.
Nongovernmental organizations (NGOs) have faced mass closures as well as harassment in the form of office raids, arrests of members, lengthy legal cases, and restrictions on travel in recent years. A highly restrictive law banned NGOs from engaging in work deemed to harm “national security, public order, public morality, or public health”; and required a regulator’s approval for any field research or polling and any type of cooperation with foreign NGOs. All NGO funding and basic management decisions are also subject to the regulator’s approval. Violations of the law can lead to dissolution or crippling fines; the possibility of imprisonment was excluded from a revised version of the law, though conditions for NGOs remained highly repressive in practice.
The government only recognizes unions affiliated with the state-controlled [NGO]. While the constitution provides for the right to organize peaceful strikes, they are not tolerated in practice, and the law on protests prohibits gatherings that impede labor and production. Striking workers are regularly arrested and prosecuted, particularly since labor protests increased a few years ago; workers at state-owned businesses are subject to trials by special courts.
A new law enacted recently eased many of the restrictions imposed by earlier legislation on trade unions, which had effectively compelled them to join the state-controlled federation and imposed controls on their structures, by-laws, and elections. Among other changes, the new law lowered the threshold for union formation from 150 to 50 workers, and imposed fines rather than prison terms for violations. It remained unclear whether the legislation would lead to actual improvements in the recognition, registration, and operational autonomy of independent unions.

